      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <head>itite</head>
        <p>itite</p>
        <p>No 1676 ONE PENNY</p>
        <p>COUN TY COUNCIL ELECTION, FRIDAY, MARCH 7TH, 1913. TO THE ELECTORS OF THE</p>
        <p>WINWICK DIVISION.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I have consented to be nominated as your repre- sentative for the Winwick Division of the Lancashire County Council. I am a resident in the Division, of 21 years standing, and have served as Rural District Councillor and Guardian of the Poor. Non-residents, and invalids are out of place as repre- sentatives of the people. I have no axe to grind, no business interests to serve. As a progressive in strong sympithy with all sorts and conditions of workers, as educationist, hygienist, humanitarian, and temperance reformer I hope, if returned by your suffrages, to render some real, practical service to the Division, and to show myself worthy of your confidence. The call of the hour is not for legislation, but for a fuller and more thorough administration of existing parliamentary powers, especially in the direction of humane education health and good environment. Our Councils, education, and Boards have too long been dominated by dummies and the self-interested— they need purging. The people ask for better value for their money,—for reasonable economy with the highest efficiency possible We may not pay too much, but we do receive too little.—Your faithful Servant, Burton Wood Vicarage, A M. PiiTCLIELL. February 270, 1913.</p>
