      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>2 MoDOOdALL'B PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR yoIiMaNIMG3</head>
        <p>2 MoDOOdALL'B PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR yoIiMaNIMG3</p>
        <p>Old ilbar CHICALA A DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, BRIDGE ST. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>in these the amateur again has a good guide as to the I different show points to breed for. The prize list, of the different leadiog shows are an ever useful piece of information, for by constant reference one is enabled to keep in touch with those fanciers who are at the " top of the tree." Space will not allow me to tell my readers of all the good things contained in this book. Let toe lecmrMend you to get a copy. I know of no more valuable book at the price, for it is only Is. 4d. post free, or in cloth at 2s. Bd. post free to be had from the "Feathered World," 9,Arundel at., Strand, London, W.C.</p>
        <p>THE BOARD OF AGRICULTURE'S POULTRY</p>
        <p>REARING DEMONS fRATION.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, in con- junction with the Cheshire County Council, have just inaugurated an interesting demonstration of a new method of ;eating poultry for the table. By means of a grant from the Development Commission they have secured for the purpose the services of Mr 1' 3 Poynter, who has for several years been conducting a series of experiments on the rearing of poultry, with the ohject of producing first class table chickens at. a minimum cost. He now appears to have succeeded in reducing his experimental efforts to a definite and satis- actory method which gives exact and almost unvarying results. This method is so suitable for adoption by smallholders and others interested in Poultry- keeping that the Board of Agriculture have decided io endeavour to place the method before them in the most practical manner, by arranging with Mr Poynter to settle down for a year in a small holdings district, and to give a demonstration of his work so that the small holders and roultry keepers in the district may see for themselves how it is done. The Board there- fore approached the Cheshire County Council, who at once agreed to provide a suitable site for the demon- stration, and offered the Board a house and land at Haslington near Crewe. This is in the middle of a small holdings district, and is within three miles of Crewe, which is an excellent railway centre. Mr Paynter's programme—of which a detailed account is given iii the Journal of the Board of Agt culture for December 1912—is briefly this. He begins hatching early in December, and starts a fresh incubator each week. He thus hatches 100 to 120 chickens every week from the end of December until the beginning of June, the total number eventually hatched being about 3,000. These are reared on grass land, the total area necessary being 31- acres. They are sold off ready for killing at the age of from twelve to sixteen weeks, and the whole year's operations are concluded about October. On the scale on which Mr Poynter has conducted his experiments, he has ascertained that with an initial expenditure of about 150 for plant, he can, as a small-holder (i.e without chargieg for his time) make a considerable profit during the year. As the work only bgan in December, there is at present little to see. A special Sub-Committee of the Cheshire County Council has been formed, by whom arrange- ments will be made in due course for orgat ised visits of small holders and others who are interested in the matter to whom Mr Poynter will show his process and explain his methods. The local supervision of the Demonstration has, on behalf of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, been placed in charge of Mr A Allsebrook, one of the Small Holdings Commissioners of the Board of Agriculture, in whose district the County of Cheshire is included, and he is assisted in the work on behalf of the County of Cheshire by Mr R P Ward, the Directer of Educa- tion, City-road, Chester, from whom further particulars of the demonstration may be obtained.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY</p>
        <p>FARMING.</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP.]</p>
        <p>FOODS AND FEEDING Proper feeding is the main essential to success in poultry farming. It matters little how elaborately poultry are housed, so long as they are ill-fed they will never produce satisfactoi y results. There is no branch of farming, in which so much ignorance is dis- played on the question of feedbag, as in the poultry department. Certain it is, we are improving, but, even yet, there are thse who still believe that poultry fed in the good old-fashioned way do alright. A pan of boiled potato pairirgs, with the addition of a handful of meal, is still the general rule in not a few instances. Some people seem to think that so long as you fill a fowl full of any kind of food, it will do just as well as others more scientifically catered for. As a matter of fact poultry keepers have told me that because they eat such food greedily, it must b right. .What nonsense to be sure! The reason they eat it, is because they are hungry and hunger knows no choice. I have seen hungry little children eat dirty orange peel picked up from the street. As a lad I have followed the Cheshire hounds for five and six hours at a stretch, over hedge and ditch. And believe me, I have been thankful to uproot a swede turnip and make a good meal of it. But you will further believe me, I am sure, that under such circumstances I would not have refused a good plate of potato pie. This perhaps is somewhat by the way. What I want to show you is that poultry are often compelled to eat inferior food because of no better choice. I want my readers to understand the value of good food stuffs, I want you to see also the economical side of right feeding. Once realized and adopted, the question of right feeding will soon bring its own reward in the shape of healthy and vigorous stick and iu turn, of course, a more prolific egg supply. THE BREEDING SEASON. Now the question of right feeding cannot be better applied than during the breeding season. The parent stock birds should always be fed on the very best quality of food stuffs. If you do not feed the stock birds well, how can you expect a healthy vigorous progency ? Speaking to a large breeder a few years ago, he told me that his chief difficulty was weak germs in the egg. The eggs were not unfertile, but a large percentage did not mature. I told him that I thought his method of feeding had something to do with the poor results. With this he agreed and at once changed his system of feeding, with very satisfactory effects. The germs were stronger, the hatching percentage was higher, and generally speaking, the chickens were much stronger. "Like begets like as sure as night the day." Strong healthy parentage means strong healthy progeny, and the main point in the producing of young life is to see that the parent stock are well nourished with the right kind of food. It is almost useless attempting to regenerate weak young chicks. Therefore you will see that it is most essential for the stack birds to be well fed.</p>
        <p>COPY NATURE. One needs no finer demonstration of health than is seen amongst wild birds. It is not often in your rambles through fields and woods that you find the diseased carcase of a wild pheasant or partridge. The beautiful green sheen of the male blackbird; the mellow tone of the thrush's evensong; the vitality of the soaring lark; the beautiful feather colouring of the pheasant with its perfect reproduction; the general trueness to type of the many and varied species of wild birds—these are all sure signs that they are thB happy creatures of nature. And .because of their unfailing fitness we are compelled to probe nature's secrets for guidance. Thanks then to our scientific food producers, for it is they, after all, who continually give to us something new and of real value on the question of right feeding after nature's own requirements. ADVERTISED FOOD STUFFS. The best poultry keepers are those who have found from experience the genuine requirements of their stock. There never was a time when poultry farmers were better catered for than to-day, and food stuffs of an inferior character are soon discarded by practical men. When I speak of foodstuffs, Ido not mean condiments, such as spices and liquid tonics. I have scanned our advertising columns and have not found a single advertiber's speciality that I could not recommend from experience, and it is because of this fact that I wish to draw my readers' notice to the vendors articles of food. - -</p>
        <p>MESSRS. CARTER AND SON. This thriving local firm are agents for the most reliable people in the poultry food market. The Old Calabar specialities are of a tried and reliable nature. Their chicken foods are especially good. Then we have the name of Armitage Bros. on our list. Their dry chick food also is of such a reliable character as to need no fulsome puff from me. I have used both these foods for chicken rearing with great success. Leaving chicken food we now come to a new food is the name of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>KAVARIS.</head>
        <p>KAVARIS.</p>
        <p>This is an entirely new name to many. For some time past now I have been experimenting with this new and wonderful food, and I can say without hesita- tion, with wonderful success too. Kavaris is a food. rich in those elements that poultry require. I specially recommend its fi ee use with breeding stock. It is a frame builder and its general use should mean strong, healthy chicks. The inventor of this new food is Mr. Hargreaves, of Widnes, who is at the present time specialising on food stuffs for poultry and young animals. It is intereAing to note, that this very earnest and conscientious scientist has in the first instance considered the feeding of poultry before that of other stock.</p>
        <p>- REARING TURKEYS. Nearly all who rear poultry know of the attendant difficulties in rearing young turkeys. Kavaris claims to solve this problem, and I ask my re iders to see what the inventor himself has to say on the question : —"The high mortality amongst young turkeys is due, not so much to disease, as to a practical starvation of a portion of the organism in consequence of a deficient supply of some of the primary elements needed (during a short but critical period) for building up the skeleton and nerve tissues of the young birds, although their food may contain a great excess of all the other elements. My new food will help young birds through this dangerous period, it will provide them with the elements they need to enable them to grow strong and healthy. It will also increass the produc- tion and fertility of eggs," I have every confidence in recommending the free and general use of Kavaris.</p>
        <p>- - GENERAL REMARKS. In this article I have dealt chiefly with food and feeding in a comprehensive manner. Next week I will come down to the more common de.ails of feeding, in the hope that my readers will be persuaded to adopt a more comonsense method of feeding than is often he case to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>REVIEW.</head>
        <p>A REVIEW.</p>
        <p>I have just received the year book published by the "Feathered World," and I hasten to offer my con- gratulations to those tesponsible for such a fine pro- duction. All previous attempts are eclipsed in this one, and if the limit of good work is reached no one could surely grumble. It is really a splendid produc- tion. It is interesting to the lay mind ; it is invaluable to the utilitarian ; and to the fancier, of course, it is absolutely essential. It is also a safe amateur's guide. Let any amateur take and study the "Feathered World" year book, and I will guarantee that by following the advice given therein he will become a successful fancier. On fancy topics it is undoubtedly the safest guide. The pictorial section, I would point out, are photographs taken from real life, so that</p>
      </div>
