      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Corn Millers.</head>
        <p>Corn Millers.</p>
        <p>UGGS for Sitting.—Kosscombed Kuode Island Reds, 124 3s. sitting.—E. HALSALL, High hug Farm, Burscough rd., near Ormskirk. - - -</p>
        <p>PURE, But/ Orpington EGGS; Cooks noted strain, from massive birds 3s. per dozen.—HERBERT MOSS. Norwood rd., March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>RUNNING AMOK.</head>
        <p>RUNNING AMOK.</p>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH'S WILD FLURRY.</p>
        <p>ATTACK ON COUNCILLOR ROBERTS.;</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., presented a more or less original political extravaganza on Saturday evening, and it had a friendly reception from a select gathering cf the faithful. The occasion was the annual meeting of the Conservative Association, but it soon became evident that the business part of the meeting was a negligible quantity, and that all the stalwarts had assembled for was to hear the Mt mber smite the enemy with bitter invective and abuse. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart, who had journeyed f; orn Irel "while we were all in bed" - as Mr. Smith obligingly told us in order to attend the meeting, was iii the chair. The work of passing reports and electing r fficers was got through with a rapidity which suggested that every- thing had been nicely cut and dried in advance Mr. Smith could not tees I another meeting at which the business had been transacted "taster or more felicitratsly " than on this happy occasion. Of each offt!er in turn we were told, with pa iriful monotony that he as " the right man in the right place " - that was about the full extent of the eulogistic elcquence employed. The annual report assured the meeting of the great and unabated- popularity of" our Member ' and of the intense interest hrid adm;ration with which his" active and splendid work" in the House of 00011130118 and among his cinstituents was regarded. The loss of one seat at the municipal elections evi- dently proved a bitter pill to the Conservatives, but the report ascribed the ITO uff to " special circum- stances," and found comfort in the assertion vita "the total poll revealed a substantial majority of votes cast in favour of our candidates." The officers elected were :—President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.; deputy- presidents, Mr J Charlton Parr, Mr Robert Pierpoint and Mr Harold Smith, M.P. ; chair man, Mr W Peter Rylands ; deputy-chairman, Mr J Fulton Smith ; hon. secretary, Mr G H Smith ; hen. treasuter, Mr E Bradshaw ; hon. legal advi-er, Mr F, A White. These preliminaries over, the audience settled down to be entertained by Mr Harold Smith, who unques- tionably succeeded in amusing them by a speech east in the ultra-Smithsonian vein. The member was exceedingly wrathful because Councill,,r Henry' Roberts had had the temerity to suggest a few perti- nent questions which he might be invited to answer, and this no doubt accounted for the excessively personal tone of his sprz”'ch. Before embarking upon a picturesquely distorted account of the Birrell meeting, however, Mr Smith treated us to a resume of the political events of the past year. This was chiefly notable for his declara- tion in favour of the conscriptiorrist agitation—although of course. be did not call it that. He said they were proud of the great work which Major Cresfield had done on behalf of National Service —a work which had been supported by another distinguished themlor of the Liberal Party, Mr Henry Roberts, about whom he would have a few words to say at a later stage of his speech. "Let us" he continued "pay a great tribute to those members of the Liberal Party who, on this question, put their country before their politics and who do support what I believe is a great practical scheme for training the young men of our laud —not by conscription, but training them for a short period each year to carry and use a rifle, and to he ready, if their country calls upon them, to shed their blood, as their forefathers did, for their country's cause." A NEW VIEW OF TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>Mr Smith advanced what impressed us as at least an original view of tariff reform when he advocated it as a means of securing a more equitable distribution of wealth. Be said that throughout our working classes there had been, as there was at the present motnent, a great feeling of discontent which he, in most cases, had always felt t 3 be justified— a feeling of discontent at the fact that while our country was amassing wealth, that wealth was going into the pockets of the few and not permeating into the pockets of the many. "We attribute that " declared Mr Smith" ti• the fiscal system which we blindly and foolishly follow." Now we know why the £l,OOO ducal diners are giving of their abundance to help forward the Protectionist movement. It is just because of their anxiety to ensure that the wealth which at present has a habit of accumulating so largely in their own pockets, shall be more extensively shared out. But surely that might be done without all the com- plicated machinery of a tariff system! Then Mr Smith passed on tl the Birrell meeting. the success of which appears to have caused great perturbation in the Tory camp It seems that Mr. Smith has been greatly troubled in mind because, during the last year or two, the Tories of Warrington have had nobody to knock down. "We have gone into the ring week after week " he said "and we have occasionally had some feeble oppenent who put on the gloves, but was immediately on the other side of the ropes. You and I don't like hitting people unless they are in a position to hit back, and we can say with confidence that the Liberal party, during the last two years, have never had a kick in them, let alone a blow with the fist. It would bo a very pleising matter to all of us who are sportsmen and anxious for a fight, to meet an opponent who, at any rate, appears to be worthy of our steel. I notice that during the past week the Liberal party have really looked as though they were trying to put some sort of life into the weary old bag of bones which represents them in Warrington. We don't like smashing away at bags of bones—it is an unprofit- able ccupat ion, and our time could be better spent" With his usual delicate sarcasm, Mr Smith alluded to the visit of Mr Birrell, who, he said, was in his honest mood when he came to Warrington and therefore, as a party nontribution,the humorous and able spec..h which he delivered would hardly be welcomed by the Libetal firebrands who were anxious for" blood and thunder." Mr Smith next tried to belittle the significance of the meeting, from the standpoint of attendance, and said he was told on competent authority that any man seen passing the Parr Hall about 7.30 on the evening of the meeting was very lucky if he was not dragged in by the soruff of the neck. Passing on to "some of the minor artillery which was fired off before or after the great gladiator stepped into the arena," Mr Smith aced ohii' need not bother about the speech of Mr Parker which he was told was very long and characteristically duli: Nor need they waste their time on the effusion of the late lamented member—a most undistinguished speech. In which (so Mr Smith had heard) he repeated "many long stories " which he had told in Warrington two days before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MR. SMITH'S MARE'S NEST.</head>
        <p>MR. SMITH'S MARE'S NEST.</p>
        <p>"The longer I live " went on Mr Smith" the more convinced I am that there is an undercurrent going on in Warrington among the Liberal Party to try and get rid of a candidate who, in many respects, is not liked —a candidate who spends so much of his time out of Warrington and so little in it, and who when he is in Warrington, I understand, lectures everybody, including his own friends in private and us in public." As to who the candidate at the next election would be, he (Mr Smith) did not care whether it was MT Crostield, that stalwart democrat Mr Brunner (of Brunner, Mond fame),or even Mr Henry Roberts—in fact the Conserva- tives did not very much min,' if it were all three of them. Then Mr Smith turned his attention to Conncillor Henry Roberts in the following terms :—Mr Henry Roberts was not going to be out-done by Mr Parker or Mr Crosfield, or the Liberal candidate for Widnes. He gave place to nobody, except to the Chief Secretary, and I dare say, in his heart of hearts, he did not give way to him. (Laughter.) At any rate, Councillor Roberts,doubtless one of these looking for the reversion of Mr Crosfield's post, comes forward, puffed with his own importance, and makes a speech in which he asks me some questions which seem to me so simple that they are hardly worth answering. If Ido answer them, it is not because I attach any importance to the ques- tions he put, but because I think the questions do deal with subjects which interest all the people in Warrington and which certainly are o/ great interest to the Unionist Association in Warrington. It is quite obvious, on reading Mr Roberts's speech, that he has sat up, I dare say until the small hours of the morning, sharpening these puny little weapons in order that he may let -them off and find some crack in my armour through which he may penetrate to a vital spot. He set out to try and find three subjects upon which he thought we were most vulnerable, and I must confess I am delighted to find that the whole concentrated brain of the Radical party can do no better than this, after my having represented Warrington for over two years in the House of Commons. He invited certain Conservatives to put to me questions which he put to the audience. He did not like to do it himself, because he did not want to bandy words with me.' Personally I agree, after reading his speech, that it would be a great waste of his time and of mine to bandy words with him, if that is all he has to say. (Laughter.) I den't quarrel with the conclusion at which he arrived, but with his reason for arriving at it</p>
        <p>Ido quarrel. He said he did not want to bandy-words with me, because he might get the same sort of answer that his son got at a time when I had a little corres- pondence with him. I would say this</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TO MR. HENRY _ROBERTS_:</head>
        <p>TO MR. HENRY _ROBERTS_:</p>
        <p>He need not be in the least afraid or alarmed. I con- sidered]Mr Gordon Roberts made s remark which was untrue, snobbish and vulgar, and I told him so. To do Mr. Henry Roberts justice, I have never found his criticisms have been either snobbish or vulgar. I have mply found them fatuous and stupid. (Laughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>KAVARIS</head>
        <p>KAVARIS</p>
        <p>The new Food for Poultry and Young Animals. (Hargreave's Patent.) Makes Birds strong and healthy; • eggs plentiful and fertile; lightens trouble and risk in rearing young stock; ensures good constitution; remarkable results. TRY IT. KAVARIS FOOD CO., Hutchinson Street, Widnes. Ask your Corn Dealer for " Kavaris."</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>Mr Smith then recalled the Debating Society incident which led to his correspondence with Mr Gordon Roberts on the question of payment of Mr Smith's election expenses. He (Mr Smith) said quite honestly that if he were so pour, and if he had been unable to pay his own election expenses, he would have been all the prouder if Warrington had taken him and assisted him to pay them. With all his faults, however, he was a man with some pride and he gloried in the fact that he had for many sears made consider- able sacrifices in order that he imight be independent of anybody and go to the House of Commons not as a delegate ')ut as a representative. (Applause) The object of Mr Roberts's attack even if the allegation had been true, was vulgar abuse, but it was not true. Such conduct came ill from a representative of a party which claimed, however fraudulently, to represent democracy. So lung as his criticisms could not be ranked with those of his son, Mr Henry Roberts need never be afraid that he (Mr Smith) would apply the same language to him. " I trust " he added "I haver been offensive at any time to Warrington citizens unless I had unusual cause for it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FOOD TAX PLEDGES.</head>
        <p>FOOD TAX PLEDGES.</p>
        <p>Mr Rdberts accused him of having stated at his election, very definitely and distinctly, that under nc circumstances or conditions would he ever be a party to putting taxes upon the food of the people. It is not true " commented Mr Smith. '1 never said any such thing, from the beginning of my election to the end, and it I had said such a thing I don't think I should be the member for Warrington. What I did say was this, in effect—and I chcllenge contrad:ction—l said in almost every speech I made, and particularly in my open-air speeches, that I would rot give a vote for it proposal to tax wheat or any other food unless I c,,,me back to Warrington and asked them to re-el' It me. (Applause.) That pledge I have carried cat and will carry out. The bald fact, of course, emerges, that the Liberal party do not understand honesty in politics. The authors of the Home Role Bill, toe Welsh Disestab- lishinent Bill and the Franchise Bill, do not understand a candidate for Pariconent who is honest enough to come to his conAituents and say 'if you elect me, I am not g dug to vote for such aud such a thing until I have come back to you and received your mandate to do .t." With characteristic modesty, Mr Smith said he would not say tca:. toe Unionist party had followed his lead but by a coincidence, the party to-day stood pledged to w hat he stood pledged to at too last election. Coun- cillor Roberts asked whether, in view of his previous pledge, he stood with our great leader," Mr &amp;mar Law, on the questi,ni of food taxes. Certainly he did. by bhuuld he differ from him ? tie (Mr Smith) had more than once announced that, owing to circumstances which he frankly admitted he regretted, owing to the, fact that the i.ineial party had for years so wickedly and insidiously spread the fraudulent lie about taxing tire food of the people and to Vie further fact that the crimes of the Liberal party were so great, he was reltutantly compelled to believe that it was the duty of the Conservatives to concentrate upon the latter, rather than launch out upon a deatable and somewhat controversial branch of tariff reform policy. For those reasons, he, with regret, adopted the suggestion and was one of the first to sign the urgent request for Mr B.A.ar Law to remain in office witn that policy at his oack.</p>
        <p>For _Mr it)oorts Cu [natio chat: cha-rgo</p>
        <p>against me" added Mr Smith "shows either great ignorance or great stupiuity. I cannot believe it shows dish.ines:y hut I do believe he has made that charge without the slightest knowledge of whit he is talking about." Mr Smith, contiuuing, said it was perfectly true that, on the eve of toe puil, he te,t, out with his voting card a pledge to the effect that he would never iur a policy which would increase the cost uf the workiugmau s food, int that was toe wuole case on food taxes. The Liberal party since</p>
        <p>they came into power had extracted from the pockets oi the po ,rest in the land ma sum of £70,000,000 in food taxes. It WAS the policy of the Unionist Party to du away .ith chose taxes on articles which we could not produce and which therefore necessitated the pay- ment ot Inc tax by ourselves, and to transfer them to articles which we could produce in great and over wirelming quantities in our own Colonies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ATfACK ON LABOUR MEN.</head>
        <p>ATfACK ON LABOUR MEN.</p>
        <p>Mr Roberts accused him of having taken part in the House of Commons in a very serious attack on the treat, unionist party. Tnat was a very serious charge to bring against tne mewber for a great industrial constituency, but, like the first, it was absolutely untrue. Altuough he (Mr Smith) nad been in Parlia- ment over two )ears, Mr Roberts did not cite a single vote or a singn3 word he uttered, to justify that attack. Having claimed that, on the basis of democratic election,he was entitled to claim to represent the trade uaionisLs of Warrington, Mr Smith asked iI r Roberts what he meant by te "trade unionist party." If he meant many of the almost self-elected leaders o° the the Labour party who sat in the House of Commons, then let it ue frankly and at once conceded that they had no more valuable critic tuan himself (Mr Smith.) He did not think they lepresented the traae union spirit in the country, and he had not been in the least afraid or criticising and attacking a body of men whom he looked upon more like a duck of sheep who were in the pockets of the thberal party, voting solidly with them on every ques- tion, except on a question which front time to Lime cropped up, on which they could safely vote against them, because they knew the ‘.voole of the Conservative party were going to vote wita them. Notwithstanaing this onslaught on the Labour M.P.'s, however, Mr. Smith did not hesitate to claim as a testimonial to his soundness on Labour questions an Brunie in the "Clarion " by Mr Jowett, a La ')our M.P., in which he referred to Mr Smith's " friendly intervention " in the debate on the Railways Bill. Discussing the Unionist attitude towards tile Trade Union Bill, Mr Smith said he agreed that the Osborne judgment went too far, but on the other hand, the Bill as introduced by the Government did not protect the individual trade unionist who might object to com- pulsory contributions to the upkeep of a party with whose policy and creed he disagreed. On that ground the Unionist party, he was glad to say, fought the Bill until it came to the third reading. So keen was their opposition on that principle that, on the report stage, the Labour party themselves moved an arnenament. Er was proposed by Mr Wardle, secouded by their friend and neighbour Lord Wolmer, and it practically settled the whole difference and conceded the whole principle tor which the Unionists had been fighting. The momeat that amendment was introduced, practically the whole of the opposition of the Unionist party was withdrawn and the Bill was supported, on its third reading, by au eloquent and able speech from their leader.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>BELFAST ATROCITIES.</head>
        <p>BELFAST ATROCITIES.</p>
        <p>Mr Roberts further stated that he never could understand, after nearly forty years of political life, how any Roman Catholic could support a Conservative candidate. " Nobody questions" commented Mr Smith "that he is speaking the truth. I don't suppose he over would underatand it. (Laughter.) As Dr Johnsoa said, we can only give him facts; we cannot give him the brains to understand them. (Laughter.) am satisfied that I have in Warrington, among my most valued supporters and personal friends, many Roman Catholics. and I c':)n't think vapourings of Mr Roberts would influence by one hair's breadth, either their personal friendship for myself or their support of the party to which I belong. He says he is utterly ignorant of any debt which the Roman Catholics owe to the Conservative Party. Has Mr Roberts forgotten that there is in power to-day a Government which is pledged to wrest the Roman Catholics' schools away from them? (Applause.) Has he forgotten that it, was only because Catholics and Churchpeople fought shoulder to shoulder for their denominational schools a few years ago that we saved them ? Has he forgotten that it is only by fighting in that way again that we can resist the appeal which is being made to the present Government to proceed with another wicked, so-called Education Bill? Mr Henry Roberts has a conveniently short momory." Referring to the atrocities which Mr Roberts said were perpetrated on Roman Catholic citizens by the Protestant mob of Belfast, Mr Smith said those attacks —which he had publicly deplored not only in Warring- ton but in Belfast—had never been made on individuals because they were Catholics, but because they were Home Rulers and because they belonged to a class, a sect, and a c )ciety which had tofouled and besmirched the fair name of our country for years past. Moreover, the bitterest attacks were made against that class who were known as the Protritant Home qulers.</p>
        <p>Touching upon Mr Birrell's Epeech, Mr Smith said it showed a deep and solemn appreciation of the Ulster problem, but did not discloss any attempt to grapple with it. They could congratulate their opponents in Warrington upon having brought down an honest politician who was not afraid to pay a high tribute to Sir Edward Carson. One might contrast that states- manlike language with the language used by Mr Crosfield when he spoke in Warrington of " King Carson "—a vulgarity which was unworthy of the bearer of the name of Crosfield—referred to his " colossal bluff" and characterised him as the man above all others who had acted in such a way as directly to lead not only to unrest but to ruffianism.</p>
