      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WORKHOUSE INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Guardian Declines to Apologise to</p>
        <p>Matron.</p>
        <p>Mr W C Brooks recently brought to the nodes, of the Warrington Board of Guardians an allegation made to him by a Visit •r to the Workhouse Hospital that the matron bad told her that she could please herself " whether she paid any attention to the request of Mr Brooks that a person should be allowed to visit a patient in the institution. It has now been ascertained that the complaint was made on a misappi e- h.nsi on, the matron not having been concerned in theincident at all. The qiii stion was again reterred to at the Board of Guardians on NNednesday. Mr EL Bent presided and there were also present Or A Jolley (depety chairman), Mrs Simpson, Miss Broads. ent, Revs T It Matthews and A M Mitchell, Messrs .1 Evan, Bil I inge, NV GHIA Brooks ,G Mather, W Houghton, W C Brooks, W Furness. It Lee, J A Hughes, G Allen, .1 A Rushworth, J Backhouse, NV t•latiagan, M NV Seton, 'l' Ritchie, A Bottoraley (clerk), J Brown (master), and C E Lawrance (superintendent of Cottage Homes). At a meeting of the House Committee further con- sideration was given to the report made at a previous meeting by Mr W C Brooks concerning a statement alleged to have been made by the matron to a woman who was visiting a patient in the Infirmary. The ' woman referred to appeared before the committee and admitted that she made the statement in question to Mr Brooks. She, however, informed the committee that the statement was not made to her by the matron, or by any cffier of the Workhouse, but by her son who was a patient in the Union Infirwary. The son stated that a sister in the hospii al had told him, with reference to Christmas visiting, that even it a relative of his got a note from Mr Brooks, or any Guardian, she migut get within the gates of the Institution but she (the sister) bad the power to say whether anyone should see the patient or not. Tnestaternent was not made with any ill-will and did not give him the idea that admission would be retuzed to any visitor who had a note from a Guardian. The Committee reported that they were satisfied that there was no intention on the part of the sister of say- ing anything deroeawry to a Guardian, and the mean- ing sue intended to convey had been altogether mis- understood. Mr Billinge said the original allegation had received considerable prominence and one could not refrain from expressing the hope that in i future Guardians would be more careful n setting in circulation reports which had reference to the character of the principal officers of the institution. They sympathised with Mr Brooks in having been led to bring plaint on wrong information - forward the corn but, as the matter had found its way into the Press, it was quite probable that wrong impressions had got abroad. He noticed the minutes contained no expression of regret or apology from Mr Brooks to the officer concerned. It was such a kerious question that it would help to clear the whole matter if Mr Brooks would make an apology. Unfortunately he had been misinformed, for he at first told the Guardians that a person who was present when the incident occurred could vouch for the statement. He (Mr Billinge), hoped the affair would be a lesson to all the Guardians and to NI r Brooks in particular, and that he would see the advisability of apologising for the circulation of the report which had no founda- tion, as far as the matron was concerned. She had only recently taken up her duties and it, was conceivable that such a report might do her serious damage or interfere with her interests in the Poor Law world. Mr W C Brooks : As far as I am concerned, I have nothing to apologise for. If Mr Billinge will only take note, the woman admitted that the statement 1 made</p>
        <p>wes quite correct. Miss BROADBENT : What Mr Billing° means is that Mr Brooks should express to Mrs Brown his sorrow at having caused her so much annoyance. That is all that is necessary. The CHAIRMAN: I think some short explanation is due. Mr BROOKS: I think the minutes and the report of the committee cover everything. Mr ItusawoßTß : There is no doubt that there have been cases—l have had one myself—of insub- ordination—if I might term it so—by some of the nurses. Therefore it is perhaps as welt that this thing has come forward so that in fut ure they will be more careful. The Rev A M MITCHELL : I think Mr Brooks has perfectly justified himself and I cannot see that there is any need for my learned friend from Hayduck (Mr Billinge) to animadvert on what has taken place. I don't, tor one moment, think any apology is necessary. Mr Brooks acted upon a very fair, straight statement and I think what has been put upou the minutes quite settles the matter. The CHAIRMAN : I think some explanation is due from Mr Brooks, apologising to some extent, to the matron for the tiouble and annoyance he has caused her. I think she is quite entitled to that. The Rev A M MITCHELL: That is a personal matter. The CHAIRMAN : It entirely rests with Mr Brooks as to whether he makes any reply or not. Mr FLANAGAN said the Guardians ought to be careful in giving credence to the tales they heard from visitors to the institution. He himself could have brought forward three or four complaints, if he had chosen to do so, but he made private enquiries and found they had no foundation in fact. MISS BRODBENT : It is not a purely personal matter. It has appeared in the Press. Mr W C BROOKS said he took whatever precautions he thought nece.sary before be brought the complaint forward. He got his informant to make the statement on three diffsrent occasions,in the presence of witnesses, and when she appeared before the committee she con- firmed what he had stated as to the grounds of his com- plaint. A mountain was being made out of a molehill. Mr Brooks pointed out that he had been justified with regard to a complaint on another question—concerning the treatment of clothing belonging to an inmate—and if he had to apologise in one case, what Was due to him in the other ? Miss BRoapnwr : The master apologised fully in the other case. Mr BROOKS : The master was ordered to apologise by letter and he has not done so. Mr FLANAGAN : In that case, the master was misled by one of the officials. When he came to make inquiries afterwards he found he had made a mistake. He didn't do it knowingly and deliberately and when he found he had been misled he, as a man, apologised to the committee. The CHAIRMAN : Next business, please. INSURANCE ACT AND WORKHOUSE POPULATION. The Clerk's estimate of expenditure for the half-year ending 30th September, was submitted to the Board. It showed that the total amount required was Z 15,000, as against £15,244 for the corresponding half-year. The call last year was £14,000. The balance in the treasurer's hands on February 10th was £5,226 and it is estimated that at the end of the present half-year there will be a balance of .C 5,000 of which £2,620 has already accounted for in the estimate of expenditure. Mr JOLLEY moved and Mr FURNESS seconded that the estimate be referred to the Finance Committee tor consideration and report. The Rev A M MITCHELL called attention to the fact that the amount estimated for in-maintenance at the Workhouse (.C3,900) showed a decrease of £2OO. How was that accounted for? Was it due to a reduction of numbers or did it mean that the Workhouse fare was of posrer quality ? The CLERK ssid he arrived at the reduced figure after considering several points, one of which was that, in view of the passing of the National Insurance Act, they might not have as many cases to deal with in the Workhouse. The Rev A M MiTcHeisr.: It does not mean a reduc- tion in the quality of the fare ? The CLERK : No. The Rev A M MITCHF.LT. said there was a slight increase in respect of the schools, but he rather wet-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Referendum is not infrequently put into opera- tion with respect to municipal affairs, end a certain vicar has adopted this course in order to ascertain the views of his parishioners, as to whether the Sunday morning service is regarded as too long and if so in what way it should be reformed. In not a few parishes the reply to such questions would favour the shortening, if not the abolition of the sermon which in a vast number of churches scarcely amounts to anything more than an attempt to fill up time. It is a survival of the ancient belief that a minister must preach to his congregation once or twice every Sunday, whether he has anything helpful to tell them or not, and this practice is largely responsible for our empty churches. People will always listen to a good sermon, but such a discourse is rather the exception than the rule, and generally when the. sermon is poor the pews are empty. Morning Shopping.—Most women have household duties to attend to in i the morning, and, if there is any i shopping to be done t s usually put off to the after- noon. That has always been the general rule, but many of the large retail establishments in London are encouraging their customers to follow the example of the American woman, who usually starts out on a shopping expedition quite early in the morning. Social habits favcur this custom in the United States. The mid-day meal is usually a cold " snack " which requires little preparation. That leaves the morning for shopping and the business of the day, whilst the after- noon is devoted to social diversions. On this side prelude to going out to tea, but growingteendency to get through the outdoor sthhoeprepiinsga is oftenth work of the day in the morning. The railways are helping in this matter, by issuing cheap " shoppers tickets before noon, and there are, of course, obvious advantages attaching to early shopping. It avoids the afternoon rush and in purchasing provisions, cut flowers, greengrocery, &amp;c., it is desirable to be early in the field. Moreover it easies matters for the hard-worked shop assistant, to whom it is an advantage to get through as much as possible of the day's work in the morning. " Cowless " Milk.—We are often told that science will solve the problem of the high cost of living. It has not as yet, done much in this direction, but in a week or two's time milk produced from vegetables— chiefly soya beaus will be on sale in London, at 3d. a quart. It is said to be equal in food value to the best tall-cream cow's mUk. from which it is scarcely dis- tinguishable in appearance or taste. Whether it will serve equally well for the feeding of intants,the making of milk pudding, and cakes, remains to be seen. Its one and only acknowledged deficiency is, that it is no good for making butter, so that it is a little premature to talk about the threatened extinction of the cow. We have long had condensed milk, and dried milk in the form of powder, but synthetic milk will be something new and the public will watch with interest this attempt of German scientists to compete with natnie in the production of staple foodstuffs, Fur-Coated Sheep —Russia, Canada, and the United States, are all taking measures to preserve their for- bearing animals, but, as the choicer kinds of paltry have become rare and expensive, almost every living thing on four or two legs that has a furry coat, is being turned to account by the furriers. Even the rat is being hunted for the sake of its skin. We de not want to see the rat cultivited for this purpose, but a new source of supply for the furrier is promised by the proposed introduction of fur-coated sheep in the United Kingdom. For this purpose it is intended to import some of the Karakul sheep- from Central Asia, which produce the well-known "Persian lamb" and "broad tail " furs of commerce. The matter has been attract-</p>
        <p>lug the attention of the Agricultural Colleges, and according to expert opinion, there is every probability that cross-bred Karakul lambs would prove a new and profitable industry to the farmers in this country. Early Spring Fashions.—Judging from what it is possible to discover, the fashions for this spring are graceful and in good taste. The grotesque would appear to have been carefully avoided. Long lines and flowing draperies will be the chief characterising. The tight skirt and the straight silhouette will di 3 hard,but _ .</p>
        <p>signs of their approaching dissolution are net wanting. Even tailor-mades are draped slightly, and this necessi- tates fine and supple materials. Indeed the new cloths are remarkable for their fine texture and smooth and glossy surface. A new coating serge, for instance, has the appearance of satin, and is scarcely heavier in weight. The new shot whipcords are most fascinating and might easily be mistaken for corduroy. Braid is much used on tailored suits, and the coloured belt that contrasts with the blouse and skirt is again seen on many of the new suits. The New Draped Skirts.—The pleated skirt has bees on its trial now, for some time, but has not attained the popularity predicted for it some months ago. Those pleats seen on the new models are wide enough to give the necessary spring and yet preserve the silhoutte. The new draped skirts are specially interesting owing to the variety dressmakers have cleverly managed to introduce into the style of draping. It is astonishing to note in how many different ways it is possible to arrange materials on a skirt. The new cross draperies are simple, and quaint. They part just above the foot, and drop in a somewhat baggy, but greceful manner. Paniers, or rather draperies suggested by the panier, have quite established themselves, for they are simple and practical. There is great variety in coats also, and the popular brocade coat will doubtless prove the fore- runner of the threequarter Directoire, in a material quite different from the skirt with which it is worn. Some Spring Ilats.—Velvet is generally worn far into the spring for it is Most becoming to the face, and chiffon-velvet drapes admirably, hence some of the new tagal straws in big shapes are draped with this beautiful material, not only over the crown, but on the brim also. Tufts of feathers to match the colour of the hat, are placed in front, and at the back, or any eccentric position. Black straw is always hard, and particularly trying to some faces. Milliners recognis- ing this fact, are lining the brims of such headgear with colored tagal straws. One of the new shapes is a glorified boat, and a great improvement on that model. The brim on the right side turns up wide and flat, but is lower on the left. The long rather narrow space occupied by the crown is entirely covered with feathers rulle is much used in this spring millinery, it is an ideal material for spring wear, and some of the brocaded hats, of the turban variety, are swathed in tulle, which is used for the upstanding mount in front. The Modish Long Chain.—Nowadays, every girl's ambition, apparently, is to become possessed of a long chain, intersected at intetvals with gems. Not all, however, realise this ambition, but the impecunious should bear in mind the fact that if gold, silver, or other metal is unavailable for the chain, one of fine beads passes muster very well—with here and there at regular, or irregular intervals, as fancy dictates, a seed pearl, a tiny cameo, any odd uncut stone, or charm of some description inserted. Many girls have by them, quite a collection of such odds and ends, which look particularly well when utilised in this particular way.</p>
      </div>
