      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>As the cup-ties draw near, the Warrington men are -shaking down into something like respectable form, ani the probable team to do battle for the trophy which has twice rested in Warrington can be easily forecasted. In all probability twelve of the thirteen Players selected for the match to-day will do duty at keighley pelt Saturday.</p>
        <p>The team is practically the same thirteen as went to Salford last Saturday and came so near springing a surprise on the Reds. Considering how thoroughly Salford over played the Wirepullers at Wilderspool in September, and that they are candidates for one of the coveted "first four" positions, it is a reasonable presumption that the local lads have considerably Improved. They were only beaten by one goal to nil at Salford and gave a very creditable display.</p>
        <p>For cup-tie purposes the forward and full back Positions are settled beyond question or discussion if the men who have done duty so consistently this year are all fit and well. Brooks awl Tranter are certain- ties on one three-quarter flank and both played fine football at Salford. Tranter is running better and kicking less—which is essential for centre play. On other wing Berry and Bradshaw are moral certain- ties, though it is a question whether either man is as speedy as he used to be. With Renwick and Dicken- son not yet sound any other selection is unnely, though Waywell is a likely candidate for any vacancy, Particularly as the three-quarter line's chief want is Speed. At half Nicholas is pretty sure to work the se!um, and if Douglas succeeds to-day against Hull he probably retain the outside position.</p>
        <p>I have great faith in " Kelly " Douglas, who has the valuable knack of beating his man on the run, and plays a good all- round game. What he needs to avoid is going too far when he has made the opening. In several "A " learn games that I have seen lately, Douglas has spoiled a fine bit of half back play by running blind ??hen he has got through the first line of defence instead of at once giving his centres possession. If he will remember that that is his mission t make oPenings for the three-quarters he will soon be a smart outside half. There is football in him!</p>
        <p>The Cup team is thus likely to be the one on view to-day against Hull, viz : Jolley, full hack ; Brooks, Tranter, Berry, and Bradshaw, three-quarters, -Nicholas and Douglas, half backs; G Thomas, Cheaters, Fearnley, R Thomas, Cox and Naylor; forwards. As soon as Skelhorne is fit he will probably displace Naylor, and another likely candidats is Clare, 'who is developing into a clever loose forward with Hpeed enough to turn scoring opportunities to good account.</p>
        <p>null are bringing a strong team to Warrington to-day, and one that differs only slightly from the thirteen which defeated Wigan last week. Greenwood (Centre) was hurt and will be replaced by J P Smith,a local player of whom much is expected. The two Australians, Gilbert and Darmcdy, are included, the former acting a 3 captain. Warrington have a hearty aP preciation of his powers, and will,l trust, look after bin] better than they did on the occasion of his last :tetnorable visit to Warrington with the Australasians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
