      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>FIVE MILES SCRATCH RACE. WARRINGTON A.C.</p>
        <p>By the kind permission of the Footbrill Club Com- mittee the local athletic club ran a scratch race after the conclusion of the football match, on Saturday last, and a large proportion of the spectators, including Mr Harold Smith, M.P., remained to see what proved to be a very interesting race.</p>
        <p>_ The distance. which was about five-miles, necessitated covering 26 complete laps with the addition of a run in of 78 yards. The race was for the team which is to represent the club in the Northern Senior Championship at Stockton to-day, but owing to the unavoidable absence of W Autrobus, A Burgess, E Scott, and F shadwell, three of the junior team were allowed to take part as a provision for eventualities in connection with National Championship which is set for decision at NVolverhampt on on Saturday next. The President of the football club, Mr T Locker, J.P., officiated as starter and referee, and despite the obvious desire of the competitors to get sell away a splen start was effected. The three juniors, A Gregson, J Hanson and J Nurse, were intent on making the pace hot, and the gait was merry enough to suit all. The runners were in close company for the first two miles, when the internationals, Vose and Holbrook, moved up and the field began to tail out Running in great style Vose gradually increased his lead until with a mile to go he lapped Nurse, who pluckily held on for over a quarter mile, and eventually Vose wan easily in the good time of 26 minutes 8 seconds. The going wits sticky and all did well to finish in good style in the following order Time.</p>
        <p>Min. Secs. 1 C \Tose 2 H Holbrook 3 F Antrobus</p>
        <p>4 W Bolton -)6 40 5 3 Hanson 6 J Parsonage 7 J Nurse That the lookers on enjoyed the race was evident by the applause accorded to all and particularly to F Antrobus and J Nurse for spirited and plucky finishing. The afternoon sport was satisfactory in every sense, as the victory of the local " A' teatu was witnessed by a large attendance of spectators, who showed their appreciation of the efforts of the local athletic club by subscribing generously towards the unusually heavy expenses of the season thus :—Per collection boxes : Copper £4, silver 8s 3d ; Alderman J Evans, silver, 6s ; Mr Harold Smith, M.P., gold, £1 ; Mr T Locker, J.P., paper, .£5 ; a total of £lO 14s 3d ; which represents a windfall for a club beset with financial difficulties and a mark of appreciation for which the management are truly grateful.</p>
        <p>CHAUFFEUR FINED. SEQUEL TO MOTOR COLLISION.</p>
        <p>Philip Henry Scamell, chauffeur to Mr Holbrook Gasket!, of Frodsham, was summoned at Runcorn on Monday for driving to the public danger on the 14th inst.</p>
        <p>Superintendent Ennion said that the defendant had a local publican out in his master's car on the 14th inst. He did not know if he bad his master's consent, but he thought it was a "joy ride" Mr Linaker (defending : Nothing of t}-e sort. The Superintendent said that about six o'clock on the evening in question, defendant was driving towards his home at Frodsham. Near the end of Mill-lane a collision took place between the car driven by defendant and a car belonging to Mrs Wilmslow, of Frodsham. The allegation was that the defendant's car was driven at a fair pace on the wrong side of the road, and ran into the other car, which was turned round by the force of the impact. Defendant's car then went on and crashed into a telegraph post. Several witnesses expressed the opinion that the man was under the influence of drink when the smash happened. The defence was that the defendant was dazzled by the brilliant acetylene lights of the on coming car, and kept a course in the middle of the road. All he was responsible for was an error of judgment. Defendant on oath, said he bad had only three glasses of beer.</p>
        <p>Four previous convictions were proved, and defendant was was fined and oosta.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
