      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Warrington's Keen Struggle with Salford.</head>
        <p>Warrington's Keen Struggle with Salford.</p>
        <p>The Wirepullers played their return fixture with Salford on Saturday at Weaste in beautiful weather. Both teams were well represented, and a determined effort was made by Warrington to avenge the defeat sustaine4 at Wilderspool earlier in the year. Though the Reds have had an unusually good season in the playing sense they are not getting very strong support, and there would not be more than 4,000 spectators at the match on Saturday. The teams were as follows : Warrington.—Jolley, back ; Bradshaw, Berry, Tranter and Brookes, threequarter-backs ; Dainteth and Nicholas, halt-backs ; G Thomas, Chesters, Naylor, Fearnley, Cox and It Thomas, forwards. Salford.—Launce, back ; Mesley, Wild, Lovelock and Callender, threequarter-backs ; John and May, half- backs ; Thomas, Ritchie, Pugsley, Rees, Thom and Goldsmith, forwards. Referee, Mr R Robinson (Bradford). Touch-judges, Messrs A Lee (Leigh) and F Mills (Oloham). Salford were the first to become dangerous after the kick-off and by good forward play they forced War- rington back, Jolley replying with a fine clearance kick. The Warrington backs replied with combined work and both Brooks and Bradshaw made runs which taxed the home defence. Launce was shaky under one attack,and a little more pace by the Warrington vanguard would have lowered the home colours,but John was given time to nip in and clear. The Reds again became aggressive but combined indifferently. Warrington's defence was admirable and there was no real danger until Ritchie made is mark and Mosley bad a shot at goal, which was not far from the target. Nicholas and Daintith, who had been punting the ball too much, now set the threequarters going, and excellent combination forced Salford to defend. Eventually Launce eased the pressure with a lofty kick and the venue was changed. The "Reds, " after a brilliant round of handling, were within an ace of scoring, Callender being only a few yards short when he was pushed into touch. Salford monopolized the ensuing play for some minutes, and May missed a repass when only about a con pie of yards short. Jolley extricated his side from a dangerous situation' and was deservedly applauded, Loveluck, John and Mesley combined well, but Brookes forced the Salford wing man into touch near the corner g. Another great clearance by Jolley was followed •"Pranter making an attempt to break through, but superior numbers prevented niuce progress being made. Warrington now had a serious misfortune, for following a temporary raid on their goal, George Thomas was hurt and the game was stopped for several minutes. He resumed, but after another pack had been formed he walked from the field of play with a bruised shoulder. Despite Vieir being short-banded, Warrington played up pluckily, but they had a narrow escape when Brookes was tackled in possession near the line. A moment later Warrington were penalized and Mesley this time placed a goal. Immediately after the interval was sounded, with Warrington behind by two points. The resumption of play was more encouraging for Warrington, as Thomas was able to restune. Salford again led the way to the line by means of rapid handling and running. The ball was tossed about with freedom, and Ritchie was tackled only a yard short. The Reds were " all over" Warrington for a time, and only great defence kept them out. Jolley was the personification of pluck, and resumed after being knocked out in charging a reply by Launce. Eventually Warrington raised the siege, and Brooks made some brilliant runs and dashes down the wing which troubled Salford greatly. But points could not be gained, and Salford came again before the close. Loveluck kicked over the Warrington line and an exciting race for possession was won by Jolley. who kicked dead in the nick of time. Final : Salford, one goal (2 points) ; Warrington, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>OFFICER RUNS THROUGH 00,000.</head>
        <p>OFFICER RUNS THROUGH 00,000.</p>
        <p>Losses of £lO,OOO on the Stock Exchange, £5,000 by betting, and £5,000 by card playing in the three years before his failure, were mentioned when the London Bankruptcy Court on Thursday suspended for three years the discharge of Major Cecil Jackson, late of Luton, and formerly in the Indian Army. It was said that since 18S9 the debtor had run through £OO,OOO ec eived on the death of his father and mother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>IMPS BAND CONTEST.</head>
        <p>THE IMPS BAND CONTEST.</p>
        <p>THE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A " V. SALronn "A."—Just now Warrington " A "are a popular team and a fine crowd assembled at Wilderspool on Saturday to see the combination match with Salford "a." Warrington bad the breeze to start with and dashing forward play carried the ball well into the Salford quarter. Here the Red's halves got possession but were robbed by Bardsley who dribbled over in dashing style, for Gibbs to add a goal from an acute angle. The War-. rington men were not to be denied and a fine effort by Davies only failed when the full back was reached Salford came away with smart passing and rushed the ball to the Warrington line. Here Wilkinson made a dash for the rut and though tackled he rolled over under the posts and the point was allowed. The goal- shot, though easy, was missed, and Warrington still held the lead. Waywell led the way in another attack and one of the Salford men was sent off fur fouling Bards icy, Gibbs kicked a fine penalty goal and Warrington reached the half way stage with a lead of 7 points to 3. tSalford opened strongly with the wind in the second half, and Hennessy mane a couple of good runs but seemed disinclined to take risks. Brown cleared smartly and the Warrington forwards gave a fine display in the loose. Jordan Fcored smartly with an individual effort, and Clare completed two fast forward runs in dashing style with tries which were unimproved Final : Warrington, 2 goals, 4 tries, (16 points) ; Salford, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RYLANDS RECS WIN AT CADISHEAD._</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RYLANDS RECS WIN AT CADISHEAD._</p>
        <p>Ryland&amp; men won an important match on Saturday when they defeated Cadishead on their own ground before a gc,od crowd of spectators. Prior to Saturday each team had only lost one match in the senior divi- sion of the League and rivalry between them was very keen. Cadishead won the toss and immediately the " Wiremen 't- became aggressive, and after a short spell of attacking the home full-back mulled and Broadhurst dribbled over and scored a good try, which Leah con- verted. The same performance was repeated shortly afterwards, Stretford this, time scoring, Leah again converting Half-time arrived with Rylands'leading by ten points to nil. Resuming, the home team at once took up the attack and Pover scored behind the post, which Forster con- verted. Play became vesy exciting, and from a brilliant piece of concerted play, started by Griffiths, Leah scored and again converted. Near the close the home team missed a glorious opportunity of adding to their score, but time arrived winh the " Wiremen " deservedly winners, Result : Rylands Recs, three goals three tries (15 points) ; Cadishead, one goal one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Goals</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. for agst Pts Great Sankey Widnes Rees 17... 13... 4... 78... 22... 26 Widnes Combination 17... 12... 3... 2... 60... 30... 26 Balfour-rd Old Boys 17... 12... 3... 2... 50... 20... 26 Widnes Athletic 16... 11... 3... 2... 42... 23... 24 Farnworth Institute 19... 7... 7... 5... 32... 46... 19 Helsby St Paul's l9 9... 10... 0... 57... 56... 18 Farnworth N'cal Sch 6... 7... 5... 35 .. 41... 17 Widnes St Paul's 20... 4... 15... 1... 36... 88... 9 Runcorn Aloion Waterloo Missson Farnworth W Guild 17... 1... 15... 1... 13... 81... 5 WATERLOO MISSION V. GREAT SANKEY. Played at Widnes on Saturday. Both sides put up a capital fight and the interval arrived with the score sheet blank. Re-starting, the visitors tried desperately hard to open their account, but T Shyland saved shots from all angles in masterly fashion. The struggle con- tinued in grim fashion, and E Glover, son., made a fine clearance under pressure. J Bond saved from Tomlinson but kicked the ball on to Shaw, which rebounded through to the consternation of the visiting Players. Stung by this reverse. :•ankey pressed both, T Cook receiving from the right, defeated T Shyland with a shot which gave himlno chance. The visitors displayed splendid combination, and after a hoe, but unsuccessful bombardment of the Widnes goal, a determined rush by the Sankey forwards were crowned with success, T Whitfield giving them the lead. Result : Great Sankey, two ; Waterloo Mission. one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY. _</head>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY. _</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Ag'st Pts Lostock Gralam Lynn 14... 8... 2... 4... 29... 22... 20 Barnton Villa Oughtrington Park 14... 7 5... 2... 29... 19... 16 Mobberley l5 6... 7... 2... 21... 26... 14 Broadheath Bowdon Vale Latchford Oughtrington Amat 14... 4... 7... 3.. 28... 38... 11 St Vincent's Altrincham Lads 1" 1... 7... ' 4... 15... 28... 6 BROADITEATII V. BOWDON VALE.</p>
        <p>This retd-r-n-IOCE-ki.;:ile.rb;"—Cook- Place Kt Navigation- Toad on Saturday. Result: Broadheath, three; Bowdon Vale, one.</p>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON AATEURS v. ALTRINCHAM LADS. At Heatley on Saturday. The Amateurs combined well and after a spell of even play opened their account in the first two minutes. The Lads were awarded a penalty, from which Broom equalized. With the scores equal the game became more interesting, especially when, from a second penalty, the La Is took the le ld. Not to be denied, the Amateurs fought desoerate ly and shortly before the interval, following good work by Gresty, the home team equalized. Half-time: two goals each. In the second half the Amateurs scored twice with-ut any reply from the visitors, only some smart goalkeep- mg by Hammett saving the Lads from a heavier defeat. Final : Oughtrington Amateurs, four; Altrincham Lads, tvi O. LYMM v LOSTOCK CRAWL</p>
        <p>The unbeaten Lostock team visited Lyrum on Saturday and suffered a reverse. The game was keenly fought and at half-thee there was no score. In the second half Lymna made repeated raids upon the visitors' goal, but found thq defence of Buckley, Reeves and Drinkwater superb. Lostock paid a visit to the other end and Hadden (goal) was penalized for an infringement of the rules. He houever, saved the penalty—a magnificent clearance. The game was very exciting. In the last 10 minutes Lymm were awarded a penalty for a trip against Reeves. Hinde took the kick and with a magnificent shot gave Lyrnm the lead. In the last five minutes Lostock tried desperately hard to get on level lerms, but the home defence held out, one of the most interesting games seen in I,ymna ending in the first defeat of Lostock Gralam with the score : Lymm, one; Lostock Gralatn, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ALTRINCHAM AMATEUR CUP.</head>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM AMATEUR CUP.</p>
        <p>THIRD ROUND. BARKTON VILLA V. MOI3BERLEY.</p>
        <p>At Barnto—n On Saturday. The final being Barnton Villa, three ; Mobberley, one. LATCH FORD RECREATION V. LATCH FORD. The meeting of these two local rivals at Latchford aroused considerable interest in the Warrington district. For a time the junior organization the Recs) put up a capital fight, but in the end the senior players proved too strong for them, and they ran out winners with the scores : Latchford, three ; Lcchtord Recreation, one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>During the last two visits to the miniature range at the Drill Hall, the above club has held two compe3ti- tions,lwhich concluded on Friday last—one for the " Bell " Medal, and the other for the "Daily Express" Medal. The prizes were ale o given in both competi- tions. The conditions of each competition were : Distance, 25 yarde ; ten shots deliberate, and ten shots rapid, in 120 seconds ; the medals were to be won by the best score off the rifle. " BELL " MEDAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Delib. R'p'd. He). Total H Bainep...Silver Fork A Shepherd I Silver Toothpick 97... 97... 2 &amp; 2... 198 (Medal) 1 Tie Shoot 99 94... 2 &amp; 2... 197 .1 Allardice( -( Tie Shoot 88... 80... 3 &amp; 3... 174 S James 94... 96... 2 &amp; 2... 194 F C Bennion G H Ingram C P Day J Towers (11. 93... 4 &amp; 4... 192 J Hartley 95.. 88... 4 &amp; 4... 191 F J Grooott 97. 86. 3 &amp; 3... 189 C Shepley qO. 87. 5 &amp; 5... 187 A Highley H Whitfield B9. 81. 4 &amp; 4... 181 P Harrison "DAILY EXPRESS" COMPETITION.</p>
