        <p>DON'T LOOK OLD—Keep the ravaies of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER,which deepen sto a natural colour ins, few days.Lockyer's maintains a youthful anpearance.Large hots verywhere. Post free 1/6. Bedford Laboratory. LoudoL S.E</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven won a handsome victory of .1-1 against Mid-Cheshire at Knutsford Cricket Ground. The turf was in excellent condition and favoured a fast game. After a moderate display in the opening half Latchford improved wonderfully after half-time and kept the home defence on full stretch to the end. Nelson scored for Mid-Cheshire and Pearson equalized, 1-1 representing the state of the game at the interval. Then Micards Ehot two fine goals and Wilkinson ran through the home backs and scored brilliantly. The Latchford halves played well and Wilkinson on the right excelled himself and completely subdued the wing opposed to him. Fletcher and Billington, the backs, were also in good form and protected the goal so well ihat Bruhl had a very cold afternoon. Fletcher was the most prominent hack on the field, Cutter, Fennell and Bebbington were the best of the home team. The Second Eleven match was cancelled.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven play their return match with Winnington to-day and the team is :—.l Bruhl, goal ; W Fletcher, C Billington, backs ; J B Wilkinson. A E Bradshaw, A linicht, halves ; R A Dale, J E Pearson,W H Minards, A -Starkey, E Banks, forwards. Leave Academy at 1.30 p.m. The Second Eleven v Winnington " A at Latchford is :—H Wright, F C Newbold, W Mull, hacks ; 11 Horrabin, S Furness, T Banks, halves ; G Laver, E F OHM, El Fletcher, D Travis, A G Marsden, forwards Bully-off 2.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING ASSOCIATION. (N.A. CENTRE)</p>
        <p>As we predicted in last week's issu?, angling is all the better for the few sharp frosts we had the week previous. The streams have now somewhat fined down, the canals have not reaped the same amount of benefit as the swifter running screams, although they look in fine c andition ; the rivers and streams are now clearer than they have been since 1913 arrived and the fish have been inure on the go this week than for some week's past, members turning up at their respective club- houses to try them over the scales. Although the roach of llb soz has not been beaten, some fair specimens have been weighed in, which were much admired by all present. The great catch of F Jolley was not taken from the Gowy, where Mr Hassall's and joins the stream, but from near the Soaker Bridge end, and the catch taken during the week by a member was from a water that is rented by the Northern Anglers,Ellesmere Port side of the Shaker. From reports during the week we hear the bri ok is in fine order and our Chester friends have not returned to Chester without taking several fish to mark up on their boards. As was stated some time ago the scaicity of specimens was due to adverse conditions and not to the water being any worse than it was last season or the season before, as the last three specimens go to show, Ist over 210z.,2nd over 18oz 3rd over 16 oz. These records have been taken in ten days from the date of the first weighed in and are all recorded at headquarters. It is questionable if their is a water similar to the Gowy in the north that has recorded such specimens in the time as these, and.we are sure that it is one of the finest specimen hunting waters for a considerable number of miles round Warrington, but as was stated it is no water for the novice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CANALS.</head>
        <p>THE CANALS.</p>
        <p>The Shropshire Union find Norton Canals were tried by several of our members but nothing beyond a few of "no class" was found. The Shropshire Union was tried Stoke way but it failed to entice the members to stay for the late train, having had quite enough by about 3 p.m. The Norton contingent returned also by the 4.45 p.m. not doing enough to persuade them to stay on till dusk. We have got to weigh in the first fish from the canals yet and the specials are going a begging.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>GOWY.</head>
        <p>THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>A larger number thau usual visited here last week end, perhaps it was on account of the good fish weighed in the week previous when roach of over lib weight was put over the scales and a pike of about 21b. was caught, but there was no big catch registered to com- pare with the one caught by F Jolley. A member had a fine day's sport on Wednesday last although there was rain for about one and half hours, and the following weights were marked up on Wednesday evening last. First roach lib 7drms, four roach 31b lloz 4drms, six roach 4lb 15oz 14drms, Day's catch, eleven roach 71b soz Bdrrus, There were many present when this catch was weighed who do not go in for angling, but when catches like these are brought to the club house they have a tendency to make members, and many anglers have spent a pleasant hour enlightening novices on the way the fish were caught. We notice that the plate of six fish caught in one week end last season was 31b, this catch on Wednesday beating it by lie 15oz 14drms. A meeting of joint committees was held at the Cooper's Arms to make final arrangements for the rambling match and to elect the executiTe council and choose the delegates for 1913. A report will be at hand during the week and will be inserted in next week's "Examiner." A.S.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S TEAM. WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE.</p>
