      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY MARCH 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>The mind of the coun- SCARE POLITICS. try will probably</p>
        <p>remain rather calm under the present attempt to make it go off into hysterics on the subject of airships. It has had' at least one previous experience of the same sort of thing. The attempt to create a naval scare about two years ago fizzled out as the more recent effort in the same direction has done, and was succeeded, as on the present occasion, by representations that we are throwing money away when we are building fleets, and that as, according to Touchstone, motley was the only wear for the forest of Arden, so a thorough, and even tremendo us, equipment of airships is the only reasonable and efficient form of national defence. One panic-stricken gentleman wrote a hook on the subject, we remember, which deserved to rank in everything but literary quality with a brilliant work of fiction, the object of which seemed to be to warn the country against a German invasion by means of coal barges. There will be more or less regular recurrences of these panics. There always have been. They are got up some- times by dull-witted but obviously sincere persons. More generally they are moves in the great political game. On the present occasion the " scare,journalists," the people who like to feel that their flesh is creeping, and those who regard it as a patriotic duty to belabour the Government,are, if not acting together, at least labouring to the same end, which is to throw John Bull into a panic about his defencelessness. It is probably a mere reckless stupidity, and a natural incapacity for appreciating facts, which leads one worker-up of the air- ship scare to remark that "with all the Dreadnoughts and other warships at our dis- posal, this country is to-day as defenceless against foreign attack as at any time in our history." That is sheer nonsense, of course ; as also is the further statement that "the danger, when it comes, will be so immediate, so universal, and so impossible of prevention as to eliminate alr possibility either of a renewed struggle or of any resistance what- ever." Whatever means are taken for the subjugation of this country, it seems— armies in coal barges,Germ an Dreadnoughts, Zeppelin balloons—we are always to be taken by surprise, to be overwhelmed without notice, to be crushed before we have made any defence or are aware of any danger. We are the possessors of the most tremendous fleet the world has ever known; but it is pre- sumed that it will be out of the way, and practically non-existent, when some Conti-</p>
        <p>,;4</p>
        <p>nental Power with a much smaller fleet determines to invade us Our coast is defended throughout by mines and sub- marines capable of keeping any enemy at bay; but we are to believe that when these means of defence are most needed they will not work. Lord. Roberts, when the last Government was in power, held, with Mr, Balfour, that in any circumstances that could reasonably be imagined it would be impossible to land more than 70,000 men on our shores. There are those who consider, or, at least, pretend to think, that that would be enough. Now, we are to be destroyed by a fleet of airships which does not exist, and are to be as incapable of defending ourselves against any airship fleet that may come into existence as if we were a colony of red ants This is a new and more dishonouring reading of Carlyle's "So many millions,mostly fools!'</p>
        <p>Scareships are being seen almost from on end of England to the other just now. Some times it is only a bright light that is seen, something like a star. The existence of the airship itself is inferred. Sometimes there are two lights, one to port and one to star- board of a vessel that seems to be wholly imaginary. The scaremongers do not pause for a moment to inquire what an airship can want with port and starboard lights. They incontinently throw up their hands and scream that we are lost. There is a rushing wind of indignation in one quarter because the scareships have not been fired on, and because, more particularly, the coast is no dotted at intervals by batteries that could destroy. What would happen, we wonder, if we fired promiscuously on every airship that hove in sight? What would happen if the Spithead forts fired on the Kaiser's yacht the next time she puts into the Solent? The one proceeding would be as justifiable as the other. We only commit warlike acts when we are at war. As for the rest, the pretence that this country is doing nothing, or is behind all others, in regard to the mastery of the air is opposed both to common sense and to common knowledge. When it is deemed necessary to make Germany's equipment look formidable we are told that she has twenty- three airships," national and private." If we reckon up the aerial equipment of this country, "national and private," it will soon begin to look rather formidable. And as to Government neglect of the aerial problem, it seems necessary only to remark that the Government has bought quite a fleet of flying-machines at the exhibition now being held in London, which certainly does not look as if the flying problem were being neglected or despised.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, OUR MILITANT M.P., must certainly be MEMBER. numbered among the " militants." If anY one ventures to question the accuracy of something he has said, or to express disbelief in his avowed policy, he replies with 0, truculence and power of personal invective which would be amazing if they had not noW become recognised as characteristic. There must be a strain of Irish blood in the Smith family, for you need but tread on the tail of either brother's coat • and he, will be on his feet in a minute, whirling the shillelagh, and shouting "Hurrah for the battle of the Boyne," or something more personal and less polite. Councillor Roberts took it upon himself at the Birrell meeting to put certain questiors to the member for Warrington. Mr Smith does not like being asked questions, particularly by the Roberts family so he classifies the criticisms of Councillor Roberts as "merely fatuous and stupid." But he devoted a lot of time on Saturday to twisting the questions into charges and answering them with all the slimness to• be expected of a barrister- politician. No doubt Councillor Roberts will be ready with the retort courteous at the proper time.</p>
        <p>We were much MR. SMITH AND interested to see what FOOD TAXES. Mr. Smith would have</p>
        <p>to sak about food taxes seeing that they form the rock on which his leader's (Mr Bonar Law) reputation has so recently split. Just prior to his election in December 1910,Mr. Smith published a specific undertaking on food-taxes in the following words</p>
        <p>- 44 I pledge myself that I will never support a policy which will increase by a farthing the cost of the people' f iod." On Saturday he denied that he ' ever said any such thing either at the beginning or the end of the election." The fact remains that the above pledge is in his own words, and is not only specific, but incontrovertible by any denials at this stage. After making the denial Mr. Smith remarked, with unconscious humour, that " the Liberal party did not understand honesty in politics." We may, perhaps, be excused for finding some difficulty in under- standing Mr. Smith's kind of political honesty, for he went on in the next breath to say that he still stood in line with Mr. Bonar Law on the question of food-taxes. Like a prudent man, Mr Smith has a qualifi- cation of his food-tax pledge ready to hand. He is not going to vote for any proposal to tax wheat or any staple food unless he comes back to Warrington to ask the people to re-elect him. But he still stands shoulder to shoulder with Mr Bonar Law. Let us there- fore see where the "great leader" stands on the question.</p>
        <p>When Mr. Bonar Law MR. SMITH AND hauled down the food- HIS LEADER. tax flag in that memor- able speech at Ashton he opened the floodgates to such an outburst of mingled criticism and compliment that he became alarmed and proceeded to haul it up again—if not to the top at least half-mast high I At Edinburgh on January 24th he stated the case thus: We shall give to the dominions of the Crown in our own market a preference, and the largest preference which is possible without the imposi- tion of new duties upon food. This was an absolutely meaningless promise containing nothing of any value to the Colonies and offering only a sham security to the home consumer. By February 7th, Mr. Bonar Law had evidently realised that this , plank in his Edinburgh platform was a rotten one, for he made the following signifi- cant admission in a speech at the Hotel Cecil: But if we find after tr3ing that it is impossible to have a prefetential system which will affect that object (the consolidation of the British Empire) without food duties, then ire shall endearour to carry food duties. Mr. Bonar Law bad either to haul down the flag and burn it, or stick to food duties. There is no half-way house between Colonial</p>
        <p>Prefer( People Trade in 1903</p>
        <p>. . Howe' been fc IS pled Harold admit Smith vote fo egrees Warrh airy re a corre " fo,</p>
        <p>gen, Fortun Marqui Mr. Ha or shal electior state ' they st next tF "What readily have stand t take a form.</p>
        <p>opp, clas care won Tar: thrc govi</p>
        <p>It is oi 'uses th not ha vinced cares would there a also a clothini and an: c,o at of zind 0 113 ind i 'taxing do that</p>
        <p>FLOUTI M</p>
        <p>U</p>
        <p>The in sigh Counci W.A.:. supply e,cceptl</p>
        <p>, Mn I ...'alica: illness ,Weeks -uoly i very h good In this genial,</p>
        <p>The to reac °lent. the nil °ll. Tu( ?rgan invite( exceed the p, freely litantL</p>
        <p>The the ni Eltati&amp; a. reve Njhher' rouOn ti end 31 !mitt ealt ..rotzt</p>
        <p>or, I be Y 3 are</p>
        <p>ir- )ll3, ase</p>
