        <p>of ing not ing</p>
        <p>- be the tnY of lief</p>
        <p>ble ,ct sh 2//</p>
        <p>le re al</p>
        <p>Preference involving a tax on the food of the People of this country and the present Free Trade system. Did not Mr. Chamberlain say in 1903 If you are to give a Preference to the Colonies • . . . you must put a tax on food. However 'reluctantly, Mr. Bonar Law has been forced to admit the same solid fact. He is pledged to food taxation, and so is Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., though he will never admit it in so many words. Unless Mr. Smith intends to resign his seat rather than vote for the policy of his leader (which he agrees with) his promise to the electors of Warrington is worth about as much as the airy rejoinder of the Marquis of Salisbury to a correspondent this week that " food taxes will not be the issue at the next general election." Fortunately it does not rest with either the Marquis of Salisbury, Mr. Bonar Law, or Mr. Harold Smith M.P., to say what shall, or shall not, be the issue at the next general election. These gentlemen will all have to state without equivocation exactly where they stand in regard to food taxation when ext they seek the suffrages of the electors. What I have said" is being a little too readily interpreted as "what I meant to have said," for even political honesty to stand the strain. Common honesty has to take a back scat altogether on a Tory plat- form.</p>
        <p>Colonel W. F. Prideaux RUNNING THE plaintively writes to HOME. the "Morning Post" (Jan. 22), admitting : It is a well-known fact that the greatest Opponents of Tariff Reform Among the working- classes are the women on whosi, shoulders the cares of household management depend. These women are firmly impressed with the idea that Tariff Reform means dear food. and argument is thrown away upon them. Women are not governed by reason, but by their feelings, and their feelings are generally at the mercy of the first clever person who cares to exploit them. It is only fair to say that Colonel Prideaux 'uses this as an argument why women should not have the vote. He seems firmly con- vinced that those " on whose shoulders the cares of household management depend" Would always vote against food-taxes. Is there any reason why they should not vote also against boot-taxes, furniture-taxes, clothing-taxes,crockery-taxes, blanket-taxes, and any other taxes that will increase the cOst of running the home? For these are the kind of taxes on which the Protectionist is now set. It would be of little use taxing articles which nobody bought, for to do that would not yield enough profit.</p>
        <p>How necessary the FLOUTING POPULAR 2arliament Act was MANDATES. appears very plainly in</p>
        <p>extrao-rdinary career of destruction through which the ouse of Lords has taken its careless and ghthearted way. The Home Rule Bill had Dehind it not only the demand of more than three-quarters of the Irish people, con- sistently maintained through a period longer sjniost than the political record of any Front 1.-51ench member of the Opposition in either -Lords or Commons; it had also behind it a 'nand ate from the majority of British electors at two successive General Elections. Welsh °Pinion has been registered at eight General Elections in support of the principles of the Welsh Church Bill. And it is specially 63i,gnificant that during the months which etaPsed between the introduction of these Veasures and their rejection by the Lords, u° Opposition never fought, much less won, any by-election on these issues. All the Piretext which the Lords can put forward for Wleir rejection, as interpreting the real will °f the British electorate, is Mr Bonar Law's 11s hope that by-elections may be won on ese issues before the Bills pass into law under the Parliament Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>L'ES AND</head>
        <p>L'ES AND</p>
        <p>The Warrington motor-buses are almost in sight. A resolution is to come before the Council on Tuesday "That the tender of W.A. Stevens, Ltd., of Maidstone, for the 811PPlY of three motor 'buses complete be accepted." * * *</p>
        <p>~.. Mr Brighouse, the coroner for South West .-..encashire has so far recovered from his Illness as to depart yesterday for an eight ;leeks trip to the Mediterranean and the ?-°IY Land. He is a man who has worked. verY hard all his life, and if anyone deserves 9 good holiday, he does. Thousands of people 14 this part of the country will wish the genial, if autocratic," Sam" bon voyage.</p>
        <p>.* * The Parr Hall organ question seems likely "3 reach an early and satisfactory settle- ItilLent• His Worship, the Mayor, will bring ue matter to a head at the Council meeting 0'34 Tuesday by a special motion" that an rgan be purchased for Parr Hall,and tenders :vitod from competent builders at a cost not ticeeeding £1,500." Since the last meeting e pros and cons of the question have been eelY argued, and there is likely to be a sub- 'antial majority for the motion on Tuesday. - *</p>
        <p>the new automatic machines which take .Pennies of the public in exchange for a7tlon platform tickets are likely to produce e,AeYenue which will make the Chancellor lous. At Bank Quay, this week, some- about 400 pennies have passed (311gh the slot. Think what that means te- the L. &amp; N.-W. system alone. The etißpirces of taxation are by no means at an Yet awhile. Luckily, as Mr. Harold Nv—e,l;ll says, the country is amassing great froth quickly. But the pennies are going the pockets of the many to the few. * * *</p>
        <p>"The Liberal Party in Warrington during the last two years had never had a kick in them let alone la blow with the fist."—Mr. Harold Smith, 111. P., at Conservative Club. his'n M.P. who can forget the flagellations ears Party has received within the last two Y 011 the delicate questions of foreign ttilks for prizes at a dumping exhibition, and iztee, exploiting of the teachers for a Tariff venl.rrn trip to Frodsham, must have a con- ki2ni_lent memory. Mr. Smith will get all the ks and blows he yearns for between now r'etict the time when he has to relinquish the Presentation of Warrington.</p>
        <p>fTheMember for Warrington has a gehius 'ne qualifying statement, to which there iB nothing more difficult to pin a man. He in favour of Colonial Preference, but if (a°lonial Preference were carried and involved ;B it would) taxes on food he would at once a3r that he had never been in favour of food taa/ces, and point to his pledge in 1910. The Whih whieth compulsory military training, of 15 an ardent advocate, with the ,eservation "not by means of Conscription." !,,o ') that, if National Service became law, and Ititentually developed into Conscription (as it -11,11(1(lubtedly would) Mr. Smith would say I\r_sat he had always opposed Conscription. tiou, Wonder he compluins that Liberals do understand honesty in politics!</p>
        <p>Among the many funny things which Mr Harold Smith, M.P. had to say on Saturday evening there was nothing so screamingly farcical as his new version of the virtues of Tariff Reform. He deplored the fact that while the country was amassing great wealth "it was going into the pocket of the few. This was attributable to the fiscal system which we so foolishly and blindly followed " —and per contra, the cure was to be found in Tariff Reform which, everywhere else in the world, has made the rich richer and the poor poorer. For colossal effrontery Mr Smith is hard to beat. There he stood, talk- ing about the unequal distribution of wealth, with Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart„ millionaire, at his elbow. We are inclined to think that Sir Gilbert at any rate was not oblivious of the irony of the situation. * *</p>
        <p>-- From the "Tariff Reform Speaker's Hand- book ": " Take away the proposed re-arrangement of our food duties and the Imperial aspect of Tariff Refcrm, with its mighty possibilities for the future of the British race, vanishes at once. The slightest consideration will show that the proposed shifting of our "food duties" is indispensable to any scheme of preference which can be acceptable to our Colonies. In order to encourage wheat- growing in Canada and Australasia, and thus extend the granaries of the Empire, it is necessary to put a small duty on the wheat of their foreign competitors. There is nothing else upon which we can give a preference that would be of any real value to our great daughter States!' _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>This is just what Mr Chamberlain said with greater brevity and so say we all. Colonial preference without food taxes is a delusion, an impossibility. The electors will not vote for food taxes and the Tories have given up asking them. Real Colonial prefer- ence will have to be based on a stronger foundation than one of bribes -and doles at the expense lof the home consumer if it has to achieve reality and substance. *</p>
        <p>"Sub Rosa" the other day told a very funny story in the "Daily News" at the expense of Mr. J. S. Harmood-Banner, M.P., Lord Mayor of Liverpool. It seems that the Lord Mayor recently presided over the forty- ninth annual public meeting of the Liverpool Sunday School Union, and in connection therewith "Sub Rosa " says : So far from saying anything against the hon. member, I wish to protest against the manner in which he was treated. I have before me a program of the proceedings, and I copy from that printed form the following particulars : 7.0.-49th Annual Public Meeting</p>
        <p>Chairman. Rt. Hon. the Lo:d Mayor of Liverpool (T s Harrnocd Baunner, Esq , M.P.) Hymn: Fling out the Banner! Now I really do hope that that hymn was not chosen by some Radical, in order to "get one in" at the expense of the distinguished chairman. If the coincidence bo accidental it is surely remark- able, for there are millions of gentlemen not named Banner, and there are hundreds cf hymns that make no allusion to banners. Yet here was a gentleman bearing without abuse the grand old name in question, and directly under his name appears this hymn, which seems to be almost of the nature of a personal allusion. And it is made all the worse by the fact that the gentleman is a member of Parliament, so that the injunction to "fling out the Banner " has a peculiarly grim significance in his case. He is far too good- tempered a man to let that interfere with the clllection—but there was an element of risk in the business. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL PARS. Nr.2</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARS. Nr.2</p>
        <p>Charity Investigation Committaa. Mrs. Joseph Hart ison, who has for many years taken the deepest interest in the work of this very useful charitable organization, recently celebrated her 70th birthday by giving a tea, supplemented by a number of dt,licAcies, to the poor women attending the sewing classes. The women congratulated Mrs Harrison and wished her " Many happy returns of the day," and in proof of their gratitude presented her with a gold brooch and a small breakfast cruet, collected for amongst themelvea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Memorial Service_ in Queen's Garden's,</head>
        <p>Memorial Service_ in Queen's Garden's,</p>
        <p>_ The anniversary of the battle of Peter's Hill, in which Colonel O'Leary fell while leading the South Lancashire Regiment to victory, will be kept to-morrow (Sunday). 4 Short but impressive service will be held in front of the Regimental Memorial in the Queen's Gardens at 12.30 p.m and will be attended by repre- sentatives of all battalions of the South Lancashire Regiment. Wreaths will be plAced at the foot of the memorial in honour of the officers and men of the regiment who gave their lives for their country in the South African War.</p>
        <p>The' Infirmary. The Bon Treasurer, Mr Roger C Parr, gratefully acknowledges the receipt of the following contribu- tions :—The Warrington Dramatic Society, per Mr Arthur Browne, hou treasurer, ;(:25, being a portion of the net proceeds of the recent performances of " Sunday " ; Shakespeare Memorial performances in 1911 and 1912, per Mr Arthur Browne, 12 8s 9d, a part of the balance; the Nursing Division, Warring- ton Corps, St John Ambulance Brigade, per Miss R (3 Ronald, ,5 ; a friend, per his Worship the Mayor,l-3; and from the IT K.C.T.A., per Mr G E Turley, 10s. VACUUM CLEANERS. Free trial at home. Price 42/- 63/-, 84/.--J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge lA., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Recital by Mr. Alfred Perris. At Thewlis-street School on Thursday evening in last week a concert took place, organized by the Workingmen's Mission Cricket Club, at which Mr Alfred Perris supplied the major portion of the enter- tainment, consisting of the following poetic and dramatic recitals: "Mary Queen of Scots" (LI G Bell), " Martin's Puzzle" (George Meredith), "The Perils of Invisibility " (Gilbert), "The Flight of little Emily " (" David Copperfield," C Dickens) and "Mrs Murphy and the Russians"a drily humorous Irish sketch, by Michael O'Mahony (Mr Perris having the author's snerfial permission to recite this sketch in public). There was a fairly good attendance and Mr Perris's versatility was exemplified in the wide range of varying character sketches he submitted for the delectation of his audience who showed a keen appreciation of his powers as an elocutionist. Death of Mr. John Cowden. It i rith regret we have to announce the death of Mr John Cowdell of the firm of Messrs Kay and Cowdell, Grocers, which took place at the residence of the deceased it. Folly-lane, Warrington on Thursday morning. Mr (lowdell was an ardent churchman and held the office of sidesmen for many years at St Peters Church, and he interested himself in many ways in connection with the church. He was of a kind and genial disposition which won for him many friends. He commenced his career as a boy in the employ of Mr Charles Burgess, Grocer of Bridge-street, also with Mr John Gandy of Church-street and Messrs T and S Woods, late of Horsemarket-street. The deceased gentleman has been in business in partnership with Mr J W Kay for nearly a quarter of a century. He married Alice the daughter of the late Councillor Joseph Wilkinson. He took no active part in politics and he was a Freemason, Lodge 148. Death of Mrs. Joseph Woods. -</p>
        <p>The death is announced at the age of 88 years, of Mrs Joseph Woods, the eldest daughter of the first Borough Treasurer of Warrington (Mr J Borrows) and stepmother to Mr E Woods, J.P. The funeral took place at the Cemetery on Monday, aud among others present were Messrs A Veinon (representing Mr E Woods), T Woods, J Leigh, \V H Bellian nephew), J W Woods, R B Woods, W Clegg, II Houghton and H Whittle. The following ladies and gentlemen for- warded wreaths: Mrs G Woods, Mr and Mrs E Woods, Mr and Mrs Leigh, Willie, Robert, Harry, Mary and Annie Leigh (grandchildren), Mr and Mrs J W Woods, Mr and Mrs J Woods (Washington, U.S.A.), Mr T and the Misses Woods, Mr and Mrs W H Bellian, Mr and Mrs H Houghton, Mr J and Miss Harding, and Mrs Isaac Houghton.</p>
        <p>The Railway Mission Conference: The annual tea and conference were held on Satur- day at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street. The chair was taken by the Rev W; Bracecarnp. and short speeches were made by Messrs Chamberlain, Forster aad Rigby, who spoke of the general good works of the Mission.—Miss Bogstrom (the Leader) also eulogised the work of the Mission during the past twelve months, The concert was very enjoyable, and thanks were tendered to the artistes who gave their services.</p>
        <p>Next Week at the Hippodrome The programme for next week includeq Betty Bar- clay and a baritone in a sefined, original and beautiful vocal scena ; Miss Gwennie Llewellyn, vocalist; Fred Evans, in a novel " dream" of Oliver rwist ; Sims Wooley, eccentric comedian; Two Canadians, instru- mentalists ; Three Brothers Iluxton, refined acro- pantomimical humorists ; The Fitzroys, society gymnasts ; Couqelli, society mimic: and the Sisters Reeve, refined vocalists. Masonic Installation. 'rue Gilbert Greenall Chapter held their installation at the Lion Hot 4 Assembly Rooms, Warrington, on Tuesday evening. The three principals were installed E. Comp. J. Waldron. ME Z, T. D. Amos, H and C. J. McDowell, J. The following officers were invested: E Comp. Charles Leigh, ; W. H. Owen, S E ; J. W. Bowen, S.N, ; W Haiusworth, P.S. ; H. Isherwood, First A S ; T. Stringer, Second A.S. ; C. Holland, P.Z., treasurer ; Comp. Parkinson, organist. " BAYARD" CARS.—Se the 8 h.p. 4 cylinder. two seater model, £l97.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Nursing Association. The Hon. Treasurer cf the Warrington District Nursing Association (Mr H Roberts, gratefully acknowledges the sum of £lO, being part of the pro- ceeds of the Shakespearian Memorial Performance given in April 1912, and also a further sum of £lO from the Warrington Dramatic Smiety, being part of tbe nett proceeds of the recent performance of "Sunday." Both these Sums have been f,rwarded by Mr Arthur Browne, chairman and hon. treasurer of the Committees concerned.</p>
        <p>Plcturedrome. " Bob's Deception " will he the principal picture on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday. Bob, a widoAer with one child. marries again. His wife is jeal,,us and does not know he has a daughter, and finding letters from daughter to father becomes suspicious. Everything is, however, sat,sfartorily settled. (hi Thursday, Friday, and Saturday there will be in exi•lusive film entitled The Fail nf E.sex " It is a fine picture 4,f QuPPn Elizah• t h and the j-alous Earl of Nottingham. Tile managetn-nt is pleased to announc,. that " Les Miserables " will be .sho, n during the week ecmmencing Match 17th. 'I he booking plans will be opened shortly.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. Mr It Wright f the Eve, ton I.L.wiera Club lectured on Tuesday ra, "Floral P.,oto!raohy " Fliq talk, ;14 he himself called it, was ei:l ir h irec,ed towards the methods to be etnploysd for tl:e slice-.still render- ing of still life by 1. hotographic pr. eesses ; what to avoid and what t leave ow rafter than what to do was his theme, and was vnr:% mice, s,fullv treated. The gems of Mr Wright's collection of pictuns were reserved to the end He is only a begin, er in auto- chrome. w9r,k,..but the dozen or so of still life picturts in the natural colour of the I.timire plates were altogether successful. Bight and full of sparkle, they are the best autochr ,tne piciure., the society has yet. seen, and if a mere beginner can show such successful work it is evident that workers have a splendid field for their energies 86 colourists midi the camera.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Society "The OA Academy retains its reputation PR the shrine of local taste and public spirit, and we trust that the society w ill continue to justify its existenc,. and to maintain its hold upon the favour of the towu by carrying out with constantly increasing zeal the noble aims which gave it birth.' Such is an extract front the annual rep irt of tho Wardnorm Society which was adopted at the annual meeting of the society in the Old Academy list evening. The report also stated that the committee had d-cided to erect a tahlet on the promitiffs it which Afe Willi im 13sa.moikt. Warrington's first Mayor, and or st illustrious local antiquary, practised as a solicitor for fifty years. Premiums of 2s and Is have been off.irtd to the students of the School of Alt for the best designs The result will be announced in due course. There were now 128 names on the books, and the acc,inuti showed a net profit for the year, after writing off the usual percentages of depreciation, of g. 17 Si 6d. - The Sunday School Union. __ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>most sti-c,c- est•ful conference was held in connection</head>
        <p>A most sti-c,c- est•ful conference was held in connection</p>
        <p>with the Warrington and District Sunday School Union, on Saturday, in Wycliffe Memorial hall. The afternoon meeting, which was well attended, was pre- sided over by Rev T T James, M.A., who in the course of his address, remarked that such a good attendance on a Saturday afternoon testified to the enthusiasm which the teachers had for their work. The Secretary, Mies V M Taylor, read a letter of apology for absence from the President. Rev W Reid, who had met with a slight accident. Miss Crosbie, Liverpool, who has already won herself a name in Warrington as an expert Sunday school worker, for an hour held the audience in rapt attention, as in a racy and graphic manner, she dressed in new garb, familiar Bible stories suitable for children of different ages. After tea, the teachers again assembled. The Rev W A Beckett presided over the meeting but as he had to leave early to fulfil another engagement, Mr T Light, treasurer of the Union, cccupied the chair for the rest of the evening. Addresses were delivered on primary work by Miss Tate, Liverpool and Miss Lang, Sealerth. A well-sustained discussion brought to a close a most profitable and inspiring conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>3 Florence and Its Art."</head>
        <p>3 Florence and Its Art."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Phythian concluded his series of lectures on "Florence and its Art " in the Co operative Hall on Saturday evening, when he dealt in an interesting manner with "The Painters of the Renaissance." The lectures have been arranged by the Museum Committee, under the auspices of the Ogden Trustees, and on this occasion, as for the entire cours“, the Co-operative Ball was again crowded. Mr. J. Upson presided, and after paying a tribute to the valuable services Mr Phythian had rendered to the public of Warrington by his lectures, he said the Museum Committee congratulated themselves immensely upon the success of the lectures that had been delivered this season, and they remembered that a great deal of credit was due to their lecturer. The committee were pleased that they had been compelled to move from the Masonic Hall to this larger building. Not only had the lectures been greatly enjoyed, but they had added immensely to their store of knowledge of art. They could assure Mr Phythian that be had not only the hearty and cordial support of the Museum and Library Committee, but also the enthusiastic thanks of the whole of the people of Warrington. (Applause). —Mr Phythian returned thanks, and said if they had art, not in the sense of things which they ran off with into their own houses, but art which was for the good of the country, and had been obtained by generous public effoit, then he thought they would be better citizens, better as a community, more highly civilized, and more religious —The latter treated his subject in his usual eloquent and pleasing style, and the limelight illustrations were exceedingly good.</p>
        <p>Warrington Philomathic Society, At a meeting of the Warrington Philomathic Society held at the Old Academy on Wednesday evening last, Mr Cecil H S Willson, M.A., Head Master of Lymm Grammar SEllool, gave an exceedingly thoughtful and interesting lecture on " Athletics and Athleticism." For the purpose of his address, the lecturer defined Athletics as the keen desire for the mens saner in eorpors sano, natural to the majority of Britons, and athleticism as the evil appendages such as profes sionalism, the " gutter press," etc., which threatened to defame the fair reputation of athletics. He then dealt briefly with the position of athletics in the ancient world, especially among the Greeks, who justly had the reputstion of haying understood the science of physical culture. The lecturer then dealt with the re.v olution that bad taken place in the last thirty years in the physical training and development of girls and women, incidentally condemning the excessive attention given by a certain section of women to athletics and the evil results frequently flowing therefrom. The agitation provoked at the time of the South African war by Mr Rudyard Kipling's well-known verses in reference to "mudded oafs" and " flannel'd fools" was alluded to, and the lecture concluded by a trenchant quotation from Dr McNamara's speech on the Army Stores Scandal denouncing the happy-go-lucky folly and indifference which was too frequently characteristic of us as a nation, where there was a choice between sport and serious national interests. An intere•tting di-cussion ensued, in which Messrs H H Taylor, N Nield, Chadwick, W H Tickell, T E Knowles, II Dutton, C S Woods, and E G Burgess took part. A hearty vote of thanks was accordedlo the lecturer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PENNIES FOR PLATFORMS</head>
        <p>PENNIES FOR PLATFORMS</p>
        <p>THE NEW AUTOMATIC MACHINE AT BANK QUAY STATION FOR</p>
        <p>PENNY PLATFORM TICKETS TO THE PUBLIC.</p>
        <p>Burrell. ISSUING</p>
        <p>A new contrivance—not unlike a clock without a face—has this week made its appearance in the booking hall at Bank Quay station. It is the automatic machine in which any non-passenger desiring admission to the platform is now required to deposit a penny, in return for which he, receives a platform ticket. The innovation has been the subject of considerable comment, but it is found that bona fide friends of passengers desiring to welcome them or see them off are not disposed to forego the privilege for the sake of avoiding the penny toll. On the other hand, those who have been in the habit of paying casual visits to the platform for comparatively unimpor- - tqnt purposes will probably be deterred in future by the knowledge that it will cost them something. The machine promptly rejects all coins except the penny and—what- ever may b. thought of its purpose—no fault can be found with its expeditious working. Up to yesterday afternoon over 360 tickets had been issued.</p>
        <p>A Lady's Bequests. hariii,s bent fit largely by the will of Miss Ann Farier Brn'eoake, f Cioft, near Warrington, whose estate has been valued tit £3(1,983. After tequeath- mg various annuities to friends she left 3:,1,000 t ) the end winent fund of Low:on St Mary Parish Church. £5OO to the iiistitution for widows and orphans. of clergymen of the di,,ce.e of Chester, Liverpool. and Manchester, and the residue of her estate (sithject to other bequests). she left up'in trust for Edith Eliza Longdon, for life, with remainder to her children or their issue ; and in the event of the fai!ure oi these trusts then she left the ultimate residue of her estate for the benefit of needy gentlemen or gentlewomen being enmmunicatits of the Church of England resident for at least ten years in the parishes of Market Weigidon, Leigh, Lowton and Croft near Wartiugton. WaNington Wesleyan Cycling Club, The annual meeting was held at Bold-street Chapel lon Fiiday, February 21st. Mr Kershaw occupied the eksie and an attendance of 30 gratified all c incerned. The fo lowit.g officers were elect,id :—Cipt,in and secretaiy, Mr C Wileockson ; vice-Captain, Mr J Ham bleu ; treasurer, Mr 8 Winstanlev. The treasurer .reported a handsome balance in hand and the secretary ri very successful season last year. The programme of runs was drawn up for 1913. Mr Ham blett then, in fitting words,presented the captain with a couple of Lucas's Holophot- lamps, for himself and his wife. with pint tins of oil to match. 11, lact fly the b xes of matches were omitted. Mr Wi!cockson acci-pted the handsome gift with words necessarily stmt.:, on account of the unexpected nature of the incident. Everybody is looking forward ti a pleasant season. The Dramatic Society. At the annual meeting of the Warrington Dr imatic Society held at the Pattern Arms Hotel, on Monday evening last (Mr Arthur Browne in the chair), the hon. secretary (Mr J W Buller]) reported that the recent performances of "Sunday " had been a financial success, that no production of the Society had cost less for a yeiy many years, and that the com- mittee recommended that a sum of .£5O should be given to oharities. It was unanimously resolved that .t 25 should bs given to the Infirmary. £lO to the Warrington Nursing Association, :4-.:10 to tile War- rington Children's Summer Camp, and £5 to Mrs Joseph Harrison's Charity Organisation Society. The latter was given as a slight t ,ken of the members' appreciation of the most benevolent work of Mrs Harrison in connection with such Society, and in remembrasce of the late Mr Joseph Harrison,who was not only an old member, but a great Hupp nter of the Dramatic Society.</p>
        <p>Ancient Order of Hibernians. A social gathering was held uader the auspices of the Ancient Order al Bib- rubies in the United Irish League rola, Win wick- street, on Wednesday evening. Bro D president, cceupied the chair assisted by Br. 'B. Hanley, Mahoney and Ryan. In his opening remarks the chairman explained that the social was held to do honour to one of their members, Bro Calily who was leaving thia country for America An excellent musical programme was provided hy members and friends during an interval. I3i-o Cut rie pre-ei.ted Bro Calily with a handsome silver cigarette case and match .lasx. Sister Kilgallon, president of the Ladies' Division, on behalf of the ladies also wished Bro Calily every success. Bro Calilv, ii responding expressed the great pleasure he had enjoyed in being a member of the A.O.H. and although he might he many miles away he would ilk ays remember the kindly spirit that everybody had shown to cards him. The musical programme continued, and at the close Bro D Mahoney proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman, seconded by Bro Ryan. Presentation to Mr. R. Townley. Mr Robert Townley, who recently relinquished the office of goods agent of the London and North-Western at Warrington, to take up a similar position in 63 North, with a view to recuperating his health, was on ' Saturday evening the recipient of a handsome presenta- tion from all grades of railwaymen in the goods department of the company at Warrington. The function took the form of a hot-pot at the Royal Oak Hotel, and over 60 members af the indoor and outdeor staffs were present. A smoking concert was after- wards held; the following contributing songs: 3Jessra Caldwell, Clarke, Cox, Fox Horrabin, M J Kite, Spencer, Warburton, and Harrison (parcels agent). A duet was given by Mr J J Davenport and M J Kite and Mr H Harding proved himself an able cornet soloist. ,Mr F Atkinson acted as accompanist' The presentation to Mr Town'ey was made by Mr R Sumner, the oldest member of the staff enjoAing his superannuation. He referred to Mr Townley as an agent whom he had had the pleasure of serving, and stated that it was a great honour to him to perform such an important duty. Mr Townley was held in high esteem by his workers, and this was more than proved by thelarge number of men who had turned up that night to give him a send-off and ask him to accept the present which took the form of a mahogany secretaire hearing a brass tablet with a suitable inscription. Mr Townley, who was greeted with cheers and , musical honours, responding, stated that it was ~?5 years since he came to Warrington, and he was pleased to see several men who were there at that time, present that evening. His first stay was only for a short period. He afterwards worked in various parts of the district, and finally came back to the town as gods agent some nine years ago. Mr William Gleave (chief foreman) spoke on behalf of the outside staff and expressed his sincere regret that Mr Townley and he were parting corn any, hut be could assure him that he was leaving them with the good wish of every member of his staff. (Applause.) Mr W Tither also spoke on behalf of the indoor clerical staff. Mr H Harrison, chief parcel agent, Bank Quay passenger station, referred to the good relations existing between • ,I• his staff and the goods department.</p>
