      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>Insurance Committee. The medical practitioners of Warrington have elected Dr Edward Joseph Fox, 112, Rylands-street, and Dr John Bassett Naden, 111, Liverpool-road, to repre- sent them on the local Insurance Committee. School Children's Bulb Show. In conn#ction with the juvenile section of the Beautiful Warrington SJciety a school children's bulb show will be held at the Drill Hall on Saturday next. It iill be opened by Mrs Henry Roberts at 3 p.m The exhibits will include flowers and paintings, and there will be musical items. Musical Society's Concert: The third subscription concert of the season, in co - nection with the Musical Society, will be held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday, the 12th inst. The pro- gramme will consist of Haydn's Oratorio "The Creation " tparts 1 and 2) and the miscellaneous second part. The soloists will be : Miss Eva Rich, soprano; Mr John Collett, tenor, Mr Richard Evans, bass. The plan of reserved seats will be open to subscribers to-day (Saturday), and to the general public on Wednesday next, at Mr. R. H. Pearson's, Bridge-street. Sprine Cleaning. Try the Daisy VAcutmt CLEANER price 42/-, 63/-, 84/-.—J. S. GARLAND, 31 to 53,Bridge st., Warriugton.—Advt. Hospital Sunday. The animal pulpit appeals on behalf of the Infirmary were ina le at the various local places of worship on Sunday. At the Parish Church it was announced that the District Nursing Association would also participate in the t.fTertot ies taken there. Preaching at St. Paul's Cinch the 14 v W Bracecamp said there seemed to be a general opinion abroad that our hospitals and Infirmaries did not need the support they did prior t the passing of the National Insurance Act. But ' that was a mis,ake, and it should be remembered that ' the great majority of those who went to the infirmaries were not insured at all.</p>
        <p>• AN ICE-MELTING PLANT. Visitors tio the Alps in early spring know well by sight the dainty little nodding bells of the alpine soldanella. Its twin flowers on one stalk, will push their heads boldly through the ice and form a border of blue blossoms on the edge of the snow-sheet. This adventurous little plant does not wait like it neighbours for the melting of the snow, but more daring than they are, be- gins to grow under the surface of the ice-sheet, and melts away up for itself by internal heat, like a vegetabto furnace. It burns itself up in order to melt the ice above it. Opening its fringed and pensile blue blossoms in the very midst of the snow it fearlessly displays its two sister bells among the frozen h eet which still surrounds its stem in the most incredible fashion. The buds begin to grow on the frozen soil before the ground is bare, under the hard and com- pressed snow which at its edge is always ice- like in texture. They then bore their ,way up by internal hea, through the sheet that covers them ; and they often expand their delicate blue or white blossoms, with the scalloped edge's, in a cup-shaped hollow, while a sheet of re-frozen ire, through which they have .warmed a tunnel for thentselves, still surrounds their sterns and hides their lo9ts and their flattened foliage. Ti:e large, leathery, tough, and evergreen winter leaves hibernate under .the snow, and it is by using up the nutriment contained and stored up by these at the proper periol, that the sol- danella manages to melt its .way out of the wintry -ice-sheet, and eo steal a march upon its competing species. It is a low-growing type, which, must flower early, or elseit would soon b 9 overshadowed by its. taller rivals. Growth is very rapid In the Alps, once the snow has melted. When the flower has forced its way .through thz, ice the nutriment previouAy stored in the leaves is used up, and .othing but skeletons remain. By doing this the plant attracts the early spring bees a-nd honey-seeking flies to its blossoms, w,hich CHTY-y pollen from nead to 'lean, and s 9 fertilis'es itss seed S for it and thus early pro- s-ides for u future generation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>BROKE OUT ON LEGS AND ARMS.</head>
        <p>BROKE OUT ON LEGS AND ARMS.</p>
        <p>38, Brook St., Halifax, Eng.—" My ailment started with my feet and wrists first, then it gradually brokel out up my legs and arms and I also had it on the ' pit of my stomach. It broke out in. blebs like small swellings abdut as big as a sixpence and they used to itch something awful, more so at night time. Now and again lumps the size of a shilling would rise on my shoulders and body and itch terribly, then go away again, I suffered eighteen months. Seeing the advertisement of Cuticura Soap and Ointment I tried them and I found that the first tablet of Cuticura Soap relieved me, in all I had three tablets of Cuticura Soap and one box of Cuticura Ointment which cured me." (Signed) F. Glover, May 23,1012. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere, A sample of each with 32-n. book post free from nearest depot : Newbury, 27, Charter- house Sq., London; R Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N.S.W.; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SHAKESPEARE THEATRE, LIVERPOOL,</head>
        <p>SHAKESPEARE THEATRE, LIVERPOOL,</p>
        <p>Monday next, March 3rd, the Spring Season at the above popular theatre will commence, the management having been successful in securing the engagement of th. world-famous Imperial Russian Ballet, who have recently m.t with tremendous success in London. For 6 nights at 7.30, and two matinees, Wednesday and Saturday, at 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>_QUERIES AND ANSWERS,</head>
        <p>_QUERIES AND ANSWERS,</p>
        <p>OUR HEADE-RS are invited to hood legal querim to be ars,vered week by week. Inquiries must km short. concise, and plainly written, and the name and address of the writer given to show that the inquiry is genuine. The replie3 are by a member of the profession, and every effort is made to secure accuracy, but we cannot assume any responsibility for the opinion or advice given. Mark all envelopes containing queries " Legal," and address them to the EDITOR," EX MINER,' WARRINGTON. If you do not wish initials to be used in the reply please suggest a 'nom-de-plume. HEALTH.— N*o. The only practitioner from whom you can claim treatment is the one you chose in your native town. Until you take steps to put yourself under the Warrington Health Committee you are in a very awkward position, because if you 'fall sick now, no \Carrington doctor will consent to accept you on his list. You must at once communicate with your Society, telling them on what date you left your native town, and came to Warrington. The next step will be to communicate</p>
        <p>with theWarrincton Health Committee and choose</p>
        <p>a Warrington Doctor. X.Y.- There has been no recent change in the laws relating to divorce in this country, but such a change may be anticipated in the near future since the report of the Royal Commission has been published. LENT—You can certainly sue for money lent on an unless it uas borrowed for some purpose forbidden by law of which you had knowledge. PENSION.—If a person is convicted of being drunk and disorderly and sent to prison without the option of paying a fine, or ordered some greater punish- ment, he is disqualified from receiving an old age pension while he is in prison, and for a further period of 10 years after his release.</p>
        <p>B.R.G.—Yes, because you received no sickness becefit. coNvicT Oft— Jt is the duty of the jury to adopt the law laid down by the judge. But there is no legal redress if they neglect this duty, and the judge is bound to accept a verdict whether he agrees with it or not.</p>
        <p>UNFORTUNATE. Yes, because you are a loser by his death.</p>
