      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>LORDS NOT JUSTIFIED IN REJECTING WELSH BILL.</p>
        <p>"Were the Lords justified in rejecting the Welsh Church Bill " ? was the question answered in the nega- tive at the meeting of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society, on Thursday evening. Mr W H STANsFIELD (C., Dublin University), who opened the debate in the affirmative, submitted that from the general election up to the present moment there had been nothing to prove that the Bill was desired by a majority of the people. At the election there was a silence on the part of Government candi- dates on the question, as evidenced by the very few direct references to it in their addresses. The Bill had been opposed not only by Liberal Churchmen but just as vehemently by Liberal Nonconformists. Mr Stansfieldireferred to the 6,000 meetings of protest held since the introduction of the measure and said that not even in Wales, except when Mr Lloyd George went down to his own constituency, was it possible to get an enthusiastic meeting in favour of the Bill. in Warrington the supporters of the Bill held a meeting, but be was told that the Co-operative Hall was only half-filled, and even then they had to drag in the Trades Disputes Bill. Mr Birrell, when he came to Warrington, spoke on every subj,ct under the sun except Welsh Disestablishment. Perhaps he had heard that one of the chief organisers of the meeting, who was sitting on the front row of the platform, had not, only protested verbally but was one of the first in Warrington to sign a petition against the Bill. The lbw, that 550,000 of the Welsh people had signed petitions against the measure proved that the Welsh nation was not. behind it. It came with peculiarly bed grace that a Government which had introduced a Bill to prevent the English people from interfering in Irish affair should call in the assistance of the Irish Natianolists to pass the meanest Bill ever presented to Parliament. in deft ince of the wishes of the representatives of Great Britain who were vitally interested. The dishonesty of that political bargaining was nauseating and disgusting to the last degree. There bad never been such a shocking instance of political degradation in the history of Parliament. He further submitted that the Lords were justified in rejecting the Bill because it was grossly unjust and confiscated property belonging to the Cnurch. Mr P L DEvONPOIIT (President of the Board of Trade) seconded the resolution. Mr E WYNNE JONES (1.4., Chester) expressed regret that Mr Stansfield's enthusiasm was not devoted to a better cause. The Welsh people had repeatedly shown that there was a genuine, widespread and deep-rooted demand for the Disestablishment Bill, audou no ground consistent with deniocratic government could their demand be ignored. Only three of the 34 Parliamentary representatives of Wales were in favour of the policy for which Mr Stansfield stood. The only ground on which a Church could claim to be the National Church was that it represented the majority of the people.. There was not a defender of the Church who would claim that the English Church in \Vales fulfltled that at. When Mr WYNNE JONES invited the House to apply the test of accommodation, there were loud Lries of " Oh " from the Government benches.</p>
        <p>Mr WYNNE JONES retorted : You are not prepared to argue these points. You are all Boner Laws. (Laughter and applause.) You have got so used to using the words" mean," " robbery," 'contemptible," and the like. since Mr Bohai. Lay. became your leader, that you are not prepared to argue any Question. Con- tinuing, he challenged opponents of the Bill to prove that the Anglican Church represented even 10 per cent. of the population of Wales at the present time. He also invited the member for lruro (Rev EC B Carleton) to prove a solitary case in Wales in which tithe was the gift of a pious ancestor. Tithe was the one bugbear, as far as the Welsh people were concerned. They were not troubling about endo.vmeuta ; the Welsh Nonconformists would not benefit to the extent of one halfpenny by the Disendowment of the Church. By rejecting this insolent resolution, the House would be making for peace and harmony, inhttad of for hatred and discord.</p>
        <p>The PRESIDENT OF THE BOARD OF EDUCATION (Rev E CE Carleton), said that in maintaining that tithe was the creation of law,the last speaker did not differentiate between seculsr and ecclesiastical law. Tithe was certainly not the creation of Parliamentary law, for tithe was paid in this country in the seventh or eighth century, whereas Parliamentary law in respect of it was not passed until the 12th or 13th century. He main- tained that the Bill, although it was termed a Disestab- lishment measure, did not Disestablish the Welsh Church at all. Fines and forfeitures were, however, to be inflicted on Church people merely because they were Church people. To only difference between the pro- perty of Welsh Churchmen and that of Nonconformists, was that the Churchmen had been longer in possession Mr C DUKES (Soo., W. Ham), said Welsh Disestab- lishment had been used as a political catch-vote. If the Government had been sincere in its desire for a solution of the question, the simplest method would have been to take a referendum of the Welsh people. What business was it of people living in Lancashire to say what religion a Welshman should have? The man who claimed to be a Secularist had just as much right to demand State assistance as the Auglica.n. Religion of any kind should be left purely to voluntary maintenan,e. Mr G TOULMIN (L., Lincoln), said the resolution was characteristic of the Tory party who were always looking to the House of Lords for help. For a quarter of a century the Welsh people had sent represensatives to the House of Commons to enforce their demand for Disestablishment, lf the Conservatives were sincere in their professions to trust the people, they would be bound to grant this measure of religious freedom. The PRIME MINISTER (Mr E A White) said the Disestablishment Bill had been promoted by politicians without principles and engineered by a coalition of minorities, each with its ovsn axe to grind. He had faith that the Bill would not become law, under the ill-conceived provisions of the Parliament Act, and he looked forward with confidence to the next general election when he believed this mean, vindictive and time-serving Government under which we were at present suffering, would be swept out of existence. Mr STANSFIELD, replying to Mr Wynne Jones's suggestion that his euergies might be more usefully employed, said the c also for which he stood was the cause of Christianity—of right against wrong. He did not de: y that there was a genuine and widespread denn.u. in Wa es for Disestablishment but, supporters tbe Bill must also admit that there was a genuine and widespread protest against it. Mr Stansfield quoted an instance of a" pious ancestor's" endowment of the Church which, he pointed out, included tithe. ' The question was then put to the meeting and the Lord's attitude was cor.deruned by 15 votes to 13. The Government raised cries of "The coalition : " the Socialists having voted with the Liberals.</p>
        <p>Ut iI</p>
        <p>1 ,1 )!</p>
      </div>
