      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>4444,11• _ – . -•- _ _ – . .</head>
        <p>4444,11• _ - . -•- _ _ - . .</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMAN'S</p>
        <p>Backache and Dizziness.</p>
        <p>Over nine months ago, Mrs. S. Gibney, who is a native of Warrington. and lives at 10, Wakefield Street, Bank Quay, said :-" I was unable to do any work for two mouths because of an attack of kidney cpmplaint I had. There were such vain. across the small of my back that I could scarcely move—it was as much as I could do to turn over in bed, I could hardly get a wink of sleep. "The pains in my head nearly drove me frantic, and 1 used to come over faint and dizzy; the complaint left me very weak. "1 could tell my kidneys were at fault, for the water was scalding, and contained a gravelly sediment.</p>
        <p>"Just at this time I read a book about Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, and so I made up my mind to try them. I had been under medical treatment, but with no benefit. The first box of Doan's pills did me so much good that I kept on with them till the pains left my back, the water became clear and natural, and I was quite well again. "Doan's pills are a fine medicine for the back and kidneys and I have often recommended them. (Signed) (Mrs.) S. GIBNEY." OVER NINE MONTHS LATER, Mrs. Gibney said :—" I have taken Doan's pills now and again when I felt I needed them, and they have never failed to do me good. lam pleased to have another opportunity of praising the medicine."</p>
        <p>About 500 GRAINS OF POISONOUS WASTE and 3 PINTS OF WATER are filtered out of the blood by the kidneys every 24 hours.</p>
        <p>That is when the kidneys are active—</p>
        <p>When they are weakened by a chill, overwork, worry, or any other cause, they leave some of this poisonous waste and surplus water in the blood—</p>
        <p>Then you are likely to become drop- sical or rheumatic; you may get pains in the loins and back, twinges of sciatica, or the sharp stabbing pains of lumbago.</p>
        <p>The water becomes cloudy or gravelly, and leaves the urinary channels burning and sore. You may be obliged to get up three or four times in the night.</p>
        <p>Ne./N.,..%/</p>
        <p>Be sure you get the same Pills as Mrs. Gibney had.</p>
        <p>•ery Picture a Story."</p>
        <p>Doan's Backache Kidney Pills help the kidneys to throw off the poisonous kidney waste and surplus water, and are therefore of great value in dropsy, backache, gravel, rheumatism, lumbago, and sciatica, as well as in disorders of the urinary system. This remedy is solely for the kidneys and bladder—it is not intended for constipation or indiges- tion, but only for disorders arising from weakness of the kidneys and urinary system</p>
        <p>In one sized package snly-219 a box, or C boxes 13/s; NEVER SOLD LOOSE. Of alt chemists and stores, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-st., Oxford-st., London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills.—ask for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, and MAKE SURE the name DOAN'S is on the box YOU buy.</p>
        <p>fatal economic distinction between master and man, or as our recent legislation more scandalously expressed it "Employer and employee" should be no more. The notion played a most important part in Morris's life. Morris looked not to government and legislation to save mankind, but to the native genius and inner spirit of men and women themselves. All schemes of ready- made, mechanical, so-called "State-Socialism" he hated and loathed, for be knew that the only genuine source of freedom was found not in the law, but in the heart of man, that the Kingdom of Heaven was the result of desire not the product of legal enactment. Morris came to revolt in the same way as Carlyle and Ruskin, by comparison of the ideal with the actual, which was the only way in which any real man could come to revolt. Morris regarded his fellows not as economic units, managers, foremen, hands and the like, but as true brothers, genuine fellow workers, potential poets and artists. And he was right. The rebuilding of society was not mainly for the better clothing and feeding of bodies, but for the saving of souls. The curse of life and the failure of life were due to false ideas and wrong points of view. Get these changed and they changed all. That work of changing ideas went on slowly indeed, but surely, with many difficulties and sadnesses, as Morris foretold.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. Annual Congregational Tea Party. On Thursday evening the members of St. John's Presbyterian Church held their annual tea party in the Church Hall, Wilderspool Causeway. About 150 people sat down to a substantial tea prepared and served by the ladies of the Church.</p>
        <p>After tea the chair was taken by Dr Ferguson, who was accompanied on the platform by the Rev W Reid (minister of St. John's), Rev .1 J Glover (Moderator of Presbytery), Rev W Jobson (Manchester), and Rev W Cooper (Legh-street). ,Dr FERGUSON, in a few opening remarks, said he thanked Mr Reid and the members of the congregation for giving him the opportunity of being piesent that night. It gave him great pleasure to be there, and to see the increase they had made in numbers at St. John's since the last time he bad been at a meeting there. It was evident from the increase in the congregation that good work was being done there by Mr Reid. They were all, no matter to what denomination they belonged, prone to their little bit of exclusiveness, and they all thought their own methods the best. That was right, but let them all try to mytually help one another whether they were Presbyterians or anything else, let them all realise that great truth. If they had differ- ences they were minor ones—their main purpose was the same, that of helping one another and winning souls for Jesus Christ.</p>
        <p>The evening's programme of music, &amp;c., opened with a pianoforte solo by Miss Cotton, followed by a song from Mr W Ray. The Rev J J Glover, Risley, the moderator of Presbytery next gave a short address. He said one thing that struck him about those social gatherings was the very close connection which they showed existed between a minister and his congregation. There were ministers who had been made great and good and noble by the kindness of their people, and on the other hand ministers who had been undone through treatment of the opposite kind. He would say this to the people of St Johns,—they had great opponunities, and if they were to make progress as a church it must be through spiritual effort—through the congregation wot king all together, Mr Reid and they encouraging each other by the way each did their work. If they wanted their church in the future to be a more assured success than ever it had been in the past, then let them pray for it, work for it and give for it, an: they would fiva that not only would the church be a greater and grander success, but a large blessing would come to their own hearts and lives.</p>
        <p>Brief addresses were also delivered by the Revs W Jobson (Manchester), and W Cooper (Warrington). Songs were rendered by Messrs Cannon, It Mitchell and R Gillespie; violin solos were contributed by Miss Lockerbie, and were much appreciated, as was also the trio " Tree Old Maids of Lea," song by Misses Cole, Cotterill and Crooks. The concluding item was the song Lighterman Tom " most ably rendered by Mr W Ray. Miss Cotton proved an excellent accompanist. The programme was arranged and carried out under the superintendence of Mr A G Lewsey, the indefatigable choirmaster and organist at St. John's, On the motion of Mr W Russell (elder), a hearty vote of thanks was passed to the chairman speakers, entertainers and the ladies who prepared and served the tea.</p>
