      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a - good attendance at this meeting on Sunday, presided over by the retiring president (Mr B Farrar).—The chairman welcomed Mr A Scholey, of Sheffield, who, as secretary of the Wycliffe Brotherhood there, conveyed the greetings of the members. Be also expressed their regret at parting with Mr James, their president. He was quite sure the P.S.A. at Warrington would be stirred up to greater work and usefulness, and that their numbers would soon be improved. The , ,Chairman reciprocated Mr Scholey's greetings' and ' wished for better times for Wycliffe, Sheffield, and Wycliffe, Warrington. Mr Woolley gave a stirring address on, "Lift up, and light up," which was appreciated. He said it, was easy to give a lift by the way, and to light up the gloom of men around them. Let them see to it that they made life around us brighter. Mr McArthur sang well, "When I survey" and "Lead kindly light." Mr Owen and Mr Beswick took part in the meeting. A vote of sympathy was sent to Mrs Lewis on the death of her husband.</p>
        <p>" William Morris and the Romantic Future."</p>
        <p>Continuing his course of sermons on "The Leaders of Revolt "the Rev Dr Mellor dealt with " William Morris and the Romantic Future" in the Cairo-street Chapel on Sunday evening. There was a large congre- gation. Dr Mellor said Morris moved through the world a great-hearted, a great-souled man, intense', human, intensely loving, keeping to the end the splen- did enthusiasm of youth, the vast healthiness of clean and wholesome manhood. ihere was in Morris nothing half-hearted or weak. He was perpetually on fire, and the full blaze of his impetuous and noble nature went to enlighten everything he undertook. When he declaimed great passages of Ruskin to his fellow under- graduates in Oxford, when all day long, clad in rough blouse and sabots he laboured in the dye-house, when, in what he called his spare moments,he wrote line after line of joyous verse, rich in colour as his own dyed cloths, when, at the head of a mob of unemployed and starving men and women he invaded Trafalgar-square, when, in his own beautiful home he entertained his friends with good food and wine, and thanked God for that He had "ever made anything as strong as an onion." Wherever he was and whatever he did, it was ever the same Morris, one molten mass of magni- ficent enthusiasm, utterly unsparing of himself, a man for whom the only conceivable policy at any time and at all times was simply "neck or nothing,=beaven's success or earth's failure.' His was the genuine spirit that would gladly upset the foundations of the world when it found the world a less worthy contrivance than it might be and ought to be. There was always something of the consecrated warrior about Morris, the devoted knight of love and • beauty. With his comrades of the Social Democratic Federation and his own Socialist League, Morris took in hand the righting of the world and the tumbling down of all our social order. Beauty and fellowship were the guiding thoughts of Morris's life; to have existence beautiful, and to find in the harmony and fit- ness of external arrangements the testimony of • fellowship at the base was his ideal. He was always a poet and wished to make his poetry a kind of beautiful house into which men and women, weary with the noise and bustle of the world, might withdraw for peace, joy and rest, and revivify that innocent sense of all clear, delightful things, which the blind follies and excesses of a com- mercial civilization were for ever destroying. In the imagination of the past he found the hope of the future and in the perfection that might, at least, have been, he saw the goal of all worthy human effort. His endeavour was to bring labour and beauty together and they had evidences of this in the whole of Morris's life. In a letter to a friend he once described himself as "A master-artizan, if I may claim that dignity." It was a striking phrase"master artizan,"worthy of deepest and most serious consideration by workmen, masters statesmen and others, for it suggested a time, not •incredible or impossible as we believed, when the</p>
