      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Christ Church. Latchford.</head>
        <p>Christ Church. Latchford.</p>
        <p>At Chester Consistory Court, on Monday, the Rev George Cecil Briggs, vicar of Christ Church, Latchford, and the wardens, Messrs David Babks and James Lowe, applied for permission to put in a stained glass window and take out the plain glass window from the third %indow counting from the west, on the north side of the nave. The stained glass window will depict St. Werburga. They applied also that a tablet might be fixed to the wall beneath, with an inscription in memory of Jane Atkinson, who for many years was a devoted worker in the church and new parish. The cost would be defrayed by volnntary contributions of the parishioners. The application and design were approved, subject to a slight alteration in the inscription.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TRANSPORTER BRIDGE TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>TRANSPORTER BRIDGE TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>A distressing fatality which cost his life to William Porritt, a middle-aged married man, living in Viaduct- street, Widnes, happened on Tuesday evening. Employed by Messrs Arroll's, bridge builders, Glasgow, he was conducting repairs in the centre of the transporter bridge. Through some cause or other, he stumbled and fell 80 feet into the river. The tide was running out strongly, and Porritt was seen to wave his hand as if for help. He paused rapidly under the old Runcorn Bridge, where he made a grab in vain at the wooden buttress. He was not seen again. Porritt was well conversant with the workings of the new bridge, having assisted in ita building.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>HIS CHILD</head>
        <p>HIS CHILD</p>
        <p>RAcr</p>
        <p>INTERESTING SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASE. An interesting, and what the magistrates described as an awakened case, was beard at the Boroueh Police Court on Tuesday when, before Mr 3 Rymer Young (in the chair) and other cnagistrates,Christonher Gaudy 16, Harbord-street, was hummoned for failing to send his daughter to school.</p>
        <p>Mr R 13 Roberts, assistant director of education, stated that the girl formerly attended the Secondary School, but the governors required the defendant to withdraw her from the school, in circumstances which it was unnecessary to mention and undesirable to enter into in court, in the girls' own interests. Since then the girl had not been to any school and the father was summoned for having failed to ensure the girl's attend- ance at a school. He was asked to appear before the School Attendanee Committee and complying with the request, was askd to give an undertaking that he would cause his child to attend a public elementary school but he declined. The committee therefore had no alternative but to bring the case before the therefore, Mr Gandy: What was the reason I declined to give an undertaking that I would not send her to an elemen- tary school ? Mr Roberts said they were anxious in the child's interests that at any rate in a public court the offence for which the parent bad been required to withdraw the child from school should not be mentioned or inquired into. The father felt that he should have been allowed to enter into the question before the School Attendance Committee, but that committee was not the body who required him to withdraw the child. , The governors of the Second,ry School were that body and consequently the committee felt they had no right to enter into the merits or re‘son of causing the with- drawal. The parent had been required to withdraw the child from the Secondary School. The position was that the child was of school age and must attend some school, and she could not go to the Secondary School. Defendant said be was a ratepayer and had a right to inquire into and defend his child's character. Mr Roberts: She is bound to go to school. Defendant : Do the Board of Education allow them to write telling me to take her away from school arid then require me to send her to school? It. ia nor, the first time I have had trouble %rich the Education Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Mr Roberts: The Board of Education has no jurisdiction in this matter. It is a matter entirely for the governors of the school. Mr Candy: Have I no voice as to where I shall send my child? The fact of the matter is that if the child's character is good enough to go to one school it is good enough to go to another school. If I have to go to the Sessions I will defend my child's claracter. The Governors of the Secondary School ars taking undue advantage of me; they won't hear rue. Surely I should have a little bit of justice some,.here. I have not had the right to defend her against the one who accused her. They have not made the accusations to me. If they had I should make them prove what they said.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the father might have some method of ascertaining why the child was required to be withdrawn, but the Court was not concerned with that point. The Bench was concerned with the fact that the child was not DOW attending any school. Did the magistrates understand that the defendant had not been given a reason for the order to withdraw the child ? Defendant : I have heard the reason but have not had the right to defend my child. The Chairman : This is a very awkward case. We want to see as far as possible your side of the case, but you must recognise Lost the child must be sent to a school. We have decided to adjourn thn case for fou-- teen days, to give you a chance of settling the matter with the Education Committee. The Bench think the Education Commitee should give you a chance of dis- proving anything said in reference to your child. Mr Gandy : If at the end of fourteen days I make no arrangement with the Education Committee am I bound to seni her to an elementary school ? The Chairman said that the case would then be put before flat Court.</p>
        <p>Mr Gandy protested that be bad never been trelted with justice. lie held that the Education Committee bad no right to force him into the Court. Mr Roberts laid Mr Gandy had never even asked to be heard. He had written two letters.—Defendant : I have w ritten`four— both of which bad been considered by the School Management Committee at different meetings but in face of the evidence they had before them they arrived at their dec sion in spite of the letters. Mr Gandy bad never asked to be heard.</p>
        <p>HUSBAND'S CRUEL CONDUCT. ANOTHER WOMAN IN THE CASE.</p>
        <p>After nine years of unhappy married life, due, it was alleged, to her husband's drinking habits, late hours and his cruelty towards her, Mrs Thomas Gregory, formerly of 6, Earl-street, but now of 95, Winwick-roaa, obtained a separation order with an allowance of 12s. per week from her husband, at the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday, Mr J Rymer Young and other magistrates adjudicating. During the hearing of the I case letters were read from another woman to the defendant in which appointments were made. Thomas Gregory, insurance agent, 6, Earl-street was summoi ed for persistent cruelty to his wife, Ellen Gregory, now of 95. Winwick-road. Mr T S Steel appeared for the complainant and said the parties ha been married nine years, and there had been ten children but only one was living. The woman had been com- pelled to leave her husband on account of his persistent cruelty. The defendant was an agent for the Pearl Assurance Co, and earned 32s per week. Ever since they had been married there had been unhappiness on account of the defendant keeping late hours, drinking, and thrashing his wife. When they were living in Birchall-street, the wife, who was expecting confinement, had just retired for the night when Gregory brought a man upstairs to her room. Defendant said the man had come to stay the night and the wife had to get up and dress, with the stranger in the room, and go downstairs. The defend- ant was drunk three or four times per week and had thrashed his wife several times. He was also a man who used the most filthy language. He also seemed to be one who was fond of other women. On Christmas Day he received a Christmas card and Written across the card was " M. to Tom with fondest love." When the wife asked who the card was from Gregory knocked her off a stool with his fist. A week last Saturday a letter was delivered at the house. The wife opened it and read: "Dear Tom, Quite disappointed at not seeing you last Saturday. I went to look for you several times, but meet me at Wil P—street aboot 6io, Monday, when you can expect a letting off. Love, M." l'he wife went with her brother to Wilson Patten-street, where she waited for some time. The defendant was in a hurry to get out from tea, and did not say where he was going. The wife saw him waiting about Wilson Patten-street for two hours. On his return she asked him where he had been, and he said that he tried to get into the Royal Court Theatre, but could not, and so he had a walk round. rhe wife told him what she had seen,hut he denied hic movemets.</p>
        <p>On the Sunday morning the following letter came to the house: "Warrington, Saturday. Dear Tom,— Just n line or two to let you know I am at Stockton Heath, but wait until I come to-night. I may be early or I may be late, but will be sure to come,if only for a few minutes. Fondest love, M. x x x Liv--rd. where arranged." On Sunday Gregory went home drunk to dinner. The wife told him about the letter,for which he asked. She declined to give it up, and be then fastened all the doors of the house, put the keys in his pocket and banged his wife about, knocking her against the door. She shouted " murder," and to stop him from further assaulting her she said that sbe had given the le•ter to Mr Firth, the police court missionary. Neighbours, hearing cries, knocked at the door, which the defendant refused to open, but eventually a neighbour burst open the door. The wife then meat to live with friends. As she was going along Wilson-Patten-street she determined to look round Palmyra-square to see if she could find any woman waiting about. As she was passing the Post Office she saw a woman addressing an envelope on the wall. Noticing that the address was " Mr Thos. Gregory, 6, Earl-street," Mrs Gregory spoke to the woman and asked her if she was the woman who was intending to meet her husband. She asked her "Will you COMP with me to the address you've put on that envelope ?" The woman used a bit of bluff and said she would accompany her. As they were walking along the street tbe woman said " The address I put on the envelope was 6, Earlestnwn." Mrs Gregory said she would find a policeman, but when they got into Sankey-street the woman made off in the direction of Liverpool-road. The letter which the wife saw being posted to the husband arrived the next morning and read : " Warrington. Dear Tom,—l will try to ba at home about 12.30 dinner Tuesday. If not I will see you at the Mulberry Tree 6.30 at night. Love, M." It was quite evident Mrs Gregory could not live with the man. She had been compelled to leave him on account of his cruelty and if he had another woman in tow naturally he would not be very kind to his wife at home. Gregory was evidently making arrangements to leave Warrington because his wife intercepted a postcard which was about another post. The defendant had suggested to his wife that she should take her things away and go and he would send her half his wages. Mr Steel's statement was supported by the wife in the witness box, and evidence was also given by two neighbours. The defendant denied having assaulted his wife. He said she screamed for nothing on Sunday, but they got all right up to Wednesday, when the wife stripped the house. His wages were only 24s per week. The Magistrates' Clerk : What have jou got to say abotit these letters ?</p>
        <p>....... . Defendant : They know more about the letters than I do.</p>
        <p>A separation order with an allowance of 12s per week was granted the wife. SEQUEL TO A DOG FIGHT.</p>
        <p>SNIART FINE FOR INCITING THE ANI,kIALS.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Wednesday, before the Mayor (in the chair), and other magktrates, Harold Edgar Hawksworth, book-keeper, 9, Canal View, Latch- ford, was summoned for inciting two dogs. to fight, thus causing them suffering on the 16th February. He was further summoned for using obscene language. Defendant pleaded guilty. Mr A Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the R.S.P.C.A., said the offence which the defendant had pleaded guilty to was a very serious one. He not only incited the dogs to fight but prevented other persons from stopping the fight. At about 10 p.m. on the 16th February a Mr Challiner was coming up Wash-lane with a man named German, when they met the defendant's wife. They stopped talking to her. Mr eh Miner h.d his dog with him and whilst they were talking, the defendant came up with two dogs, an Airedale and a Welsh terrier. He saw Mr Challiner's dog and said "I have had my knife in that thing (indicating Mr Cballiner's dog) for some considerable time. Corre here Towser ! I'll make you worry it." He incited the dogs while Mr Challiner did everything he could to stop the fight, hut the defendant threatened him and stood over the dogs with a stick. Mr Arnold Nield came up and asked the defendant what he we: doing to let the dogs fight. Defendant said " What has it got to do with you? It is not your business," at the same time making use of ,bicene expressions. Ile threatened to hit Mr Nield. The dogs stopped fighting but the defendant came back, set the dogs on again and when Mr German tried to stnp them the defendant struck him a violent blow. He would not allow any- one to separate the animals. Two meu named Burns and Cook came up imd, each getting bold or a with great difficulty they separated them Unfortun- ately for the defendant, althoogn he said he Lad it, in for Mr Chfilliner's doe, his (the deferidant'sl eog was badly beaten and must have saffe,ed very great nth'. The dogs would never h.se f lilght if the defendant, had not urged them on and wee-need peoule by !Nears of threats and standing over the t.ogs with a stir,. It was a gross case of cruelty. The whole of Wash-lane was in an uproar.</p>
        <p>Defendant said he did not incite the\dogs in the first place, he did the second time. The Clerk : You have pleaded guilty. Mr Browne's statement was supported by evidence and in fining the defendant £5 including costs, with an alternative of a months' imprisonment, the chairman said it was a groes case of cruelty and the penalty was a comparatively light one.—not a quarter of what it might be.</p>
        <p>_ 11n the 0-I _ OU RTS ,s MA' 'ER.</p>
      </div>
