      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WE PUT NO DROPS OR DRUGS IN THE EYES</head>
        <p>WE PUT NO DROPS OR DRUGS IN THE EYES</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr R T Fairclough (in the chair), Councillor Locker, Messrs A J Joll3y and J Upson. CHIEF CONSTABLE'S PLEA FOR CoNVlCT.—Robert Crittersll, of Rochdale, was charged with being a con- vict on licence on January 4th, 1912, and failing to notify his intention of leaving the County Borough. Chief Inspector Williamson said the prisoner was dis- charged in the Plymouth Police District, on licence, after serving five years penal servitude, on or about December 23rd, 1911. He left there for Warrington where he reported himself but disappeared again on January 4th. Nothing was heard of him until a few days ago, when he was found at Rochdale.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner had a bad record, com- mencing in 1886, and the last sentence was the one of five years. He, however, asked the bench to deal as leniently as possible with the prisoner, as during the time he had been at Rochdale he had not been known to do anything wrong. The bench said be would be sent to goal for three months,with hard labonr,instead of going back to complete his term of penal servitude. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Aldermen Smethurst, Holton and James Kvans, Messrs W Roberts, S Wright, G PilliOg, W Furness and T Looker. NOT THANKFUL FOR SMALL MERCIES —William Hook, a seaman, of no flied abode, was charged with having been drunk in Winwick-street. He asked an, officer to lock him up as he wai destitute.—The magis- trates said they would deal leniently with the prisoner. If he would promise to leave the town the case would be dismissed.—Prisoner said he would leave the town, adding" I am destitute and I should be pleased if you could give me something to help me on my way to Manchester."—The Chief Constable: I suggest that the best way for you to help him on his way is to fine him, with the option of seven days' imprisonment. He will get to Manchester then and get some good food and be ready for the fray when he comes out.—Prisoner was fined ss. or in default seven days imprisonment.— The Clerk : You will get to Manchester now. RUNNING AWAY.—Edward Barker, 6, Carlyle-street, was charged with running away on the 22nd January and leaving his child chargeable to the Union.—Mr Basil Shearer stated that the man left home without saying anything. The Guardians had spent is. on the child. Prisoner had given them trouble for the past fem years. —Prisoner said his wife and he had some bother. She kept telling him to go and accordingly he went.— The wife said prisoner would not work. When he had paid" I will leave you" she had told him to go.—The Chief Constable : He owes us a for a child he has in an industrial school. The police had given him many more chances than he was entitled to.—On prisoner promising to remain in the town and make an endeavour to pay, the case was adjourned for five weeks.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Councillor Henry Roberts (in tbe chair), Colonel Edelsten' Messrs J Rymer Young, T , Locker and C Jackson Holmes. _</p>
        <p>CRUEL ABANDONMENT OF -A CHlLD.—Elizabeth Hughes, of no settled ebode was charged with having abandoned her child David, during the last six months thus causing him unnecessary suffering.—Mr A Browne, who appeared on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. said that the woman formerly lived with a man named David Hughes They were in and out of the Workhouse a great number of times, and ultimately the man left the woman and her child. He was arrested on a warrant, but the case had to be dismissed because the woman admitted that she was not married. On September 16th the prisoner left the Workhouse with her child and went to a lodging-house in Stanley-street saying that she was sick and tired of the Workhouse. The next morning she ' asked some women to look after the child while she ' went in search of work. She did not return for three days, and when asked where she bad been gave an unsatisfactory reply. On September 19th she went off again. Mrs McCabe, of the lodging-house, looked after the child, but was ultimately compelled to call in Inspector Wainwright, who removed the child to the Workhouse.—Nothing more was seen of the prisoner until she was apprehended in the tramp ward of the Workhouse.—The prisoner said she was tired of the Workhouse, but she left the child because she thought it would be better than taking it about the country with her.—The Clerk : Why did yon take it in a lodging-house instead of the Workhouse ?—Prisoner made no reply.—The Chairman : You will have to go to gaol for a month. We are only sorry we cannot deal with the man.'</p>
        <p>YOUNG GIRL, ASSAULTED.—WaIter Barker, 28, War- burton-street was charged with having assaulted a twelve-year-old girl named Maddock whilst in a picture palace the previous afternoon.—He pleaded guilty.— After describing the details of the case the Chief Con- stable said the prisoner was certainiy weak and did not appear responsible for his actions.—Prisoner was remanded in order that the state of his mind might be inquired into. WANTED A DOCTOR.—Patrick or John Fleming was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street and pleaded guilty. He said he bad</p>
        <p>only bad a drop of drink and was going fore doctor as he was bleeding, He was told to go to the Infirmary but they took him to the police station. The Chief Constable: The doctor would ray anything to get rid of him. They think they ought to have a doctor on their door step now, your worships. -A fine of 13s. 6d. was imposed with the alternative of 14 days imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alder- man Smethurst, Messrs W T Williams, C Jackson Holmes, and J W Hayward. TROUBLESOME FOR YEARS.—Patrick Ward, of no fixed abode, was charged with running away on the 17th February and leaving his four children chargeable to the Union.—Mr Shearer stated that the prisoner had deserted hia family, and the children had had to be admitted into the Workhoese. He had worked for a Mr Owen, but left his employment and went to the Union Offices the previous day and reported himself. Since last August the children had been chargeable 12 times. The man would not look after his family, and had been most troublesome for years. The children were continually in a neglected state —Prisoner, against whom there were 25 previous convictions, stated that his wife was always "nagging at him." He was sent to prison for three months with hard labour. AN UNFOUNDED CHARGE.—James White, labourer, 27, Gas-street and James Wood, labourer, Bank-street, were charged with stealing on or about the 25th February, three shirts and one singlet the property of some person unknown.--Chief Inapector Williamson stated that be was in the police clihe when the first prisoner was brought in on suspicion of stealing the goods. He said be had bought theta from a man in the Crown and Sceptre public house for is. Shortly afterwards the second prisoner was brought in and said he had sold the goods to the other prisoner for twopence. He afterwards said he had sold them for a pint of beer. The men were confronted and told varying stories of how they came by the goods.—Tbe Chief Constable said in that case he had no owner for the property but since the court had been sitting they had heard of a clothes line robbery the previous day. They could not say as yet whether the goods which were the subject of the present charge were the ones stolen or not, and it was necessary that the case should be adjourned.— One of the prisoners stated that be had got the goods for whitewashing some outhouses in Percy-street .—The Chief Constable : We have inquired in Percy-street but we were unable to find anyone who had given the goods.—The prisoners were remanded. --•</p>
        <p>-------- -The prisoners appeared before tEe presiding magis- tra•es, Mr S Wright (in the chair), Aldermen Pierpoint and James Evans, Messrs H Thornton, A J Jolley, W Furness and T Locker, at the Police Court on Friday. The Chief Constable repeated his previous statement and said since the adjournment they had been able to find out that two shirts were given to one of the prisoners for whitewashing which he had done. They could not find an owner for the other goods. They could naturally assume, however, from the tact that two of the shirts had been given to the prisoners that the ~ther two were given them as a reward for their labour. They had only themselves to thank for having been Kept in the cells curing the adjournment on account of the varying statements they made at the previous hearing. There was a second charge of pedling with out a licence hut he would'offer no evidence in that case.—The prisoners were then dismissed.</p>
        <p>TIIIIRSDAY.—BAfore Mr .1 Rymer Young (in the chair), Dr Naden, Messrs %V Roberta, A J Jolley and W T</p>
        <p>WOMEN IN STREET FIGHT.—A sequel to what the Chief Constable described as" a general melee " was heard when Jane Patten, Mary Ann Leonard, Ma,tha Burns, and Catherine Stretford, all of Cockhedge. appeared in answer to summonses for fighting. The Chief Constable said some fourteen days ago one person was summoned arid it transpired that there was prmotic- ally a tree fight. The care that Turning was the sequel to it. The women were all fighting with each other. Witnesses stated that the women were all " r.win g " together. The Ch;of Constable: It was t. general melee. The Chiirrutin : It seems so. Defendants were arguing with each other in the. box when the Chief Constable, turning to the Bench, said : If you tolerate too much of this you will have them starting in Court. A witness stated th.t the women were pulling each others' hair. She heard a lot of people shouting "Go on, Mrs Burns. Give it to her." She did not see Stretford or Leonard. Defendants were fined 53 each, including costs. The Chief Constable:, There is no rsason why they should not go the Stadium if they want to settle their differences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WOMEN WORKERS. What the Vote would do.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WORKERS. What the Vote would do.</p>
        <p>Many people would be in favour of women's suffrage if they really thought it would help women to improve the conditions of their work. If they would look at the history of the franchise for working men, they would find their answer. Men have steadily used their votes to get better condi- tions as workers. Instances of their success are many, among the most notable being the legislation of Trades Unions, the Truck Act, the various laws regulating work in factories, the appointment of factory inspectors and many others. All these have been passed in the interest of the workers, and some of them have helped women as well as men. But where women have themselves the right to vote, they have taken further steps to protect themselves. In our country the most sweated workers are women. In New Zealand and Australia sweating has been abolished. The women of New Zealand were enfranchised in 1893, in 1894 a law was passed which, gradually amended and improved, has resulted in the disappearance of sweating, though before 1893 there were cases of it just as bad as there are here. In Australia the beginnings of reform were made before women had the vote, but the thoroughness and completeness of the work has followed, and not preceded women's enfranchisement. In two of the six Suffrage States of Ameria, there is an Eight Hours day for women, and in one there is a Nine Hours day. In none of the States of America where women are disfranchised is there an Eight- Hours law, and in very few is there any restriction at all. The person who wears the shoe knows best where it pinches, and women want to be able to give their own opinion about laws regulating their work. Even Anti-Suffragists have to admit (" Anti-Suffrage Handbook," page 42.) "Women are limited to 45 hours a week in New Zealand, except in woollen mills, when 48 hours is allowed; and in Australia 48 hours a week. In this country the limitation of hours for women is—" In textile factories, 561/2 hours net.; in non- textile factories, 60 hours net." It is a pity that this handbook is only circulated privately; the Anti-Suffragists would no doubt be afraid to scatter this statement broadcast as a leaflet.</p>
        <p>E. L. B. WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE-NATIONAL UNION'S POLTOY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies, which is being held in London this week, is of unusual interest. Never has the advance of the constitutional movement been so rapid or so marked as during the past year. The membership of the Union now numbers 42,000. It is stated that the membership has increased, since the last annual report was issued, at the rate of 1,000 a month, and over :t43,000 have been handled during the year. Shortly after the withdrawal of the Franchise Bill in January, the executive of the National Union declared its opinion that the offer of facilities for a private Ecember's bill next session could not be regarded as an equivalent ,of the opportunity promised by the Prime Minister of proceeding by means of an amendment to the Government Franchise Bill, and that nothing could now fully redeem his pledges except the introduction of a government measure including women. The future policy of the National Union will be discussed by the Council on Thursday and Friday. Delegates from all parts of the country numbering some 600 will be pre- sent.</p>
        <p>In view of the importance attaching to the decisions of the Council, the National Union has decided to hold a public meeting at the Kensington Town Hall on March 7th to announce its policy. The chair will be taken by Mrs Henry Fawcett, and the speakers will include the Earl of Lytton, Mrs F T Swanwick, Mr Philip Snowden M.P. and Miss Royden.</p>
      </div>
