      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>CLOSED STATIONS. °n M'edaY, under circumstances explained in this paper last week, the London and North-Western and Great Western Railway Companies commenced their experiment with automatic ticket machines in respect et admittance to station platforms. I shauld not be eOrprised if in the course of a little time the Lanca- shire and Yorkshire Company follow the example. At nPrrae;sneat' 60 far as I know, they are content with the that—arY closed station arrangement. That means trLav-eliale°rll': o a may "' Pass the barrier without either a o ,nicial platform ticket obtained from the Banding there. Solar as my observation goes, w.u°Wever, platform tickets are issued indiscriminately ; r etat I naean is that I have never known of a ease of izenel• Obviously the subtitution of the automatic a 'fume would be of great advantage to the Company their servants. The would-be station visitor who :required first to put a penny in the slot may well :Presumed to have legitimate business there ; he fik aYs his money and is passed by the inspector just e th_ any cut judgment ticket bearer ; the official is relieved of . ."o tax on his udgment (supposing he submitted to ito i hich, as already intimated: I doubt) and of all ot er trouble and responsibility n connection with the alteration; and the company dis:inctly gains in revenue. A. 15- OSINIAN'S CASE. 'lle presentation to a superannuated postman referred to in a local paragraph last week reminded Lae of one who had serious troubles well-nigh on the Ivoe et his retirement on pension after not far short of Years' service. The unfortunate fellow was saa etr-edeeoly overtaken by illness while walking along the t• He happened a few minutes before to have a glass of beer, and those who judged by their of smell concluded that he was drunk. That ;deems to ha ve been taken for granted, and, without htle investigation the poor fellow was discharged. He ad for 80 much evidence in his favour that he could not a moment think of allowing the matter to end and he easily found friends ready to testify, not O'Y to his sobriety and general good conduct, but to absolute innocence of the specific charge upon teh the authorities linl so aarshly acted and the tsenti' the lee.s-a of the illness of which he had been red Victim. As they were unable to obtain ress through the ordinary channels they 1~ the facts before the local MP. and -fltlested his intervention, He was a supporter of the Zernment in power, but he declined to make repre O 1 - ';';adwis in official quarters, avowing his con- -8,1-fliten that nothing could be (lane. The humble ere's polr it. friends, who were mostly of the same teal Complexion as their Member, turned in dis- prr et from the latter and made their petition to a 15tttrnentary representative on the other side. The pr took up the case earnestly, and worked at it so ioenraPtlY and effectively that the injured postman's e; °cenoe was completely established and he was te,-astated without stain on his character. `dIeERNING LAWYERS. Birrell's story of the lawyer and the gun r-rclincled me of two or three like unto it. Au advo- cate for a isoner accused of highway robbery was Cilircalted by the awkward fact that the thief had left 8 hat behind, and this, found near the scene of the l'iroe produced in Court, was well calculated to iceritoinate the accused. his counsel devoted his engeneitY and energies to this awkward piece of vidc, th ene e and finally succeeded in disposing of it,with a . result that the pi isoner was found not guilty. On "mg told he was t liberty, the man leaned over the dock 8 a and said to his lawyer in an audible whisper, ".Please may I have my ? " There are somewhat similar tit-bits with reference to a pair of trousers and a horse. Indicted for stealing a hcrse, and discharged as the result of his counsel's efforts, a grateful fellow Proposed to reward his lawyer by presenting him with the horse in question. On the other hand, one who Ile conviction was asked if he wished to say anything iiekrore sentence was passed upon him, said he would 1,4 a few minutes in private with the young lawyer had been appointed to defend him. Another, n tue like question responded—" Why, yes ; I'd like to 4.BkaY good evening ' and go home if it's agreeable o.re Present company." 1-11,Elt EXAMPLES. fr- navs a fairly good collection of lawyers' stories ro which I take two or three more. Mr Justice Jell, 'en Recorder of Shrewsbury, was once trying a case ti Which the prismer wterly broke down. " Why do pu weep, ~ asked the Recorder. " Oh, my lord, my ar .usr(l," replied the prisoner, " I have never been auTrison before." " Don't cry, prisoner at the bar, I t gelog to send you there now." "You cannot ..eaeh i rr .mo manners," said a judge who had an icating habit of interrupting those addressing him. hot °'. ray lord," readily acquiesced counsel, Big It wasn't s ) much what he said as the aeirtcant tone in which he said it. Another laodgette rudely told counsel that what the ht thr Was saying to him went in at one ear and out tete ?her. " To be sure my lord," was the quick saatirt, what is there to prevent it ?" Judge Wight- had before him a witness who, describin g a ,sri4oo iede connected with the case, referred to him as" an thslj'endent gent." " An independent what?, ' asked atickudge sharply. "A gent, my lord." "Oh, I 81?. i,tland," remarked Judge Wightman, "that's chet, tunig short of a gentleman, isn't it? " A Cet,P2inted litigant once threw an egg at a County Sa.,,t;elge. His Honour dodging the missile, slid, The'blY that was intended for my brother Bee n." lahr, reference was to a famous County Court Judge ara,:ct very long ago died at the age of 75, and lig„whose sayings the following may be mentioned : they d "Preters are persons who translate a language riot understand into one they know less"; 4 r-"m will out, even in the witness box "; " When the is in debt and pressure comes, wiOdo feelings go away through the toeeta Sometimes the clever cross-examiner vvell-ic 19 Match. Serjeant Cockle, who in his day was that I,"wn on the Northern Ciicuit, telling a witness sauce" was very saucy, added the query, " Pray what 6aues,,do you like best ? " "Any sauce but cockle /v„,as the Instant retort. The late Serjeant a dro-- " : avy in early life had carried on business as he Ny„gglst and had become a bankrupt. At an assize tic), "a cross-examining an old lady as to her recollec- • sslda Certain. event. "And pray, my good woman," how is it that you should be so particular day r„emenaber that this affair happened on a market that an " Why, sir," was the reply, "by this token— (lrupti,4_t the cry of the city went that Mr Davy, the had that morning shut up shop and run • ay,a Too , wh-„ "EauLous. catraat " Uncle Caleb" had to tell the children about a ea;,eget, fishes " brought to my mind an old story of a5l. Or lad who, home from his first voyage, was woade Y his mother to narrate something of the a we re he had met when at sea. " he began Na,s,r, lots and lots of flying fish, and" interrupted, "Don't impose on yoar poor • with such inventions. Give me actual experi- belie sober matter of fact." "So you don't Jo that about the flying fish? All right I'll ingLllen toB )mething else. When we were cross- the Red. Sea we had occasion to anchor, and when if elghed again we found it unusually hard work,as 'otnethin aPpear 14 Was dragging at it. When the anchor (that 8,1 wive the surface of the water we found kh t had hooked one of Pharaoh's chariot wheels !" his mother, "that I can believe, We orveeardw i in about Pharaoh and his n tho lied Sea.', A'ITIER FISH YARN atso, the climbing perch of which we read on page Week's issue, Carter Platte tells of an angler</p>
        <p>out in India, who need to set night lines in the Ganges fastening them to pegs in the high bank overhanging the river. Morning after morning, when he went to look at his lines, he found them gone. It was not that big fish had taken the bait and broken the lines, for no broken portim s were left attached to the pegs —the lines were gone entirely. His servant could supply no satisfactory explanation, and so the angler determined to watch one moonlight night in the hope of solving the mystery, and when he saw how the thing had been done he simply kicked himself for not having thought of such a palpable explanation before. Ther Ganges swarmed with climbing perch at that point, and one after the other these fish would grab the bait, and, finding themselves hooked Fe yond the power of release by tugging and jerking, would quickly swarm up the line, fin over fin, and untie the end of the string from the peg with their teeth, after which they would drop into the water, and swim away with the line, to digest it at their leisure. STEALING BY FINDING. There was a charge of this character at the Borough Police Court last week, but it was not established. Such are rarely brought, in fact—for reasons which must be fairly obvious. Only one of the kind has come my way, and in that instance conviction followed It was a case in which a woman taking a seat on a railway station platform found thereon a bag which a former occupant had absent-miodedly left behind Without saying a word to anyone on the subject, the newcomer quietly appropriated the bag. On a report and description of the missing property being given to the officials, one of them remembered having seen such a hag in the pos,--,ession of the accused It was subsequently discovered at her house, therefore she was charged with stealing it and was convicted. THE DEFORESTATION OF LYMM. Last week's news paragraph under this heading was painful reading. Such things ought not to be possible nowadays -only under proper regulations, which would ensure that the mischief should not be apparent or have permanent effects. Some time ago I read how the Board of Agrictilture had published an account of forestry law in witzerlind,froin which it appears that in most cantons great care is taken of trees. The forest area of a canton, whether public or private, cannot bs decreased rsithout permission of the authorities, and clearings of all kinds have to be replanted within three years. Complete clearings ma' only be made with the consent of the Deparment of Agriculture Periods of cuttings and removal of wood are also defined. Most countries take better care of their trees than we in England do. We shall realise our mistake one of these days when it is too late. Oh, for a national afforestation scheme! It would be worth a great deal more to the country than riny of the things we make a tremendous fuss about.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WOMEN GO ON STRIKE.</head>
        <p>WOMEN GO ON STRIKE.</p>
        <p>The conditions under which so many women are forced to labour call for attention. Bc tween girlhood and middle age there are ailments well understood by women which render them totally unfit for work Pains of frightful intensity occur in the back; there is a dull, weary ache at one spot in each side. Head- ache and a vague, awful feeling of helplessness add to their torment. Every woman should " strike" against such oppression. Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkliam's Vegetable Compound, a woman's medicine for women's ailments, which acts in harmony with Nature, restoring and pre- serving health, has brought quick relief to thou- sands of pain-racked women. A woman who toiled long hours in one of the great factories of Lancashire suffered frightfully at one time; but elle writes : "I always had a pain in the groin. I did not know what was the matter with me. I consulted various doctors, and took various medicines, but they did me no good. One night, when I was very bad,my husband brought me a bottle of Mrs Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable C.tmpound from the chemist's. After I bad taken three dcses I felt an improvement. I feel all right now." The Lady Manager, The Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead-road. London, NW., will gladly send free a private book to women only. She will send direct,or any chemist will supply the remedy at 2:9 a bottle, or 4/6 for a very largo bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>It is surprising what a hold the Titanic disaster has upon public imagination to-day, nearly a year after the event. People of all apes have flocked to the Palace and Hippodrome this week to see the great spectacular production which illustrates in most vivid fashion the disaster amid the North Atlantic icebergs which sent the world's largest steamship to the bottom of the sea, with the bulk of her passengers and crew. The production has nothing of the cinemato- graph character about it, but is presented by Poole's of myrierotna fame., with all the skill and stagecraft for which they are justly famed. There are no les- than eight tableaux showing the launching of the giant steamer, her departure from Cork Harbour, at full speed in the Atlantic, and the scene of the catastrophe amid the floating ice-field and the towering bergs. The motion of the ship on the water, her ultimate sinking "by the head," while ablaze with lights, are wonderful examples of what can be done in the way of conveying realism to the stage. The last scenes are awe-inspiring, and the whole production one of great merit as a show pure and simple. A description of the disaster is related in stentorian voice and moving tones, the while the panorama passes before our eyes—but this is a doubtful advantage ! There are many other items in I the evening's programme, trostly in lighter vein. The Arthur Ciallirnore Trio maintain fun of the fast and furious variety in their comedy sketch. Every moment is full of movement and every movement provokes laughter. A fair display of juggling in the Japanese style is given by the Royal Alatsuis, whose tricks are more skilful than original. Pedro Pascador scores well with his dog, which has been admirably trained in equilibristic and somersaulting specialities A capital athletic navelty is given by the Pa.olos, eho demonstrate that there is still scope for originality among the muscle metchants; Payolo himself is a remarkable example of physical development in a small man. Evelyn Taylor sings a couple of chorus songs, which take well among the " gods," while Woodhouse and Wells keep the ball of humour ever rolling with their patter and eccentric dancing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HANDS AND FACE MASS OF SPOTS.</head>
        <p>HANDS AND FACE MASS OF SPOTS.</p>
        <p>11, Station Rd, Portslade-by-Sea, Eng.—" For eight years or more I was troubled with eczema. back, hands and face came out in a mass of small red spots full of water, which was very painful and sore I could not be dressed s-metitnes for days together. I tried all sorts of soap, also had an ointment made up, but none did me any good. I was about the same, very wretched and miserable. All' spent was in vain until I bought a box of Cuticura Ointment and a cake of Cuticura Soap. After I had used them I got much relief, and found the raw places getting soothed, healed and dried, and the inflammation gdng a lot S) I kept on with both Cuticura Soap and Ointment, and soon got completely well. I am clear as a bell to-day. "My sister has been a dreadful sufferer. I have seen her raw on body, hands and face. So I sent her a box of Cuticura Ointment and Cuticura Soap. Now she would not be without Cuticura Soap and Cuticura Ointment as they have completely cured her." (Signed) Mrs Rosanna Parfrey, March 7th, 1912. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free from nearest depot: Newbery, 27, Charterhouse Sq. London ; 11. Towns A: Co., Sydney, N.S.W. ; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay ; "Cuticura," Boston, U S.A. Tender- faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>CO-PARTNERSHIP AND PROFIT SHARING</head>
        <p>CO-PARTNERSHIP AND PROFIT SHARING</p>
        <p>IN BRITISH GAS COMPANIES. [By C. E. Parker.[</p>
        <p>Particulars are before us of the growth of the principle of co-partnership in gas producing. The figures relate to conditions as on December 31st, 1911. They show. that 35 towns have adopted the principle. The South Metropolitan was the pioneer, and the inauguration and working of the scheme was due largely to the late Sir George Livesey, who started it 22 years ago. Over 5,000 men are employed by this company and the amount divided amongst these in 1911 was £43,000; representing 81/4 per cent on the wages earned. The amount distributed since the adoption of the scheme is £548,706. In addition to the percentage on the wages the amount of shares and deposits held by the employees in the company is £423,721. Taking the whole of the 35 Gas Companies that have adopted the principle there are 22,263 employees and the amount distributed in 1911 was ce103,390 ; representing an average of about 51% per cent. on the wages earned. The rapid extension of the co-partnership principle in gas undertakings as contrasted with the slow progress, and many failures, in other industries is probably due to the fact that the selling price of gas is steady and not sub- ject, as are the products of many other industries, to constant fluctuations. This has always been a serious difficulty to the intro- duction of profit sharing in ordinary under- takings. Workers get by the arrangement, say in one year, a very respectable bonus from profits, but in the following year the selling price of the article produced may have gone down thereby materially affecting the profits. The worker may contend that he has put in just as much work as in the previous year, result—disappointment with the system. It is difficult to see how other- wise profit sharing can be regulated if it is to be profit sharing. The amount divided must depend upon the profits earned, and if it is a yearly division it must be on the year's profits whatever they are, and until workmen take the long view, and are prepared for variations, profit sharing will not make rapid progress. Perhaps the adoption of the principal of a three years average as followed in the collection of income tax from indus- trial concerns might be considered as a means of equalizing profit variations i.e. the bonus would be r. aid on the same figure as assessed for income tax. One thing must be clear : the share of profits should not involve any diminution in the regular wage, the men must al ways receive the standard wage in any industry in which the principle is applied. The amount of profit received must be entirely independent of, and extra to, the remuneration for labour at standard rates. Another serious drawback to co-partnership is that it involves publicity in regard to profits. Firms doing exceedingly well are not anxious to let their competitors know what they are earning, and firms doing badly are just as averse to making their results public. There are,however, a very consider- able number of industries, probably the major part, in which the results of the year's working are at present public property ; this objection therefore only holds good in the smaller concerns. Railway Companies would afford a likely sphere for the operation of the principle, their selling price for services rendered being fixed and their accounts published. Many Trade Unionists are averse to this principle of profit sharing and believe that their policy, the sole reason of their existence, is to fight capital, not to co-operate with it. It should, however, be recognised that a fighting policy is any sphere is wasteful, that the remunera- tion of labour can only come from the successful results of the undertaking, and that industrial conflict diminishes trade. In the matter of international conflicts it has been recognised that war injures more than benefits both victor and vadquished, so in industrial strife both workers and capitalists permanently suffer whatever the immediate result of the strike may be. The gains seldom 'balance the losses of the indus- trial strife. Co-partnership does not involve the abandonment of trades union activity. The standard wage, the main point with . which trade unions are concerned, is left unaffected by the adoption of co-partnership. If it does affeet it the form of co-partnership is a bastard type. We should like to know if in the gas produc- ing industi y co-partnership has prejudiciously affected the standard wage. It may be con- tended that the profit-sharing principle tends to alter the relation batween employees and masters by making them more friendly, but is this a loss to the workers? Is it not the fact that those trades in which the workers and masters have co-operated by various systems of regulating wages have benefited from the workers' standpoint more than those in which there have been no such arrangements and in which there have been frequent appeals to the strike weapon as the only means of improving wages? A further objection to any form of co-partnership based on profit-sharing is the doubt that arises in the mind of Imany workmen that any plan emanating from the masters' side is designed to benefit the masters, to get from the work- men an increased exertion out of all propor- tion to the possible profit coming back to the individual man. I do not think this suspicion can be justified by facts, and consider it is a mistake due to want of thought. The fault in our industrial system is not that the men as a rule give forth too little muscular effort, but it is rather that their interest and heart are often not in their work, directing their effort and making it effective. I have in a previous article dealt with this question, and likened the industrial system to a bicycle full of grit in the driving chain, and each man may be likened to a link. In many cases the man is indifferent to the progress of the machine and thinks little of how the grit in his own link retards the progress. In the wage system there is little direct or proportionate relation between the individual worker and the total outcome. The worker having given forth a certain amount of effort, a considerable pro- portion of which may be spent in overcoming the effect of the grit in his own link, con- siders he has done his part, done all he ought, and wonders why his share of the total result is not greater, forgetting that so large a proportion of his effort produces no result, is wasted in friction His business, he considers, is not to think about grit ; he accepts without question the idea that in the division of labour he has to do his share in the driving, and the thought which would bring about the removal of the grit belongs to the boss or his deputy. Now suppose that by having his thoughts brought to bear upon the total result out of which his share must come, he applies his mind to , making his own part work more smoothly and economically, it does not follow that he is called upon to give forth any extra exer- tion, indeed the very opposite will probably be the result of his thought. The fact is workers are partners in the concern, whether</p>
        <p>p i,e/N../\,-"\x,",eVv‘ew, \•‘,.e\,..‘" iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB. •,-r‘".•.e..,Ne-'xiwNe-,‘"%e-,‘,.v</p>
        <p>THE ANATOMY OF AN OYSTER.</p>
        <p>I never see people eating oysters without wondering whether they know what they are swallowing and what a lot there is under the slippery exterior. Let me give my readers a little lecture on the parts of an oyster's curious body. Every oyster has a mouth, a heart, a liver, a stomach, and other necessary internal organs, including a set of cunningly devised intestines. The mouth is at the small end of the oyster's body, near the hinge of the shell. It is oval ill shape, and, though not readily discovered by an unprar..,ticed eye, it may be easily located by gently pushing a blunt bodkin or similar instrument along the folds of the surface of the body at the place mentioned. Connected with the mouth is the canal which the oyster uses in conveying food to the stomach, from whence it passes into the curious little set of netted and twisted intestines.</p>
        <p>To discover the heart of an oyster the fold of flesh which oystermen call the "mantle" must be removed. When the mantle has been removed the heart, shaped like a crescent or horned moon, is laid to the view. The oyster's heart is made up of two parts, just like that of a human being, one of which receives the blood from the gills and the other drives it through the al teries. The liver is found in the immediate vicinity of the heart and stomach, and is a queer shaped little organ, which is supposed to perform all the functions of a blood filter.</p>
        <p>Perhaps my readers have read enough to discourage them from eating oysters. I don't eat them myself, and don't ever intend to do. And if I told you what a scavenger the oyster is, and what sort of food he puts into his stomach and bowels, I don't think you would want to swallow him whole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MODEL SCHOLAR.</head>
        <p>THE MODEL SCHOLAR.</p>
        <p>" That intelligent-looking boy there," said the schoolmaster to the top-hatted parent he was showing over the school premises, " is Brown. I am proud of Brown. I have inculcated in him the love of learning to such an extent that he now prefers study to play. I expect at this moment he is writing Latin prose on that sheet of paper there while all the other pupils are at play. I will ascer- tain."</p>
        <p>He called the lad to him. v," Brown," he said, " let us see the result of your industry." " I—l'd rather not, sir," blushed Brown. "Note his modesty." whispered the schoolmaster. "Come, Brown, let me see what you have been writing." Still the boy demurred. But the school- master insisted, and forcibly appropriated the paper. And there in neat imitation ot feminine handwriting ho read the following ; " Please excuse my son James from school to-day. He is wanted at home." MOTHER NATURE'S WINTER QUILTS.</p>
        <p>Wis 3 Mother Nature started out, one raw, bleak autumn day, With her plump arms filed with bedquilts she'd made to give away. She had " patched" them for her children (some were new and some were old), To keep them safe in winter from getting stiff and cold! For the little plants and rootlets, when the North wind moans and grieves, She brought to keep them cozy a heavy quilt of leaves To the squirrels, tired of playing all the 1 ,ng, warm• summer through, She gave a quilt of tree-holes--the best thing she ' could do! The little future butterflies received, each one, I'm told. A cocoon bedquilt, soft as silk, their bodies to enfold. The woodchucks Mother Nature wrapped in bedquilts made of earth ; Twas not because of other things, I'm sure, she found a dearth! The bears in bedquilt caves she rolled, to keep them warm and snug, ' Where they might lie the winter through, as tight as in a rug. For all her other children, too, she had some quilts as well. What were they and who had them, I wonder can you tell ?</p>
        <p>A. F. CALDIVELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SOAP SECRETS.</head>
        <p>SOAP SECRETS.</p>
        <p>MESSRS CROSFIELD'S IMPORTANT ACTION,</p>
        <p>An important action, involving soap trade secrets has been proceeding in the Chancery Division this week, before Mr Justice Neville. Although the action is brought by Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., Warring• ton, against the Technii-Chemical Laboratories, Ltd., and Nils Testrup, of London, it was explained by counsel that the real plaintiffs are Brunner, Mond and Co., and the real defendants Lever Bros. The plaintiffs asked for an injunction to restrain an alleged infringement of their Letters Patent 1515 of January, 1903, granted to Dr ilhelm Norntann, of Hesford, Westphalia, for a process for converting unsaturated fatty acids or their glycerines into saturated compounds for the purpose of soapmaking. The defendants denied infringement, ann claimed that the patent was invalid by reason of anticipation and prior public knowledge, and that the process was not sufficiently described in the plaintiffs' specification. The process which is the sunject of litigation was det.cribed by counsel as a master patent which had revolutionised the soap trade. On Tuesday Dr Liebmann gave further evidence as to experiments he had made on the directions given in the specification, and the successful results he had obtained. Mr Astbury, KA% (for the defendants), stated that his case was that the results could only be obtained by , the use of a secret process not disclosed in the patent. , His Lordship suggested that the best course would be to refer the point to some unprejudiced chemist to say if the instructions were such that an ordinary compe- tent practising chemist could get the results alleged. Otherwise the natant was invalid. Mr Astbury said the defendants were very anxious to have his lordship's decision on this point, end not the judgment of a minor person, whether he was a chemist or a lawyer. They preferred to have the matter decided by a judicial tribunal on legal grounds. The case was then proceeded with, and Dr Liebmann, in cross-examination, denied that the reason why the plaintiffs' process would act with some bodies and not with others was a mere chemical trick. Dr Liebmann was still under cross-examination when the Court adjourned. The whole of the week has been occupied in hearing technical evidence, Dr. Liebmann, the eminent expert, being in the witness box five days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SIR FELIX SCHUSTER ROBBED.</head>
        <p>SIR FELIX SCHUSTER ROBBED.</p>
        <p>Sir Felix Schustar, the well-known banker,with Lady Schuster, attended Westminster Police Court on Thurs- day to prosecute a young German footman, named Albert Franz, who was sentenced to three months' hard labour and deportation for stealing jewellery worth £lOO from their house in Cadogan-place, Chelsea.</p>
      </div>
