      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DRUDGE-STATE,</head>
        <p>THE DRUDGE-STATE,</p>
        <p>MR. J. DALBY'S PLEA FOR IDEALISM IN WORK.</p>
        <p>" LIGANTIC CONSPIRACY AGAINST CRAFTSMANSHIP."</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Dalby gave the last of his series of lectures on " Old English Trade Unions " at the Socialist Hall, Wilderspool Causeway, on Monday evening. His subject was " Decline and fall of the Guilds and their value tor us." Mr Dalby summed up briefly the media, vat history of the guilds, and traced their subsequent history up to the early nineteenth century when the development of machinery brought capital and labour, those forces whose opposition the Guilds could no longer hide,facc, to face,and the modern trade union was formed as a result. If we looked merely at the services rendered by the English Guilds to their own time the debt was enormous. They saved society from drifting into disorderly anarchy or being gripped by the hand of a tyrannous central control. Voluntary association had been condemned by Socialists, but con- demned only as it was practised to-day—i.e., tea a fine and beautiful thing prostituted to the base use of whitewashing the ghastly scars left everywhere in society by the working of competition. English people to-day were bold in defence of the principle of volun- tary association as applied to an indifferent rnaVer like healing the sick, but they thought a person was mad when he suggested the same principle was applicable to" a really serious matter" lilo industry. Voluntary association was looked upon as a poor, delicate rpirit which would vanish defeated,before the strong passion '1 of human greed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>FREE SPIRIT.</head>
        <p>A FREE SPIRIT.</p>
        <p>The guilds aimed to embrace all the most vital parts of the life of the mediwval worker—his religion, his trade and, to some extent his hmuseruents. He was not saying, of course, that they were completely successful, o anything like it, but it was an attempt. The worker then certainly had no notion that be could " do wha he liked" ; he was far removed from anything like the laissez faire point of view. That did not mean that we were to rush to the other extreme and to say of him, with the bureaucrat, "Yes, of course be had to obey the laws of the State." Certain people seemed quite incapable of seeing more than two possi- bilities in this matter. Either a man might do exactly what be liked, or else he must lose hit liberty and obey the rigid laws forced on him from above. There was a third way, however. Realising that in association with his fellows, he was to find his highest good. a man might cheerfully put aside his narrowly selfish projects and in the end he would not only have played his right part in the common life, but he would also have enriched himself It in thc, future we were to have anything like the old Guilds, the organization must be as free and uncrarnped as possible. A tree spirit was constantly changing and it must be free to change. For instance, were we to have—he was only putting forward a suggestion—En organiza- tion of trade guilds, then the rules binding on till those joined together in one trade must be as few as possible and must leave room for a good deal of minor guild organizations to spring up below. RENT, INTEREST AND PROFIT.</p>
        <p>In conclusion 11Ir Dalby laid down two conditions which he said were essential for the future—an outward economic cne and an inward spiritual one. The guild spirit of co-operation (he said) cannot flourish in any society which is dominated by rent, interest and profit. These three evil forces—really the manifestations of one grand error—once allowed the slightest room in society end by enthrallinr, the worker. Under such conditions, the worber may be organized in guilds, and it will avail him nothing. His labour will still be regarded as a paid comm dity—an incident in successful production, a means perhaps an important mcans, towards the great end of the production of wealth which is to fliw more magically into the hands of a select few, even as the riches of El Dorado were once conceived as a magic, inexhaustible fountain. As soon as labour is regarded as " personal " and there- fore an end in itself, not subservient but paramount in importance, then you have the Guild spirit, and although there is no time; here for me to outline the methods by which rent interest and profit are to be abolished, yet all history goes to show that it is just when these interests are dormant or only half-awakened that you get the Guild spirit. There is before us to-day an example of a great concern worked mithout regard to rent, interest and profit which foreshadows faintly what could be done by guild organisa- tions. The Panama Canal requires for its con- struction ..R.:75,000,000 capital—capital we call it,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>GRAND IDEAL.</head>
        <p>THE GRAND IDEAL.</p>
        <p>The grand ideal of William Morris is scoffed at to- day. We need not mind that. It is an honourable distinction nor can I say that the study of history shakes my own belief in that high and serious doctrine of work. I will end this course of lectures on this note, which has just been sounded again so bravely by the "New Age ":—" Kill the craftsmanship of an indus- trial country, and what remains ?" rays a writer in that journal. "Yet, to-day difficult though it be to believe, the vast majority of the manufacturers of Western Europe and America seem to be in a gigantic conspiracy to crush out that very crafts- manship that is the life-blood of their occupation. The reason is simple ; mechanical production necessi tates intense specialisation, so that to-day a man no longer learns a trade—he is put to a section of it, and there he sticks for the rest of his life. But the workers are by nature gregarious and companionable, so that by exchange of experiences the tradition of each trade is maintained—a tradition that will bloom into human reality when labour ceases to be non-human commodity and becomes as richly human as it was under the mediieval Guild. Motive ! What workman is there who would not sell his soul to become a craftsman ? "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NECESSARIES AND CONIFORTS.</head>
        <p>NECESSARIES AND CONIFORTS.</p>
        <p>When we travel we all try to leave troubles behind, but aches and pains are apt to make themselves felt wherever we go, spoiling all enjoyment. Those Home Companions Holloway's Pills and Oint- ment—should also be Travelling Companions— Remember! Holloway's Pills—the Pills which give strength—make you " Fit " and Well. Holloway's Ointment—the Ointment that soothes and heals. HOLLOWAY'S PILLS cure Constipation, Indiges- tion, Biliousness, Dizziness, and Sick-Headache, so frequently induced by change of air and other attendant conditions Holloway's Pills will be found a sure and safe remedy. HOLLOWAY'S OINTMENT cures Cuts, Bruises, Abrasions, Rheumatism, SUE' Joints, Sprains and Strains, and quickly relieves Colds, Coughs, and 'ections of the Chest and Lungs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Philip Snowden, speaking in Blackburn on Tues- day evening, alleged that too little Parliamentary time in the past had been given to the question of the con- dition of the people. Labour unrest was due to work having become harder and the struggle to live, while wages had not gone up with the advance in the price of commodities. He suggested that the State should enact that no employer should pay a wage which was insufficient to provide the necessaries awl comforts of existence. I]4 also alvocated a gradual reduction in the hours of lablur till hay were brought down to a maximum of eight per day.</p>
