        <p>Woodfall Hill, Downton, Salisbury. April 30th, 1911. Dear Sir,—Flaving used your pills for three years and reaped so much benefit from them I think It my duty to give you a testimonial. Now this last week I had rheumatic gout come in my band and then in my foot I COULD NOT SLEEP WITH THE PAIN, and the swelling. I had no pills at the time and could not walk. I sent and got a bottle and took two in the morning and by night</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>– "V- • Nt</head>
        <p>- "V- • Nt</p>
        <p>Free Colds</p>
        <p>yet there is not interest on it, for it comes direct from the U.S.A. national revenue. here are no profits either, for the building of the Canal is being undertaken for large ends and not to make a direct profit for any Company. M itch of the surrounding country was a deadly swamp, infested with mosquitos, yet this wa boldly faced in the communal spirit, a medical corpss was formed and up to now sanitation has cost no less than £3,000,000. Incidentally the workers are comparatively healthy and, though they live in a country naturally unhealthy, the death-rate from disease is only 7.72 per 1,000. Then, 65,000 people are fed and clothed without a penny of profit being made, for the army officer in charge does not attempt his task in the spirit of Sir Joseph Lyons but as one who knows that it is his business to supply a great number of people with the necessities of lifo. No doubt the enterprise is a unique one and the new spirit is easier of application there than in one of the great normal compet.itiva iniustries, but let us be quite sure that it is only the power of the vested interests of the great capitalists that prevents the same principle from being applied all-round.</p>
        <p>THE CRAFTSMAN'S LOVE OF WORK. And secondly, there is the inner principle—the spirit of craftsmsnship, which is the spirit of wort; under- taken because in it joy and life are found. The craftsman loves his work because he is creating a thing of beauty, and the creation of a beautiful thing is the highest task of man and his supreme happiness.. Even that large portion of soulless work—dull mechanical stuff—which will alwa3s remain to be done will share in this spirit of life, for it will be done by men whose real interest will be going out concurrently to work of a far higher order. Besides, dull work undertaken in a strong conviction of its value for the community, as nece.sary service, becomes thereby ennobled. Here I would insert a note of advice : Most of us are condemned to work under conditions calculatsid to turn us into drudges—unwilling or conscientious, it matters not. We cannot escape as individuals wholly from those conditions, but we can, at the same time as we are doing our utmost to abolish thew, make effective resistance in our personal daily work. Let us keep burning a lively feeling of revolt and let us also refuse, as far as in us lies, to be used as drudges. Those who are young have a chance of preventing that drudge-state,felt consciously at first, from becoming a habit. We can insist on the personal value of our own work as far trascending any mete commodity, capable of re .ard. We can refuse to do a great many tasks which are given us in our capacity of labour machines end we can make serious attempt to seize upon portions of our allotted work and commit there the noblest sabotage. Sabotage is the name given to any attempt to injure the cornmereial value of a product—a very different thing from its real value to the community, which can never be produced apart from a high personal spirit of work in the individual. There are only two kinds of real work : that, whose dullness is redeemed and glorified by the spirit of com- munal service and that which is done as the creative expression of our pursuit of the beautiful.</p>
      </div>
