      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>601611</head>
        <p>601611</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 1, 1913.</p>
        <p>NEW WYCLIFFE MINISTER.</p>
        <p>Public Recognition of the Rev. T. T. James.</p>
        <p>THE CHURCH AND THE PROBLEMS OF THE DAY.</p>
        <p>The Rev T T James, M.A., was formally welcomed to the pastorate of Wycliffe Congregational Church on Tuesday evening, when a public recognition meeting was held. The large attendance and encouraging tone of the gathering showed a determination on the part of the congregation to rally round their new minister in the great work which he has undertaken in succession to the Rev John Yonge, whose faithfnl pastorate of thirty five years will ever be gratefully remembered. One couldnot fail to be impressed with the warmth of the testimony paid to Mr James by those who have been associated with him in his labours at Sheffield, and it was evident that not only had he thrown himself whole-heartedly into the undertakings of his own church but bad exercised considerable influence on Free Church life in the city as a whole. His attachment to the workingmen in his district was the subject of special appreciative comment. The welcome of the municipality to Mr James was fittingly voiced by the Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) and the Rev W Bracecamp promised sympathetic co-operation in the name of the Church of England clergy. . _ _ .</p>
        <p>The Rev Dr W F Adeney (Principal of the Lanca- shire Independent College and Chairman of the Congregational Union of England and Wales) presided and am ng others present were the Mayor of War- rington (Councillor Dr Joseph), the Rev T T James, the Rev Alfred James, of Leicester kfather of the new pastor), the Rev Professor Armitage (principal of the Yorkshire United College, Bradford), the Revs E II Tichmarsh and J F Matthews (Sheffield), the Rev W Bracecamp (vicar of St Paul's, Warrington), Coun- cillor J Hawthorn, Messrs C E Parker (president of the Free Church Council), Edward Offlallington, W Beswick, P T Winskill, B Farrar, J Owen, C Eccles (church secretary),E Shorrock Eccles (Lancashire Con- gregational Union), FSkelton and Jefferson (Wycliffe Church, Sheffield). The meeting opened with the hymn " Faith of our fathers,' Mr W H Payton, Mus. Ban., being at the organ.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH NOT A REPUBLIC.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, in emphasising the sacred import- ance of a recognition meetlng, said they could not be too clear as to the real appointment of their minister. He was not appointed by any external human authority and, as far as human arrangements vere concerned, there was nothing outside those walls that had to be considered. Some people carried the idea of vigorous independency further and said that the church had a perfect right to choose whomsoever it would to be its minister. He recently read a book called "Christian Republics" which was supposed to expound Congregational ideas. Its title was, however, a misnomer. A Congregational church was not a Republic and if a group of people—saints they might be—simply met together and said "Nobody shall interfere with us ; we will do just just what we like" and acted accordingly, that did not make them a church,nor did it give them any authority to invite a minister. The Church was the Church of Christ and as that was so with the whole Cburch which they called the Catholic Church, so it was with the local church, the individual community. Therefore in the true appointment of a minister, the idea was that they met in prayer and conference, realizing the truth of that great saying of Jesus—which they might call the charter of the Church—that " where two or three are gathered together in My name, there am I in the midst of them." They brought their case before Christ, laid aside personal preference and prejudice, prayed for light and desired to know the will of the Master; and if a church acted in that way when they came to their decision it was not merely the vote of a democracy but the interpretation and expressicn of the will of Jesus Christ Himself. (Applause.) Dr Adeney then referred to his own intimate associa- tion with their new pastor when Mr James was warden of the settlement in connection with the Lancashire College in Manchester. He was a man thoroughly in sympathy with workingmen, able to get on with them and to win them, and at the same time he was a true minister of Jesus Christ. He had now come to be pastor of a church which had a noble record and his work was worthy to be entered into in the hope of a great and beautiful future. "I do most heartily com- mend him to your love and confidence," concluded Dr Adeney. "It you support him loyally he will respond. If you think he has to do everything, he might he the Angel Gabriel or the Archangel Michael,but you would be disappointed. But with minister and people acting together I think there is a very happy prospect of good work and fellowship in this church." (Applause), WELCOME FROM THE CHURCH. -</p>
        <p>Mr C Eccles (church secretary) then detailed the steps leading to the appointment of the new pastor. At the outset he paid a tribute to the work of the Rev John Yonge whose powerful evangelical teaching resulted in the membership ot the church being doubled during the thirty-five years of his pastorate. Acknow- ledgment was made to the church's indebtedness to the Rev A.l Viner, secretary of the Lancashire Con- gregational Union, who had acted as moderator in the interregnum between Mr Yonge'a retirement and the filling of the pastorate. They believed there was a great work for Mr James to do at Wycliffe, and on behalf of church officers, members and congegatioo, the teachers and scholars in the Sunday school and the mem- bers of the other church organizations, they °flared to Mr and Mrs James a hearty welcome into their midst, assuring them of their loyal co-operation in the high and noble work to which they were called, praying that the union might be a happy one, that health and strength might be vouchsafed to them, and that God would abundantly bless them and the people of Wycliffe as they sought to carry out His work in this portion of His vineyard. (Applause). THE NEW PASTOR'S WORK IN SHEFFIELD. Mr F SK ELTON (representing Wycliffe Church Sheffield), said Mr James had been their minister for six years and, for anything he knew, he would have been there another six years, if it had not been for the call from Warrington. Mr and Mrs James were greatly beloved and respected by young and old alike. The work which Mr James had done for their church would never be forgotten, but would always be looked upon as a bright part of its history. During his pastorate considerably over £l,OOO was spent on extension and renovation of the church, and he felt sure Mr James would never have left them while a penny of debt remained. As a preacher Mr James had excelled; wherever he had gone, they had been anxious to hear him again. A more generous, kind-hearted and humble-minded man one seldom met. The very poorest members of the congregation need not fear rubbing against him ; they would be all the better for doing so. (Applause). Mr JEFFERSON, the church secretary at Wycliffe Church, Sheffield, said they would always remember Mr James as one of the best friends that church ever had. He was an intellectual man who could not only take them up into the rarer air of spiritual things right on to the very Alps, but who would also come down into the valleys with them. In their sorrows and troubles they would find that he was indeed a mifiister and it shepherd. If they supported him loyally, he was sure the town of Warrington would be cleaner, sweeter, purer and nobler for his work. (Applause). The Rev E H Titchmarsb, on behalf of the Free Churches of Sheffield, said he doubted whether they had had any bigger reinforcement of the spiritual forces in Sheffield than they had when Mr James took up his ministry there. His church was in a working-class district away from the centre of Sheffield, and it was quite easy in a big city for a bard-working minister to be largely occupied in his own district, and make no impression on the life of the city as a whole. That was far from being the case with Mr James. He did not neglect his own church to do "star turns" elsewhere, but he attended to the duties of his pastorate and also used other opportunities in such an effective way that he made a distinct impression upon the city as a whole. They had in Mr James a man who was, in the best sense of the word, a man and not a mere official. He was a real living voice and not an echo. He would give them in the pulpit the reselt of real thinking—not I the mere, abstract thinking of the recluse and scholar, but the thinking of a man who was in touck with books on the one side—who knew how to select the vital and living books—and who was in touch on the other side with actual men and women, the lives they lived and the problems they had to face. The Rev. J. F. MA'FTHEWS (formerly of Latchford Baptist Church) had a hearty reception on rising to endorse the welcome on behalf of the Free Churches of Sheffield. He said he would not be sorry to find himself back in the dear old town of Warrington. It was not that he loved Sheffield less, but he loved War- rington more. Referring to Mr James's work in Sheffield he said that, after serving his own church to the full level of his bent, he had given himself to the wider service of the Free Churches of the city. " I have ventured " continued Mr. Matthews " with some degree of timidity on to a political platform with Mr. James. I have even ventured to address an Independent Labour Party platform with him. One has found that wherever he has gone, there has been uniformly accorded to him, on the part of the Sheffield public, an affectionate welcome of which any man might be proud. Boys and girls, men and women have all felt the impact of his strong personality, and have responded to the affection he has meted out to them. You are singu- larly fortunate in having as your pastor one who will keep up the brilliant succession of ministers which it has been your good fortune to possess.!' (Applause).</p>
        <p>Church. Placing his band on his son's shoulder, Mr James touchingly concluded: I ask you to stand by this lad, in co-operating with him, loving him and especially in praying for him. LETTER FROM MR ZIBA ARMITAGE.</p>
        <p>The Rev Professor Armitage then read a letter from Mr Ziba Armitage, in the course of which he said : Although our relation of pastor and people is to be measured by only a few weeks, it is possible to say what I believe to be absolutely true, namely that there is increasing satisfaction among both young and old that Mr James was led to accept our call, that he has come amongst us, and that he has already given evidence that he is a leader and a teacher sent from God Short though the time is, we are able to rejoice in the confident expectation that our pulpit, enriched by long and faithful service in the past, will suffer no decline of influence during Mr James' pastorate. We have had abundant testimony to prove what happy relations have existed between Mr James, and his Sheffield people, and are assured that Wycliffe Sheflield's loss is Wycliffe War- rington's gain; ani whilst we rejoice in our happy fortune, we sympathise with our namesake, which is thereby plunged in the troublons waters of "Supply. We trust and pray that our Sheffield friends may be divinely led and directed, as we believe we have been ; and without damage to the ship,may eventually reach a peaceful anchorage,to resume all their noble service for the highest welfare of their neighbours. In your name and my own I welcome Mr James to the Pastorate of this church, believing that, in his own words, we shall , be drawn together more closely, "in the happy fellow- ship of the Gospel." I am running no risk in con- fidently predicting that the membership will enter whole-heartedly and zealously into his beneficent scheme; and follow his lead in working and praying for the extension of Christ's kingdom, and the uplifting of our fellows. Professor ARMITAGE referred to his own long association with Wycliffe Church and extended a sincere welcome to Mr James on entering upon a new lease in that great enterprise to which be had consecr4ted his life. Mr James was a child—indeed a grand-child—of the manse, and his father must be deeply moved with joy and gratitude to God that his Christian example had inspired his three sons to emulation. Having spoken upon the high vocation of the minister as a prophet or seer—one who conld perceive the things that really mattered in life—Professor Armitage said Mr James had come to this town with large sympathies with the crowded homes of the poor. He had wrought in Man- chester and understood something of the times in which we lived. With the enormous growth of mushroom towns and the transformation of the face of England had arisen problems which should lie in some degree upon the heart of every minster. He exhorted Mr. Jatnes to set before the people of Warrington a sacred end, to inspire their lives with great and holy visions. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr %VIM-JAM BESWICK also welcomed Mr James on behalf of Wycliffe Church. They believed they had found in him the right man, a man sent of God. In entering upon such an undertaking, there was no self- seeking or selfishness. Their new pastor had come to them with the gospel of peace, in the joy of the Holy Spirit, and they hoped he %you'd not be diappointed as be continued his ministry, declaring messages from the King.</p>
        <p>THE LANCASHIRE CONGREGATIONAL UNION. Mr E SIIORROCK ECCLKS welcomed Mr James on behalf of the Lancashire Con- gregational Union. He was glad to know that Congregationalism in Warrington was making a stride forward, and he was quite sure there was an opening for the new church which was being erected in the Padgate district. Wycliffe church was one of the important churches in the Liverpool district and they were very proud of it. Prom all he had heard, Sheffield's loss in Mr James's departure was Warring- ton's gain, and they would learn to love and esteem him. (Applause). THE REV. JOHN YONGE'S WELCOME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Wallington read a letter from the Rev John Yonge expressing regret that owing to absence from home and the state of Mrs Yonge's health, he was unable to attend the meeting. He continued :—Phe accession of Mr James to the oitice among you which I have been enabled to sustain for such a lengthened period,awakens not only my deepest interest,but creates my fullest satisfaction and delight. I believe that you have been Divinely guided to a Pastor who will prove himself to be "the right man in the right place." The occasions upon which I have had the opportunity of listening to his discourses have assured me that Mr James is a teacher " not needing to be ashamed'" but one who " rightly divides the Word of Truth." From all I hear and see about him, I believe he will show himself to be a wise leader, an earnest worker, and a man of zeal and enterprise in the Master's service. If I had been in your midst at your meeting, I should have cordially invited on his behalf your fullest sympathy and co-operation. He will probably seek to lead you in hitherto unaccustomed paths of work and service. He may propound new methods and suggest new departures. But, as he comes at your direct call to take up ministry among you, may I urge you to show oornpletest willinghood to accept his proposals and follow his lead ? Very especially would I beg the younger people among you to rally heartily around the by no means untried leader whom you have called to highest service. The future of our ever beloved Wycliffe is as truly in your hands as it is in your new Pastor's hands. If he should beckon you to follow along new paths of service, let me beseech you to obey his signal with alacrity and hopefulness. If all the people enthusiastitally join willing hands with the new leader, then the future of Wycliffe will be brighter and better than even the best of the past." Afcer extending a welcome to Mrs James the letter continued :</p>
        <p>I cannot say how intensely I hope that Wycliffe people will adequately estimate the importance of this newly opening period in Wycliffe history. Already they have suocessfully inaugurated the long con- templated new buildings in Padgate-lane. My sincere hope is that the cause of the Master will be thereby promoted and extended. But Wycliffe iteelf will need to be vigorously and continually upheld and developed and this can only be by the enthusiastic industry of the entire body of Wycliffe people. The new ministry in Pulpit, School, P.S.A. and Pastorate will depend for success as truly upon the people as upon the ministry. Will you permit your old minister, by these earnest \ words to sound a rallying note, and summon all his ' old and beloved friends to fresh consecration and new endeavour? So will the future eclipse the past, and make our ever dear Wycliffe a" praise" in Warrington. Mr WALLINGTON also announced that letters of apology for non-attendance had been received from Mr Gerard N Ford (late chairman of the Congregational Union), the Revs G Shillito (Oldham), A J Viner (who had acted as moderator), Dr Mellor and W Reid. The Rev W COOPER, secretary of the Free Church Ministers' Fraternal, wrote stating that the whole of the Free Church ministers united in extending a whole- hearted welcome to Mr James. They sincerely trusted that his ministry would be fraught with all that was necessary for the spiritual prosperity not only of Wycliffe Church but of Warrington generally. The Wycliffe Sisterhood, Sheffield, wired "happy greetings ' to Mr James. THE MAYOR'S WELCOME. THE FIGHT AGAINST SIN AND ERROR.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he had no special mandate from the people of Warrington in this matter, nor had he their permission to attend this meeting. However, be did not think it was necessary in these enlightened days, when all the forces of religion and of good bad joined together as regiments and battalions of one huge army to fight against the common enemy—sin and error. He congratulated Warrington and the country upon the fact that religious sects, instead of as formerly having internecine feuds, fighting sometimes bitterly among themselves for supremacy, were all helping one another like brothers in the same grand and noble cause. (Applause.) One reason why he deemed it a privilege to attend, was that the work, influence and character of a minister must inevitably have an effect on the efforts of the civic authorities to maintain the order, good government and health of the town. Cleanliness was next to godlLess, and be was strongly of opinion—he did not know whether he was right—that the work of a minister did not end in the pulpit. (Hear, hear.) It was far more reaching. He visited the sick and his influence was felt in the cause of sanitation. He was pleased to hear that Mr James took an interest in the poor, and in Lthat direction his services would be very welcome in Warrington, and, without overlapping, they would be welcomed by the civic, authorities. They had heard of the Warrington Town Council—(laughter)—but corporations, like individuals, were not perfect. He could, however, say without fear of contradiction that the Council supported the Health Committee most loyally not only in its usual routine work but in the sending out of health visitors to do social work and encourage the people to lead clean and healthy lives. He therefore, regarded the ministers as.colleagues, for their work and that of the Connell was very closely allied. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>CHURCH OF ENGLAND WELCOME. '• THE GOSPEL OF DISCONTENT."</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. BRACECAMP welcomed Mr. James on behalf of the Church of England. He said he would have sacrificed a great deal to be present, if only to witness that touching little scene when the Rev. Alfred James put his hand on his son's shoulder and pleaded with the congregation to pray for him. That would be the secret of their success in the future. Congregations were very fond of criticizing, but he was afraid they were not fond of praying. It they asked God to bless him who had been put over them every time they prayed, there would be more conversions and greater</p>
        <p>spirituality among the members of the churches. Mr Bracecan3p also emphasised the importance of Sunday school work, and said he was afraid the Churches had been apt to neglect it in the past. There should also be diligent visiting of the people. A visiting parson made a crowded congregation, in these days clergy and ministers ought to emphasise more and more the old story of Jesus and His love. If that could not fill our churches, nothing else would. There was a tendency—and he was afraid we were not free from it in Warrington—to preach a gospel of dis- content, opening wounds which they knew existed, but giving no remedy whereby they could be cured. He was certain, from his first sermon, that Mr James in his ministry there would put God first, and if he did so his work would be a great success. Although he had not been sent to that meet- ing by his fellow-clergy, be believed they were friendly and brotherly enough to recognize a good man coming amongst them, lie was certain they would agree with him in wishing Mr James" God speed and praying that he might be an instrument, in God's hands, to lead men and women out of darkness into His most marvellous light. (Applause). TuE FREE CHURCH COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>Mr C E PARKER said he was sure Mr James would receive a cordial and loving welcome from the Free Churches of the town. The Mayor had referred to the strife and jvalonsy which used to exist between the different churches, and they were glad such a happy change had taken place in their relationships. There was, however, some danger of going to the other extreme and of thinking it broad-minded not to connect oneself with any place of worship but to float about, like drift-wood, from one place to another, and not to get one's shoulders into the collar or do any definite Christian work. He hoped Mr James's work in War- rington would be a great blessing to Wycliffe Church primarily and also to the whole town. (Applause).</p>
        <p>THE PASTOR'S REPLY. THE DANGER OF THE PRESENT DAY.</p>
        <p>The Rev T T James, in reply, said his wife and he appreciated the cordial welcome which had been extended to them, for they held it to be the genuine expression of the personal and collective feelings of those anatt.ng whom they had come to work. It had been difficult to sever all the ties of affection and friendship which bad been formed by their six years of service in Sheffield, I and if what had been said by the representatives of Wycliffe Church, Sheffield, had seemed, in some way, exaggeration, it could be forgiven because it sprang from love. Having thanked the Mayor for his happy and wise words, as head of the civic community, Mr James referred to the presence of the Rev W Bracecamp. He was glad to say that both at his farewell meeting at Sheffield and at his recognition meeting at Warrington, he had had a Church of England clergyman to wish him well. It was a happy thing that on such occasions as that theygot that common intercourse and good feeling between the different branches of the Church which had been shown that night. He hoped his relationships with the representatives of the Church of England, and their own relationships as a church would be very happy. One feature of the meeting which bad given him much encouragement was the reading of the letter from the Rev John Yonge. There could be no greater inspiration to a new minister than to follow in the steps of a man who, by long and evoted service, had endeared himself to his p,eqglg., trusted very sincerely that the work they were beginning together, as minister and people, would be exceedingly happy and have good results in the future history of the church In these days they were rather inclined to imagine that they could stand en the vantage ground of criticism and approach the work of those who had gone before them from a standpoint of superiority. They were apt to think that the Church of the past did not know how to tackle its problems. It was, therefore,a good thing that they as a church were assembled in a building like that, where its historic associations, its honourable story and the memory of many saintly and heroic lives continually spoke to them of what religion meant in the way of self-sacrificing service in days gone by. He was not so much afraid lest they should in these days repeat the mistakes of those who had gone before them as be was fearful lest they should not come up to the standard of the tremendous sacrifices which their forefathers were prepared to make and, at what they conceived to be the cost of religious conviction, they were prepared to pay. Our problems were quite new in the form in which they came. We were con- fronted with new circumstances, as far as the externals were concerned, and the one thing we could learn, above everything else, from those who had preceded us in the history of the Church, was to catch again that spirit that was prepared to make sacrifices and to put into the work of Christ things that really cost something, if we were to meet the situation that faced us to-day. They at Wycliffe worshipped in a church the long history of which was eloquent with deeds, memories and associations. It represented the best traditions of Church life. Surely that should be an inspiration to them—he believed it must be if they could only have historic imagination. Mr James impressed upon the meeting the value of public worship and especially of right preparation to participate in it. He had been preaching a long time, and he had dis- covered that his greatest difficulty was not to prepare a sermon but to prepare himself—to get into the spirit, the attitude of mind and the condition of soul which made the Irving touch between God and man. Alluding to the work at Wycliffe, Mr James said they had made an encouraging beginning and they looked forward to the days that lay ahead of them.. I:hey needed brave hearts, courage and, above all, a high ideal of what their work was and of what the Church stood for. They could not make the ideal too high. Sometimes they imagined that the work of the Church could be realised by lowering their ideal, but it could not. They must have a high ideal of the task set them in the town, of the influence they were to wield in the com- munity, and of what Church membership meant to them. They must see to it that. nothing interfered with that Ideal , but that they kept it clear in front. They were there to prove that the Gospel of Christ was vital for their own age and community, and to help to bring nearer the Kingdom of God. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PASTOR'S FATHER SPEARS.</head>
        <p>THE PASTOR'S FATHER SPEARS.</p>
        <p>The Rev ALFRED JAMES said that was not the first recognition meeting he had attended in connbction with his three sons who were Congregational ministers. (Apphuse.) In pleading for the co-operation of the congregation with the new pastor, Mr James said his son believed in an aggressive Christianity—hence in an aggressive church—and he (the speaker) had been made to feel that that was part of the creed of Wycliffe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SONG-WRITER'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>SONG-WRITER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>The death is announced of Air Edward William Rogers, a well-known song writer. Among his most successful songs were " ! "If yon want to know the tima ask a policeman," and "Baby."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>A</p>
        <p>THE TRADE OF THE MERSEY.</p>
        <p>The population in this country dependent on ,tbsg. oversee trade of the Mersey is probably as great as ttm, of the whole of Canada, and is far greater than tbi; population of Australia. Even if the Empire were Oa to supply all that we must buy, it is idle to suPlOw- that the Dominions could buy all that we want to And if we want to sell to other nations we must b: ready to buy from them in exchange what they have sell. If we refused to buy any more wheat from OP Argentine, how could the Argentine continue to 01 your galvanised sheets ? And if Canada does 0° want to buy your galvanised sheets, how can pay for Conadian wheat? The facilities for exchsegt must bees freo and as wide as we can make them. Tll°4 is the policy that we have followsd for sixty years se upwards. That is the policy which Mr Chamberlslo told us ten years ago was leading to immediate disaster; Under that policy the value of the overees trade of tb, Mersey has increased by upwards of fifty per cent.durilo the last ten years. Our politicians talk of an average duty of 10 er cent. as a moderate one—but here, where we live, MO to face with the realities of the world's oompotitil trades, we have to watch fractions of one per co", The dues levied by the Mersey Docks and Harlow Board for the purpose of maintaining the Port of Livildr pool do not represent a quarter of one per cent. tiPv., the total value of the imports and exports, but we kW)" what. would be the effect on our foreign tradea if thole dues were doubled. .• • The cost of handlill the cargoes imported into Liverpool is represented 11 the master porter rates, and the merchants at tbl moment are fighting against a proposal to inorel,o° these rates by a sum which equals a 1-25th of one r cent of the value of tha cargoes—that is, against °°l increase representing less than 10d upon a value .£loo.—Sir Norman Hill, at Ellesmere Port.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WORKER SAVED BY ZAM-BUt</head>
        <p>A WORKER SAVED BY ZAM-BUt</p>
        <p>---- ____ Wonderful Healing of Badly Poisoned Hand.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. W. Egby, of 109, Field-road, Reading,Berke3 is firmly con vinced that but for Zam-Buk he wouto have lost his right hand. To the "Reading Observer, Mr Egby said : " On getting home from work one night I found! little gathering under my right fore-finger nail. Vl' sore quickly spread, and in a few days the hand 1.0 practically useless. Though I have a wife and eig d children to keep I was obliged to remain off work, efj was at hrme altogether for seventeen weeks. " Finding ordinary ointments useless, I went to OA Royal Berkshire Hospital, where they told me lei hand was badly poisoned. The surgeons lanced tbe hand in five or six places, and also amputated 11,1.10 finger at the second joint. I went regularly to 41, hospital for several months, and was then told tbe; they could do nothing more for me unless I had till remainder of my poisoned finger cut off. However, refused to face the knife again. "I really didn't know what to do until my sisteri law persuaded me to try Zam-Buk. So I got a bog.ll Zam-Buk from the chemist and obtained such rell‘el from the first dressings that I said to my wife believe I have got the right stuff at last.' Zam-13111 soothed my pain and enabled me to sleep better &amp;le had done for months. So I kept up the Zara-136 treatment regularly. The poison was all drawn flit; my hand, the sores were cleansed, and in a few week' my hand was sound and well again." Always keep Zam-Buk handy. Sold by chetnie! in sealed packages only at is. 11d. or 2s. 9d, Bewtory of cheap imitations and substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SCALDED TO DEATH.</head>
        <p>SCALDED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Engine-Room Tragedy on Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>As the steamer City of Liverpool was coming doff° the Manchester Ship Canal between Moore and RuncotO on Monday night, a steampipe burst in the engine.roo/ deluging Arthur Inman, aged 30, of Clverston 0 formerly of Speakman-street, Runoorn, with 14104 water. The vessel was stopped at Runcorn, but t_i° unfortunate man died before a doctor's arrifo's Another man had a narrow escape. Inman is a brother of Christopher Inman, 06 Runcorn Northern Union footballer.</p>
        <p>WINTER OCCUPATION IN ONTARIO. The winter season in Ontario gives the lumberman opportunity to gather in the raw material for his mit Camps are formed and gangs of men Ju t and haul se - trees to the rivers or railway stations. Many yourl6 men who have spent the summer on the lend prefer t 7 Winter winter in the woods, because large wages are effete The new settler is busy outting the pulpwood telegre9, poles, ties or larger trees for lumber, and hauling thero to the railway, thus getting the first returns from 13"„ Government grant. The snow gives excellent trap; portation facilities through the woods. Lumbering an occupation which will last under the restoratle' system for decades, and the probability is for centuriet Ontario's timber resources are enormous, the ArNi includes over 100,000 square miles, and the settle, amount of timber is estimated at 20,500,000,000 fee' The pulpwood estimates are 300,000,000 cords.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLCRES.—According to " Mercantile Gazette," the number of corm:Dere l failures published in Great Britain and Ireland duri the five weeks ending Saturday, Feb. 22nd, was 63 ; The number in the corresponding five weeks of last Ye.o was 649, showing a decrease 12, being a net dscreal in 1913, to date, of 123.</p>
