        <p>1</p>
        <p>LYMM. hit exIL WILL—The last will and testament of the he: -"s Eliza Ouston, of Higher-lane, Lymin, has tin declared for probate purposes at £4,039* rieb?sPITAL SUNDAY —ln conjunction with the War- prer,o Hospital Sunday observances sermons were Y Mr a b cued the th L Wesleyan Church on Sunday last - E Passmore, when collections for the above havitation were made. The local Congregationalists I\i'3 alai) forwarded a donation. lar GoULD's HIRE HORSES.—There have been fa rig 9 musters of breeders, buyers and sellers of the etilno4l British Shire horse at the 34th. annual London t " the Shire Horse Society which has been held 1u pr.,"e Agricultural Hall, Islington, from Tuesday to aY this week Amongst the awards list is the name MAT Jalues Gould, of Lymm, whose horse obtained of","l2orth prize in the three-year-old stallion class, out a exhibits. In the yearling stallion class his '4 ,Imni Dreadnought " was highly commended and his itynnt. Baronet," commended. the iMPERANce. MEETING.—On Wednesday afternoon Te -,.?rarn branch of the National British Women's ii ttilhverance Association held a very successful meeting 'Y i° Primitive Methodist schoolroom there being an r teem et attendance. Mrs G L Weliord, the branch . ri of trts:-.ut, presided, and in her opening remarks spoke Wetr lormation of the branch and the benefits that 1i01,,1 Probably accrue. Mrs Carver, of Cranage Hall, trei,7ea Chapel (president of the Cheshire County said that the fact of their being no branch at Year,,r4 had lain heavily on their minds for a number of the -aad it therefore gave he: great pleasnre to be at greer asent meeting. She spoke principally on the to Power of prayer, especially in its relation twe `6toPerance work and gave as examples Stie the characters In " Broken earthenware." thern”as followed by Miss Agnes B Dutton. of Stao Coe Rail, Middlewich (hon. treasurer of the Cheshl% the taion), who spoke of how to make a Branch of life A.T.A a success. Drawing illustrations from the beta:', soldier she spoke of the value of units and the Ivor-cBl4 of individal members doing their share of the d She said that they must regard their meetings ing ti;'• Mrs Welford proposed a resolution support- „ action of the police in their endeavours to pr.,- a.e3 :na renewal of the licence of the Anchor Inn. This Toe e"onded by Miss Leigh and carried unanimously. AfterPeakers were heartily thanked for their services. tneet 4°oll tea was banded round. The Branch has been the mauccessful since its formation a few months ago, p4,7embership having now attained a total of fifty. ragr."4ll-1 OF REV. JOHN WATSON.—It is With sincere of ip -p Of 4that we record the passing away, at the residence ,B°n-in-law in Guernsey, in his eighty-first 'ear, .IYtitsn'e„ Rev John Watson, D.D. For forty years r was a valiant worker in the Primitive Methodist Afca ti—l'nation, first in the North of England, then in Coan '. Australia and subsequently as principal of the his wel;Ptial College at Manchester. On the death of Cowic,'"e, with his younger daughter, now Mrs. thB came to reside with his son-in-law and daughter, !ilivand Mrs Wilson Eccles, who were at that time jag LYnani. He went with them to their succeed- Glier-rcuita Swinton, and after three years, on to beetnseY• Though of a retiring disposition, the ho or won the esteem of a large number of people. A Vooezautt 12,e or ant n-tstgree ren,t: igelY attended funeral service was held in the Great Church, Manchester, yesterday after- rcyh. the interment took place at the URBAN DISTRICT DISTRICT COUNCIL. LACK O Th- HOUSING ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>monthly meeting of the Lymm Urban District was held on Thursday evening in the Council ptt,i`tOber, Mr W G Groves presiding. There were also (vitiset --Rev G B Thurston Messrs T H Warburton 0 fl'ehairman), G L Welford, J H Holt, S Davies, r\k4rOtnith, R Gotobed, F Lockyer, W Milliard Dr Edward Meyer de Jong (medical officer), "tt (surveyor), and W L Donaldson (gas manager). Mr 1. DITCHFIELD'S BRIDGE. mid eokyer said he would like to ask the Clerk if he bitoCrie_e,r,d, anything from the County Council re o Bridge. eat Clerk : I have not heard anything further than I 'ted at the last meeting. -14°okYer thought they ought to ask the County hri'd"il to Put danger signals at each side of the said te. The , NEW ROAD FROM LAKESIDE. COofir Minutes of the Roads Committee, which were toad tmed, stated with reference to the proposed new kid eAr°,to the lake side to Cherry-lane that the plan 9,.h0-n-eti°o had been produced by Messrs W and vore;., eo behalf of the Lymm Estate Development to L'enY• It was decided to instruct the clerk eoujellte .Messra Owen to the effect that the ~.,oooatr raised no objection to the propmed mode of `-'oun',lletion, but it must be clearly understood that the Nab did not in any way commit itself to taking over Y when completed; also that the whole cost an efficient sewer for the property to be '44t4-te4f.Togaide the road, must, be borne by the Lymm L'avelopment Co. „Ott +k EDUCATION SUB•COMMITTEE. ue_nroposition of Mr Davies it was dPeided that e,ocl " B Thurston and Messrs Lockyer, Warburton, 'he 11,(145,ves be re-elected to represent the Council on °Diointilitlistration sub-committee for education. In his tePtA-11 theY could not find more suitable members as -413tat1ves. THE EXTRA REPRESENTATION. t44,.lektirßrit Produced the order of the County Council ti,ettiloitig the number of members of the Lymm Council members and it would be confirmed at next ,k.stoi,,reg of the County Council. ilgitti-,;;T:toR to the Rev G'l3 Thurston, the Clerk said -Lt"elli two stated that one of the councillors for h-Etthhm Ward (to which the additional member "Loco allocated), should be elected and hold other 4or three years and should then retire, and the fc't "uocillo should be elected and hold office Itt, the?, Years and then retire ; the order of retirement Diltet n",,rBt election to be determined according to their °EI t,he poll at the election—the Councillor lowest I,cleestit-Loll retiring first. If there was no poll, or if a rt,IN arose in consequence of an equality of votes "„etertii;;ll the two councillors, the matter should be tirned by ballot conducted under the direction of 00 th -B,ltriot Council. The order comes into operation "ate of its final approval by the County Council. COUNCILLORS AND GUARDIANS. Ath ,`-qt °Q .erk informed the Council that Saturday April •ett fixed for the elections of Councillors and °Deo Cn b a for the Urban District. The poll would e r°ro 12 am to 8 p.m. At tt WESLEYAN CHAPEL GAS ACCOST. (!iIZ/414th,,--eoeling o'f the Gas Committee a letter was read LAN `-;.,?ecret.ary to the trustees of the Wesleyan a compromise. The Clerk was</p>
