      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>OtIRES THE DEAF</head>
        <p>OtIRES THE DEAF</p>
        <p>IN 30 DAYS</p>
        <p>•arvellous Effect of New Home</p>
        <p>Treatment</p>
        <p>A FREE TRIAL TO ALL.</p>
        <p>42.01'e1Y has so much interest ever been Nhti"sed as that caused by the discovery of a a(dlerfully simple cure for Deafness and Noises. It has proved so remarkably I)ea'reilsitil even in the worst forms of chronic ess, that the Discoverer will gladly tbiß free trial to any sufferer. Mention 13,0141 aper and enclose id. stamp to cover e. Address :—Elmer Shirley (Suite Great James Street, Bedford Row, 4011, .w.e.</p>
        <p>(Sana(ak, Ltd.)</p>
        <p>instructed to write saying that the committee could not agree to that, but to atk for payment of the account. BREAKING LAMPS.</p>
        <p>On charges of breaking gas la-mps, three youths appeared before the Council. The Chairman severely censured the lads and said that if the case had been taken into the court they would probably have been fined 403 and costs. It had been a difficult matter for the Council to keep the cases out of court. The Council hoped the appearance of the lads before them would serve as a warning to them and to others. They should not throw at lamp glasses, neither should they play football in the street. Mr Warburton said they had had so much damage done that they could not overlook the offences. One'youth was fined 4s 6d and the other two were ordered to pay the cost of the damage which had been done. It was decided to remunerate persons who had given information of the damage to the Council. MEDICAL OFFICERS ANNUAL REPORT. The Medical Ofticer(Dr de Jong) submitted his annual report.He stated that the population on the 1911 census was 4,989, an increase on the 1901 census of 282. The present, population was 5,025. During the year 1912 there had been 104 births (53 males and 51 females), the birth rate being equal to 209 per 1,000 of the inhabitants. There had been 67 deaths, giving a death rate of 13 per 1,000. Of the 67 deaths, 25 were of persons of 65 years and over. Of that 25, 18 were over 70 years of age ; 5 between 70 and 75 ; 4 between 75 and 80 ; 6 between 80 and 85 ; 1 between 85 and 90, and there were two deaths of persons over 90 years of age. There were only two deaths from cancer, as against the average of six for the preceding five years. There was one death from nhthisis, the average for the preceding five years being five. Eighteen cases of phthisis were notified, four of school children. There were 16 cases of death from organic heart disease; the average for the preceding five years was nine. There had been a large number of sudden deaths from heart disease during the year. In Lymm they had kept a record of measles cases for the last 10 years ani briefly, there were 511 cases between 1902 and 1911 with three deaths. Every one of the three 'deaths were of children under five years of age and (lie only a fortnight old, which gave a rate of •6 per thousand of the cases attacked. This year there were 307 cases, giving a percentage of •7 of the cases attacked, with two deaths, which was a remarkably low percentage. Although he did not think that the spread of measles could be checked by notification or the closing of schools, he did think that notification led to such care and treatment as lessened the severity of the attacks, and as a consequence therefore lessened the number of deaths. In regard to infantile mortality. there had been 13 deaths of children under 12 months of age during the year, giving a rate of 121 per thousand births registered. The average for the preceding five years was eight deaths,giving a rate of 70 per thousand of the births registered. The figures for 1912 were remarkably high. Nine of the deaths were of children under four weeks and five were under one week old. The Rev G B Thurston in moving the adoption of the report and proposing a vote of thanks to the medical officer said the doctor's report was one of the most important they received during the year. As a member of the Sanitary Committee he moved a vote of thanks to Dr de Jong. As one moved it, 0110 did so in no formal manner. The care that the doctor took of health of the district was beyond all praise. He knew of the large amount of time and attention the doctor devoted to his work. Anyone who knew Lymm, and especially the young people of Lymm, knew that the children were well looked after and kept free as far as possible from any epidemics that could be stopped, except of course measles which they could not control. For there being so few cases of scarlet fever end other diseases they had to thank the doctor for the care and skill he exercised in keeping them free from those diseases. (Bear, hear.) He would also like to include the name of Mr Mort for the assiduous way in which be performed his duties. With Dr de Jong backed up by Mr Mort, they could feel that they were in the hands of two gentlemen whom they could rely upon and have a certain feeling of security for the health of the district.</p>
        <p>Mr Welford seconded and the report was adopted and the vote of thanks accorded.</p>
        <p>HOUSING IN LYIIIII.</p>
        <p>_ Mr Gotobed said be had intended to bring a matter forward but he would defer it until the nest meeting. He gave notice that he would call the attention of the Council to the lack of housing accommodation in Lymm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GI;ILD.—In connection with the above guild, the annual festival was held on Sunday and Monday last. The Rev R Wilkins Rees, of Barrow-in- Furness, conducted the Sunday services, and preached two magnificent inspiring sermons. On Monday even- ing Mr Rees lectu red on "Theology in modern fiction," his wonderful knowledge of books, together with great elocutionary capacity, made the evening an extremely edifying and enjoyable one. Miss Dutton presided with her usual good taste. A hearty vote of thanks to Mr Rees was proposed by Mr Meldrum and seconded by Mr A Davies. The collections were in aid of Guild funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHURCIL—Special services were held on Sunday last in cOnnection with the Home Mission anniversary. Eloquent sermons were preached by the Rev F 11 DIM (of Sale) in the morning, and by the Rev John Gunnell at the evening service.—On Tuesday evening the annual meeting was held. The Rev E Morgan conducted the opening exercises and later intro- duced the lecturer, the Rev T Galland Hartley—chair- man of the Liverpool district. The chair was occupied by Mr Frank Harris (senior Circuit steward). The Rev T G Hartley delivered a very interesting and instruc- tive lecture on "The Shetland Islands." The subject was finely illustrated by a series of beautiful views of Shetland scenery. Mr Parkinson ably manipulated the lantern. A vote of thanks was passed to Chairman. lecturer, and lanternist, upon the proposition of Mr F Wright seconded by Mr T B Duckworth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GRAPPENHA LL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHA LL.</p>
        <p>THE BIG SEWBRAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday the Grappenhall Sewerage Sub-Committee reported having met, and recommended that plans, estimates and apportiontments for making Albert-road, Victoria-avenue and York Drive, be approved. A letter was received from the Local Government Board with respect to the Councils application to borrow .£18,560 for purposes of sewerage and sewage disposal for Grappenhall, Latchford Without and Thelwall, expressing their willingness that one tank and one filter be omitted from the scheme, pro- vided the Council will undertake to carry out the additional works as soon as the dry weather flow of sewage has increased to such an extent as to render their provision necessary ; (2) If the Council desire to revise their estimate to meet this modification fresh ' notices would have to be served upon the overseers ; (3) The Board should be informed when definite arrangements have been made for access to the outfall works, and when the consent of the Railway Company has been obtained to the crossing of the line ; (4) That manholes should be provided instead of lamp holes at the heads of the sewers. The Committee recommended that the Board be requested to accept the full scheme as submitted with the addition of manholes at the beads of sewers instead of lamp holes. (2) That in view of the fact that the Local Government Board are prepared to sanction the scheme as submitted to them by the District Council ; the owners of the land over which the Council require a right of way to the proposed outfall works, be urged, in the interests of the parish of Thelwall, to withdraw their refusal thereof, and so save the expense which will be incurred if the Council are forced to apply for a Provisional Order. Os the proposition of Mr G Pilling, seconded by Mr Mclntyre the report was adopted, and it was also decided that the materials required for the making of the streets be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>i.,..,ii:;ii,i,K,,,.,i,,i.i::.!iiiii,,i„:..gi!i1.::;.130.iiii-iiiii,,i.nii!iii.iiiiil!il:ii!,.,!iiiii,,:,iiiii.:,:i.liiiis</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>'The Examiner, Saturday, March 1, 1913,</head>
        <p>'The Examiner, Saturday, March 1, 1913,</p>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>TRADES UNION MEETING.—On behalf of the local tanners, the Liverpool and District Tanners Union held a most successful meeting in the St. Mary's Infants' School, Penketh, on Monday last. This being the first meeting of the Tanners' Union a good deal of interest was created in the venture. The speaker's were Mr T Dunford and others. Mr Sage, the president, was unavoidably absent. Between 30 and 40 tanners joined the Union at the close of the meeting.</p>
        <p>MISS DOROTHY VERE, As Princess Sadie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>CROFT.</head>
        <p>CROFT.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION.—The Rev H J Wadeson, Rector of Croft, has been presented by the members of the choir with a handsome stole (purple and gold), a sermon case, and a combined vestment and travelling bag, and by the church officers, Sunday school teachers and a few church workers, with a writing case and a reading lamp. The respective presentations were made by Mr Hurdus (organist and choirmaster), and Mr Ingham (people's warden).</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>EXHIBITION OF FANCY DANCING.—On Wednesday evening the pupils of Miss Gertrude Brown gave their annual exhibition of fancy dancing, at the Hall Cafe, before a large and enthusiastic audience. The programme opened with the Tarantella, in Italian costume, by Misses D Tyrer and May White, who gave a vivacious and finished performance of this difficult yet attractive dance. Miss Southerden followed in a very picturesque fan dance, and the pre- cision of movement gave ample evidence of the careful training bestowed. Skirt dancing has not now the vogue of a few years ago, but as an exhibition of the rhythm of motion it would be difficult to find a more suitable dance and little Miss May White, with her flaxen hair,in startling contrast to the crimson and silver costume,gave a most excellent and graceful performance and the audience would not be denied a repetition Much amusement was caused by the pierrette and pierrot " Flirtation " Dance' and the billing and cooing of Misses A Cook and M Sutherden left nothing to be desired, and an encore was demanded. The first portion of the programme cotcluded with a set of Lancers, danced in perfect time and style by eight maidens, four dressed in green and four in crimson A resumptien was Earle with the Coon dance by Misses A Cook, M White, A Tyrer, E Domville, H Domville D Tyrer, M Southerden and M ewall, and the audience was delighted with the costumes, singing and eccentric gyrations of the smiling dancers so typical of the band of Uncle Toms' Cabin, an encore being the inevituble result. Ihe Highland Fling followed and was danned in Scottish costume, by Miss May White in very spirited fashh n In contrast to this last item Miss Annie Tyrer gave a dance the title of which did not appear on the programme but would be well named "a poem so full of meaning and grace were all the movements. The Misses E Domville and D Hughes next danced the Irish jig in Hibernian dress as though they had been trained in the Emerald Isle, the quick steps and catchy music being very pleasing. A feature of the evening was the dance named " Simplicity " by Misses M White, M Halliwell D Tyrer and Mas ters Brayshaw, Halliw ell and Domville and song by the three boys, the actions, singing and dresses so greatly pleased the audience that the dance had to be repeated. The programme concluded with the ever popular Sailor's Hornpipe performed by six young " Tars " in costume, their breezy style and lively actions causing much amusement and they, too, were encored. interspersed in the dancing was instrumental music, brilliantly executed by Mrs Haviour, Misses C Newall, A.R.M.C.M. and M Bouchier, and this was much enjoyed Miss Dolly Jones proved an efficient pianist for the dances. Great credit is due to Miss Brown for the excellent manner in which the young dancers had been trained, and special mention should be made of the fa, t that the skirt, coon, fling and simplicity dances were composition of this talented young lady. Mr H M Ashton gave a reading in his inimitable humorous style and his bearers would not be content until he had been twice recalled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WHAT DYSPEPTICS SHOULD EAT.</head>
        <p>WHAT DYSPEPTICS SHOULD EAT.</p>
        <p>A PHYSICIAN'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>"Indigestion and practically all forms of stomach trouble are, nine times out of ten, due to acidity; therefore stomach sufferers should, whenever possible, avoid eating food that is acid in its nature, or which by chemical action in the stomach develops acidity. Unfortunately, such a rule eliminates most foods which are pleasant to the taste as well as those which are rich in blood, flesh and nerve building properties. This is the reason why dyspeptics and stomach sufferers are usually so thin, emaciated and lacking in that vital energy which can only come from a well-fed body. For the benefit of those sufferers who have been obliged to exclude from their diet all starchy, sweet or fatty food, and are trying to keep up a miserable existence on gluten pro- ducts, I would suggest that you should try a meal of any food or foods which you may like in moderate amount, taking immediately afterwards half a tea- spoonful of bisurated magnesia in a little hot or cold water. This will neutralise any acid which may be present, or which may be formed, and instead of the usual feeling of uneasiness and fulness, you will find that your food agrees with you perfectly. Bisurated magnesia is doubtless the best food corrective and antacid known. It is not a medicine, and has no direct action on the stomach; but by neutralising the acidity of the food contents, and thus removing the source of the acid irritation which inflames the delicate stomach lining, it does more than could possibly be done by any drug or medicine. As a physician I believe in the use of medicine whenever necessary, but I must admit that I cannot see the sense of dosing an inflamed and irritated stomach with drugs instead of getting rid of the acid—the cause of all the trouble. Get a little bisurated magnesia from your chemist, eat what you want at your next meal, take some of the bisurated magnesia as directed above, and see if I'm not right."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. Visit of the O'Mara Opera Company.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. Visit of the O'Mara Opera Company.</p>
        <p>Grand opera, presented on a scale of completeness worthy, in every respect, of the chosen works of great masters, was the attraction at the Royal Court Theatre in the earlier part of the week, and 'Warring- tonians had their first opportunity of hearing the fine company which Mr Joseph O'Mara has got together. Though the public patronage fell short of what might reasonably have been expected for productions of such outstanding merit, it was gratifying to note that each succeeding evening witnessed an increase in the attend- ance until on Wednesday, -when " Maritana " was staged, most parts of the house were comfortably filled. The only regret of the faithful musical enthusiasts was that the stay of the company was so abort. The engagement opened with Gounod's " Faust" and one found it exceedingly difficult to account for the meagre audience at what was undoubt- edly a notable and distinguished performance. The beauty and grandeur of its music—not least the wonderful orchestration—the deep emotional and tragic appeal of the story, and the variety and colour of its settings and incidents combine to invest "Faust" with a spell peculiarly its own. The performance on Monday impressively demonstrated the all-round capa- , bilities of the O'Mara Company, and the principal parts were admirably placed. Miss Edith Evans, as Marguerite, presented a brilliant study of that difficult and trying role. Endowed with a beautifully flexible voice of remarkable range, she charmed the audience by her artistic and soulful vocalization, vihich was revealed to the best advantage in the " Jewel song "and the thrilling finale of the last act. Her work throughout was quite in accordance with the ideal traditions of the part. Mr William Boland, as Faust, rose to the occasion with eminent success, his vocal and histrionic equipment enabling him to impersonate the passionate wooer in remarkably powerful style. He Bang " All hail ! thou dwelling pure and lowly " with great fervour and tenderness. The purity of his voice, particularly on the upper register, made his singing throughout a joy to listen to Mr Kingsley Lark took a large share of the honour. as Mephistopheles—a part which calls for all the resources of tho actor, as well as for outstandit.g musical powers. He was most realistic in both respects, investing his:portrayal with sardonic humour and:malicious cunning. Miss Anna Lindsey showed distinct talent as Siebel and scored a great success in "The flower song" which she gave with charming freshness. Mr James T Pursaill fully realised the highly dramatic possibilities of the part of Valentine, the death scene being remarkably awe-inspiring. Miss Maud Baker and Mr Jay Ryan as Martha and Wagner respectively, also extracted the maximum of interest from comparatively minor characters. The chorus—a splendidly-drilled and well-balanced body of voices— did their work in finished and whole-hearted style. The orchestra distinguished itself by its tasteful playing, tone and execution alike giving cause for congratulation to Mr llarrison Frewin the conductor, who so ably and tactfully directed the whole of the forces under his baton.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening Bizet's "Carmen " was presented with Mr. O'Mara in the part of "Don Jose," Miss Irene Ainsley as " Carmen," Miss Rita Wallace</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LITTLE MARY O'HARA.</head>
        <p>LITTLE MARY O'HARA.</p>
        <p>"The Pride of Byzantia," the first of a new class of twice nightly attractions produced by Mr Francis Laidler, specially suitable for vatiety theatres, will be produced at the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, next week, and it is booked fur months ahead by the principal variety t-yndicatea in London and the Provinces. The low comedy element is very strong, every scene being brimful of fun. The play itself is a combination of musical comedy and pantomime, the pantomime predominating, affording unlimited scope for the comedians, of which they avail themselves fully. •No fewer than six of the principals ate comedy characters and "The Pride of Byzantia " is practically one laugh from the rise of the curtain to the fall. It is staged on a lavish scale, there are seven gorgeous scenes, and the costumes both for the chorus and principals are magnificent, and from special and exclusive designs. l'otbing is spared, the eye is always pleased with the beauty and richness of the settings and costumes, and the play itself quite bits the popular fancy because it suits all tastes. "The Pride of Byzantia " opens with a fine full set, "Mr Oofensteiu's office." Scene 2is "The Hotel Oriental, London," and scene 3 is a magnificent park scene. "The home of the Blenkiesops," a quaint kitchen scene comes next and that is followed by a beautiful full scene Gay Ostend," and scene 6 "The saloon cf Pi incer,s Sadia's Yacht." The finale scene is "The palace of Byzautia," which is truly a dream of Eastern sp'endour. Miss Dorothy Vere has a charming stage presence and makes an ideal "Princess." Elsie Pennell and Mary O'Hara, a pair of clever juvenile artistes, as " Barry " a London newsboy, and " Winnie " a Lmidon waif, are also pleasing features.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>YOUR LAST OPPORTUNITY.</head>
        <p>YOUR LAST OPPORTUNITY.</p>
        <p>" Suits " Ltd.'s sale of suits and trousers to measure at sensational prices finishes to-morrow, and those who have not paid a visit to " Suits " Ltd. shou:d not lose a moment if they wish to have a suit made at below cost price. Hundreds have already taken advantage of this firm's enterprising manner of conducting business. Rather than carry over what goods are left lifter each season, " Suits " Ltd, prefer to reduce their prices below cost. This guarantees their customers having only the newest and most up-to-date materials to select from. The public have more confidence in such a firm, and what they lose during their sales is more than made up by the ncreased prestige they gain and the c,nfidence they enjoy generally. Naturally, those wh are keen enough to recognise a genuine bargain when it offers itself benefit by such sales as " Suits " Ltd.'s. Prites for suits to measure range from 12/11 upwards, and trousers from 419, until (Saturday), night when the sale finishes.</p>
        <p>Any who have not yet ordered their extra suit at below cost prices will be wise if they visit the establishment of "Suits" Ltd. to-night, or to-morrow, the latest. Their address is Jubilee Buildings Ilorsemarket-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>BOY'S FATAL PLAY.</p>
        <p>INQUEST ON ST. HELENS RAILWAY VICTIM. An unusually sad story was told in St. Helens Town Hall, on Monday, at the inquest on Thomas Duffy, six years of age, who was killed by a Liverpool to Manchester express at St. Helens Junction Station, on Saturday afternoon. Arthur Traverse, aged five, said deceased and be were playing with whip tops near the station. Eventually Tommy went on tbe platform, but he (witness) remained outside the gate. Tommy's top went on the metals, and be went after it. Soon after he had gone on the line a fast train went through the station. Witness then went to look for Duffy, and saw him lying hurt on the line. He told a man who passed, and then went to tell Duffy's father. Frank H Gurney, a porter, described how he found the little boy's body on the line. He and another porter were about to lift it when the boy's father arrived.</p>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S HOME.</p>
        <p>The new home will be opened in March. THE COUNTY RATE.</p>
        <p>Mr W Hutchen, who represented the relatives of the dead boy, asked if it was not the practice for the porters and officials to go on the platform when an express train was due, to warn people to stand back from the edge of the platform. Witness said they usually did that, but not always. He had received no instructions to do it.</p>
        <p>The Foreman (Mr Beards) said it had been suggested to him that at some stations there was a special apparatus for warning everybody when an express was going through. If that was so they ought to have a warning signal at a station like the Junction. He understood there was a bell at Earlestown. The Coroner said that at Wigan and Earlestown a bell rang, but a bell ringing would not mean much to a child like that. The Foreman: But it would draw the attention of the officials. If it is necessary at Earlestown it is necessary at St. Helens Junction. Henry Yearsley, driver of the Liverpool to Manchester express, said his train went through St. Helens Junction Station at 3.19 on Saturday afternoon. He did not see anything on the line, and he certainly would have seen the boy if he had been on the plat- form. He whistled for a considtrable distance as they approached the station. His train would be travelling at the rata of 65 miles an hour at that place. On examining the engine at Manchester he found human rarnains on the tank and axles.</p>
        <p>VISITING COAIMITTER.</p>
        <p>After a retirement the foreman said the jury had agreed to a verdict of "Accidental de Ali," but they bad made many suggestions of what ought to be done in the way of signals &amp;c. But it was not for them to teach the railway oompany their business, and they recommended that some means should be provided to warn both the passengers and the officials when an express was about to pass through the station. They also felt that it would be better if the porters were men of some standing and experience, instead of youths. Mr W J Bland (who represented the London and North-Western Railway Company)said the jury's recom- mendations would be carefully and properly considered. The company regretted the accident,and on their behalf he expressed sympathy with the relatives. The Coroner (Mr F A Jones) recorded the verdict that the deceased was " accidentally killed wilhe trespassing on the railway."</p>
