        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday. Mr J Spruce presiding.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government Board approving of the appointment of Mr and Mrs Goddard as superintendent and matron of the Children's Home at salaries of £4O and £25 per annum. It was decided to hive an opening ceremony in con- nection with the Children's Home and to issue circulars asking for subscriptions from the Guardians and members of the Ladies Committee in order to make the necessary arrangements. The ceremony is to take place the day previous to the chi;dren being transferred to • the home.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the County Accountant re the County Rate for the year 1913-14. He said he expected the County rate would be :—Police rate 2d. in the £, Elementary Education 10d. in the £ and Higher Education 3d. in the £ which were the same as pre- viously. With regard to the provision in regard to main roads he was advised that there would be a large addition in the expenditure which would probably mean a halfpenny or three farthings in the £. He advised them to make provision for a rate of Bd. in the £ which was a halfpenny or three farthings of an increasa.</p>
        <p>Mr Roylance : On account of the heavy traffic. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>The Visiting Committee recommended that a vote of thanks be accorded the Preston Brook Band for a con- cert .which they bad given in the Wcrkbouse.</p>
        <p>A letter of sympathy to be sent to Mr P Collins on the death of his wife. The minutes were approved. TENDER ACCEPTED.</p>
        <p>A sub-committee appointed to go into the question of accepting tenders for the supply of linoleum and drapery at the Children's Home recommended that the tender of Mr Alfred Dodd, of Runcorn, for the provi- sion of fixing 47 blinds and the provision of linoleum be, accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CANDIDATE FOR WIDNES. VIEWS ON LABOUR QUESHONS.</p>
        <p>Mr J B Martindale, the prospective candidate for the Widnes Division, opened his campaign by visiting the district surrounding Widnes. Hough Green and Hale- bank were the meeting places on Tuesday night, and at both places the candidate was well received. Mr Pritchard Whelan, the newly-elected chairman for Hough Green, described the candidate as a most suit- able one for the constituency. Mr Martindale said that as a gentleman Colonel Hall Walker stood supreme, but as a politician his ability was of the lowest. He attached so little importance to political matters that he only attended 134 out of 604 divisions. To imagine a man attending only one-fifth of his duties was evidently a desire not to keep in touch with serious politics. Mr Martindale said he had youth on his side and a certain measure of business responsibility, but, if returned, be would have time to be in the House of Commons day by day. Careful organisation was needed, and he was going to devote a considerable amount of his time to the work. They as Liberals were well satisfied with what was happening in the House, and peat Liberal measures were well on Os way to the Statute Book. He (the speaker) was going to get full value out of the land question, and be would bring many striking illustrations as to how it affected Widnes. At the present it stultified industry and prevented development. He favoured a system whereby disputes in labour could be better dealt with. Instancing the case of the dismissed guard on the Midland Railway, he said it was not satisfactory for railway workers to break rules laid down by the Board of Trade. The union were behaving in a statesmanlike manner, and he sympathised with the men. The case of those people who were not paid proper wages at the present time would have to be submitted to legislation in future. He agreed that men should retain their right to strike and the employers to have the right to lock out, but strike, there be some system of arbitration whereby settlement could be speedily obtained. At Halebank officers were elected to work the district and afterwards Mr Martindale addressed them. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday Mr C E Linaker presiding.</p>
        <p>HOB HEY LANE.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the Frodsham Lordship Parish Council drawing attention to the bad state of Hob Hey Lane. It was stated that the road was in practically an impassible coudition. The matter was referred to the Highway Executive Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICERS' REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICERS' REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported one case of diphtheria at Appleton and one case of pulmonary tuberculosis and five other tuberculous cases.</p>
        <p>A BAD ROAD.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr It Ashton, Park-lane, Stockport, calling the Council's attention to the road from J Owen's, at Marsh Greens, leading towards the river. It was a disgrace to any township and seemed to have been used for tipping nightsoil. Further on,the mud had been thrown out of the brook in cart loads and was left upon the road and in wet weather it was like a morass.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : That is a road over which this District Council nave no jurisdiction at all. The Clerk : There is just the question as to whether a nuisance arises. Mr Ashton refers to the road having lase!, spoiled through the laying of sewers. The Chairman: There is some truth in that. There is no doubt that the laying of sewers did cut up the road to a certain extent.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the InApoctor visit the road and report if it were necedsary to erect sign boards in regard to the nuisance ; also that the repair of the road be referred to tbe Frodsham and Frodsham Lordship Joint Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Shad by a Shadow</head>
