      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Accident In Saw MIII.</head>
        <p>Accident In Saw MIII.</p>
        <p>Mr Thomas Beddard, 438, Knutsford-road, met with an accident whilst sawing wood in the John- street Steam Firewood Works at 10 a.m. on Monday. Mr Beddard was sawing a piece of wood when a portion flew back, bitting him in the face. He was taken to the Infirmary where it was found he had sustained a fractured jaw. He regained consciousness on Tuesday, and is now progressing satisfactorily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>--_ - - -</p>
        <p>HALL _ _N. Sir,—Referring to Councillor Pemberton's letter ie your issue of last Saturday, I wish to point out that I have always freely admitted that if the benefit accru- ing to the Musical Society were the only reason f,r mstallitg a good up-to-date on ia the Parr Hall,nly plea that one should be provided would perhaps not in itself carry sufficient weight, though I strongly maintain that the interests of the Society are also the interests of the town as a whole. It is possible, though I do not admit it, that the music-loving public are in a. large minority, but even so the micority are entitled to some consideration It this be denied, surely it would only be logical to at once close the Baths, Gymnasium, the School of Art, the Parks, &amp;c., &amp;c., which cost the ratepayers a far greater sum per annum than the provision of a g, rd organ uould. I do Dot suppose that Councilor Pemberton would wish for one moment to do this, and I certainly should not, but I venture to say that by far the larger majority of the inhabitants of Warrington derive no direct benefit from such institutions. Why then deny the musical minority (if such) the same privileges and consideration? It is quite possible and probab'e that much could be done by private enterprise, but it would nct be very dignified for the Corporation of such a town as Warrington, who are custodians of the welfare of the town, to go round with the hat, or get anyone else to do it for them, and I know that a number of the Conncil are of that way of thinking. if one or more of the wealthy members of the community would voluntarily come forward and present a good modern organ to the town well and good; it would be a nob'e and generous act which would be very highly appreciated, but failing this I contend that an organ ought to be provided by the town, and that ~iithout delay. In estimating the cost of such to the Town, Coun- cillor Pemberton, who I have no doubt opposes the proposition conscientiously, quite ignores the fact that quite apart from the Musical Society, an organ is badly needed, and that if such were available it would be used many times in the year, and would certainly reduce the loss on the working of the Parr Hall. As to the church organs, " Organo Pleno " in his letter fully disposed of the assertion that such are available and sufficient for all requirements. In the first place very few of such organs are capable of supplying the need, that in the Bold-street Chapel being, I believe, the only real modern up-to- date organ of recent construction in the town, and in the second place the opportunities of hearing recitals in churches and chapels are inadequate to meet the requirements of the community and none are available for public use, concerts, &amp;c. As to the cost of a new organ to the ratepayers, a very small fraction of id in the F.., on the rateable value would provide a much larger organ than I have suggested. I therefore again enter a strong plea that one should be provided, and that without further delay. Yours faithfully, JOHN P. REYNOLDS. Marlfield, Grappenhall.</p>
