      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>KNUTSFORD DIVISION LIBERALS. ---- MR. P. M. OLIVER MAKING HEADWAY.</head>
        <p>KNUTSFORD DIVISION LIBERALS. ---- MR. P. M. OLIVER MAKING HEADWAY.</p>
        <p>There was a large and enthusiastic gathering in the Reform Club, Manchester, on Saturday afternoon on the occasion of the annual meeting of the Knutsford Division Liberal Association. Lord Sheffield presided and among others present were : P M Oliver (prospective candidate), H P Greg, J R Tomlinson, C Jackson Flolmes, H Roberts, J I.ongland, E Lloyd Jones, J Kenworthy, and H Cliffe (secretary). The annual report reviewed the political events of the year. It stated that, judged by every known standard, the basis of our prosperity and the condition of trade are absolutely sound. Free Trade had come triumphantly out of the criticism and attacks to which it had been subjected during the past few years, while the advocates of Tariff Reform and Protection had, in utter confusion, suddenly hauled down the flag of food taxes. Protection was not only dead but damned.</p>
        <p>THE NEXT MEMBER. "To come to our own personal conceins (continued the report), your committee can only express to you their high estimation, increasing as the months roll on and as high hopes grow into positive knowledge, of the work that Mr P M Oliver is doing in the consti- tuency. "If strenuous application, if persuasiveness and ability, if the clearest exposition of Liberal principles are to count, Mr Oliver should be our member at the next election. Your committee feel we have been singularly favoured in having secured for the division such single-hearted service. In Mr Oliver every advantage is combined and they prophesy for our prospective candidate a distinguished political career." The committee also expressed their continued high appreciation of Mr H Cliff° as Secretary and Registra- tion Agent. The work at the Revision Courts was again done in a most efficient manner and the result was a substantial gain to the party in votes on the 1913 register. _ _ _</p>
        <p>THE OFFICEP S. The officers were re-appointed as follows: President, Lord Sheffield; chairman, Mr F A Haworth; vice-chairmen, Messrs 111 N Bickerton and A J King; bon treasurer, Mr P Greg; hon secretaries, Messrs A Arnold Thompson and J R Tomlinson. Messrs F A Haworth, R Holland Owen and H Cliffs were appointed delegates to the Lancashire and Cheshire and North- Western Liberal Federation. FRANCHISE REFORM. Lord Sheffield, in the course of an address on the political situation, said there was one important piece of legislation for which Liberals had been asking for a long time, and which they were now promised. They had felt the extreme injustice of having the local resident in a constituency outweighed by the voter who came in in consequence of same mete property qualificationivvithout living in the district and voting in a place where he had no interest except that of owning property. (Hear, hear). Liberals advocated the abolition of the plural ,voter, not because it would do them good, but because they thought it just. They advocated it beceuse they thought that an election should be local, and that an election should represent the opinion of the people in a particular locality. Liberals were not in favour of email pocket boroughs, and little sham constituencies. Votes should be of equal value throughout the country. If the country was to have free choice of candidates the whole cost of the election should be put on the public funds and a stop put to the present inordinate expenditure. That would do something to sweeten political life. (Hear, hear). MR. OLIVER AND THE LAND QUESTION. Mr P M Oliver touched upon land and housing reform. He said fixity of tenure had long been inscribed on the Liberal banner, and now it should be transferred to the land, not only for the farms, but also for the cottage homes. The great evil of the land monopoly, as far as he could see it, was not only a monopoly over the bodies of men, but it was also an attempted monopoly over the souls of men. There were many landlords who would never dream of asking a man's politics or religion, but they were honourable exceptions. They had hoped that the Tories would not have allowed that to continue, but the time had arrived when State interference was absolutely neces- saiy. The oppressed could not look to the landlords nor to the Church for relief. How few parish priests were there who had lifted up a voice against tyranny which they must know existed at their very doors? The Liberal programme was a large, democratic pro- gramme, and they must look for democratic support. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ULCERATED STOMACH And Anremia—Cured after 14 years by VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC.</head>
        <p>ULCERATED STOMACH And Anremia—Cured after 14 years by VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Excell, The Glebe, Barming, Maidstone, Kent, writes :—" For 14 years my daughter was a chronic invalid, being bed-ridden for half the time. She suffered from Ulcerated Stomach and Anmmia, and everyone said she was incurable. At last, in despair, I tried Veno's Seaweed Tonic, and now, after three months she is quite cured." Writing 4 years later, Mrs. Excell says :—" My daughter, is still keeping well, and people call her "The Walking Wonder.' Veno's Seaweed Tonic is a safe, speedy, and guaranteed remedy for all Stomach, Liver, Kidney' and Blood Diseases, and contains pure ingredients never before used in Great Britain. Price is. 11d. and 2s. 9d., of all chemists-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>I'lxaminer, Saturday, March 1, 1913. CHESHIRE HOUNDS KILL AT 'WALTON</head>
        <p>The I'lxaminer, Saturday, March 1, 1913. CHESHIRE HOUNDS KILL AT 'WALTON</p>
        <p>A SNAP-SHOT OF THE LAST SCENE IN REYNARD'S CAREER.</p>
        <p>loltoway.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire hunt had fine sport on Thursday of last week at Appleton and Walton. Three foxes were put no, and the last from Grimsditch ran through Appleton, up to the Firs, back to Walton, across the Canal, the hounds finally killing at Walton Hall.</p>
        <p>did not mean to say that the beslth laws wers ha, but they acre in some rteipetts n4iiciocjt This of the lieriltn Gann:lite. found that, they could go ii certain ciistance very well, hut whin li4y n- act point when they woeki like to go furth r, there was a sud.ien stopprige. Their jourbey could, however, he oontinwid try such aii sas they pot friim the Gitlin of Help. (Applause.) The Health Committee were doing their ntraust, as far as the IhNV .0011 a:I4,V, to improve the town and its saniiat'on, but it wis rather show work. If it weie only to, the co-operation which it willingly gave ii health vkiita tion in the poorer dis•ricts, the Guild deserved the gratitude and support of any town in which it had sway. During the coal strike the authorities derived great assistance front the Guild in edrninistie•ing the relief fund as bent ficielly as possible and guarding against imposition. His Worship contruended the aeticu of the Guild in regard to consumptive cases, pointing out that it gave practical help to the patients in matters in which the Health Gororuittee had no power to a t. It was a twitter of great satisfaction that censemotion seemed to he on the wave. To uses Ilitiernianism, the best way to cure consumption was to oreveht it, and the measeres to he taken with that end ie view wi-ris to ensure plenty fresh air, sunlight end good food, and a regular mode of lvirra, avoiding Arty circ,ini- stances that might depress the ssst,ro. The latest report with regard to tunerculer diseeses in Werrington was to the eff,ct that such cases were dimihishingi Only a year or two ago, one person out of every seven died of consumption of the lungs. To-day, one out of every eight died of the e hole !list of tubercular diseases. That was very satisfactory and spoke most eloquently of the fact that they were on the right track. Ile put down some of those good effects to the active and cordial co-operation of the. Guild of Help with the health authorities of the town. (Applause). SIR ALFRED HOPKINSON'S SPEECH. "NO CAUSE FOR DESPAIR."</p>
        <p>Sir ALFIVRD HoPKINSON said that in these days one found in the state of the country and of the world it great deal that was cause tor anxiety and for the deepest sorrow, hut nothing that was cause tor despair. There were certain inflireeoes that abid,3 tor ever, and forces were at work, to-day, perhaps mote stronelv than at any time in the past, which gave us cause for real hope. What we needed was two things—one was earnestness and enthusiasm the other was wise direction and sersib-ey practical effort. He did not believe we would eccom- plish our cork successfully by either without the other. When work bad to be done which involved a certain amount of sacrifice, either of leisure, of actual work or of means, we could not carry it out; • effectively without having somewhere about our nature' —though perhaps it was as well no often to express it —some real keen interest, some burning fire, some strong desire to get. rid of abuses which excited tier indignation and to march forward towards the accom- plishment of hopes which appealed to us and which presented to us a state of things, in our country and in the world, different from that which we saw around us. " There is nothing I dislike more" continued Sir Alfred "than what is called platform eloquence. f detest the thing, whether it is in politics or in the pulpit, or on occasions like this. We must have the burning fire and then never rest satisfied until we translate our feelings,into actual action on right lines." One of the most hopeful features of the present day was the growing realization of the demands made by common humanity—by one man upon his fellows, but Sir Alfred insisted that help should be given in such a way RS not to paralyse or demoralise the recipient. Ile condemned the" modern folly "of saying that people who would not work hard or deny themselves should e provided for by the State or municipality. It was the sheerest rubbish possible. Heaven helped those who helped themselves and, with safeguards against fraud and iolence, freedom was more likely to produce a healthy people, morally, intellectually and physically than grandmotherly State intervention. Even the strongest of us, however, could not always stand alone and that was where the Guild movement was of immense value.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter MiHedge gave a practical address and Mr. C. E. Parker warily enologised the work of Mr. Rowland, remarking that the Guild had come to stay.</p>
