      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>"MODERN FOLLY."</head>
        <p>"MODERN FOLLY."</p>
        <p>SIR:ALFRED HOPKINSoN AND GRAND- - MOTHERLY LEGISLATION.</p>
        <p>GUILD OF HELP COMMENDED.</p>
        <p>"The claims of humanity—of man upon man—are, I believe, more widely recognized at the present time than at any other period of our history" declared Sir Alfred Hopkinson, Vice-Chancellor of Manchester University, in a striking speech at the annual meet- ing of the Warrington Citizens' Guild of Help, held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Wednes- day e‘rening. Sir Alfred spoke strongly aLout the ".modern folly" of saying that people who w,uld Jatot work hard or deny themselves should he proviled for by the State or municipality, and commended the system of the Guild because it worked on discriminat- ing lines, without demoralizing the people who availed themselves of its help. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) pre.iderl over the meeting and was supported on the platform by Sir Alfred Hopkinson, Mr W Milledge (pioneer of the Guild of Help movement), Mr C E Parker, Mr and Mrs F A Frost, Rev W Bracecamp, Mr J Moore Murray, and Mr Wilfred Rowland (secretary of the Guild). Mr Harold Smith. M.P., wrote offering his congratu- lations to the committee end members of the Guild on their efforts to help those who bad fallen by the way- side. The annual rt port spoke eloquently of the practical manner in which they worked. They helped to brighten homes without leaving them the stigma of pauperism. Apologies for noL-attendance were received from Mr. SV Peter Rylands, Mr Vi N Hutchings, the Revs the Hon C Cross, G Va'e Owen, A M Mitchell, Dr Mellor, and 'l' T dames, Captain Crostield, Mr J J Cros field and Mr J J Whitley. THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The annual report recorded a year of faithful service on the part of helpers. Organized in seven district committees, these volunteers held fortnightly meetings to think out the problems of their less fortunate fellow townsmen. Literally, hundreds of miles were willingly tramped by the helpers in the visitation of the poor, and these friendly visits involve a large expenditure of patience, sympathy, and tact. The following summary shows how cases arising in 1912 were dealt with :—Helped in material ways with work, food, clothes, medical comforts, convalescent treat- ment, &amp;0.,05 ; helped by advice and friendly visitation, 53 ; referred to local authorities or other societies, 36 ; no help needed, 20 ; false addresses or gone away, 42; help ret used to unsatisfactory cases, 34 ; not yet classifiable, 3 ; recorded cases during strike period, 121; total, 404. In addition to the above, many cases were continued from 1911, and others were not, recorded during the strike. Those assisted at the time of the strike benefitted mainly from the Mayor's fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>TYPICAL CASES</head>
        <p>TYPICAL CASES</p>
        <p>helped by the Guild included the following: Child Labour.—A family where the father was per- manently invalided, was anxious to supplement its income by sending out one of the boys to work, before his normal schooling was completed. To prevent a bright lad losing some of his education, a District Committee agreed to pay a small weekly sum in lieu of wages for six months. Money Lending.—Another family, despite a regular income, never seemed able to make both ends meet. The wife was entangled with an uniegistered money- lender, who was securing exhorbitant interest. The police were informed and, after making their own inquiries, prosecuted the offender and secured a con- viction. The borrower made a fresh start in another district under happier conditions. White Slavery.—A domestic servant in a distant town was warned to break off correspondence with a man whom she had never seen, but who was asking her to marry him secretly. Here, also, the police were notified, but the man had already decamped, as be had swindled a local tradesman, and his fraud was certain to be discovered. He had corresponded with other young women, and there were reasons for believing that he was engaged in the White Slave Traffic. Social Parasites.—A widow with two unmarried sons to support her and two younger children kept by the Guardians at the expense of the ratepayers is an inveterate and successful beggar. So also is a lad, often notified to the Guild, who limps round with boot laces. He might to-day be a skilled workman if he bad not run away from the Institution where he was being trained. The general public, by their thoughtless gifts, enable such people to live without working. Money so expended is not merely wasted, but is steadily sapp- ing character. The Coal Strike.—The outstanding incident of the year was the coal strike. As the Guild was represented on the Mayor's Committee, and a report was issued at the time, little need be said here. After the distribu- tion during the rush of the first week-end, all applicants who could not get a recommend from some employer, were referred to the Guild of Help. Our accumulated records then proved to be of the utmost value, and the Mayor's Fund was saved from a large amount of imposi- tion. A willing hand of voluntary workers. came daily to the office so that they might be directeJ to the homes of fresh applicants. Co-operation in Cases of Tuberculosis.—Thanks are once more aactorded to the many public otiicialr, whose courtesy makes it easier to do philanthropic woik on sound lines. The Guild has been in contact with many poor plithisical patients. The following needs have been met :—(a) Warm clothing for those going to Sankey Sanatorium. (h) Milk and other nourishment at home. (c) Suitable employment for path uts partially or wholly cured. In November the Guild called a conference on sanatorium benefit, and invitations were sent to members of the Health Committee, Insurance Committee and Board of Guardians. Mrs Haslam, of Bolton, gave an address and a resolution was passed affirming the need for a Phtaisical Care Committee. It is hoped th,t such a committee may shortly be established and that the public will give it generous support. Reading Circle and Library.—During the autumn a reading circle was formed in affiliation with the National Home Reading Union. The object was to secure systematic reading on the administration of charity. Twenty-five persons joined and useful dis- cussion meetings have been held monthly. A small library has now been started at the OfrlC and presents of up-to-date books on social problems will be gladly received for the use of members of the Guild. With its combination of thought and service (the report con- cluded).the Guild is proving to be a veritable school of citizenship. We can confidently look to the future, if each one patiently works for the welfare of the town. _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>The Mothers and Babies' Welcome has had a success- ful year, and there has been an average attendance of from 20 to 30 mothers. The Welcome is open every Wednesday afternoon from 2.30 to 4.30,at the Academy- street, Adult Schoolroom. Mothers bring their babies to be weighed, and there is a short address on the care and management of babies and of the home given by one of the Lady Inspectors from the Health Depart- ment. THE MAYOR'S COMMENDATION.</p>
        <p>The MAYort warmly commended the Guild as a very useful aid to the health authorities. There was a saying: "It is much better to have bad laws well administered than good laws badly administered." He</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>GUARDIANS' ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The triennial election of members of the Warrington Board of Guardians wit! take place on Monday, April 7th. Nomination papers have tei be delivered not later than noon on Thursday, March 13th, at the office of the Clerk to the Board (Mr A Bottomley). It is too early as yet to give any idea of possible contests, the work of selecting candidates being only in the preliminary stage. We hear that, owing to the state of his health, Alderman James Evans will probably withdraw from the representation of Whitecrosa Ward, and Mr Thomas Dale is suggested as a candidate. The follosking is t. list of the retiring Board :</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Latchford Ward Miss Constance Broadbent Town Hall Ward Mr A J Jolley Whitecross Ward Mr James Evans Bewsey Ward Mrs Simpson Orford Ward Mr J A Hushworth St. John's Ward Mr W C Brooks Fairfield Ward Mr R Lee Howley Ward Mr G Allen St. Austin's (No. 1) Ward Mr W Gill St. Austin's (No. 2) Ward Mr J A Hughes</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK. West Ward ‘lr \V Flanagan Central Ward Mr .1 Billing° East Ward Mr J Cook</p>
        <p>NEWTON. Town Hall Ward Rev T R Matthews Crow-lane Ward Mrs Collingwood Newton Ward Mr T Ritchie Viaduct Ward Mr NI Seton ‘Vargrave Ward Mr E T. Bent (chairman of the Board) RURAL DISTRICT COUNCILLORS.</p>
        <p>- - The following are the [tor.' District. Councillors, who are also members of the Board : Burtonwood Rey A M Mitchell Juerdley Mr John Backhonse Houghton, Middleton and Arbury Mr J W Knowles Penketh Dr Murray Poulton-with-Fearnhead Mr G Mather Riston-with-Glazebrook Mr A Brooks Great Sankey Mr W Furness Soutlaworth-with-Croft Mr J Ball Winwich-with-Hulme Mr P Longton Woolston-a ith-Martins- croft Mr W Houghton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Royal Theatre of Varieties.</head>
        <p>Royal Theatre of Varieties.</p>
        <p>Mr Manville Morton entered upon the management of the above place of entertainment on Monday and is rapidly making himself popular with the artistes and the numerous patrons. His one aim is to satisfy the public and place the Theatre Royal in the front rank of provincial stock drama theatre. The "Royal Stock Co. have presented ths powerful drama "Man's Enemy" in a highly successful manner during the week. The drama of human pathos "Life's Darkeat Hour" will be staged next week. It is a stirring production from the pen of the new manager who has also made a name for himself as an author and playwright, as well as theatre manager. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>LATEST ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Arrangements for the visit of their Majesties the' King and Queen to Warring- ton on Monday, July 7th, are already being rapidly pushed forward. At the request of Lord Derby, the Mayor has convened a meeting of representatives of the -various authorities interested in the visit to Warrington and neighbourhood, for the purpose of conferring with his lordship as to thf.-, details of the programme. The confer- ence will be held at the Town Hall on Wednesday next.</p>
        <p>We are informed that their Majesties will traverse a short route in the town,which has not yet been definitely fixed but which will probably he as follows :—From Bank'Quay Station, along Wilson Patten-street, past Arpley Cannons to Warrington Bridge, thence up Bridge-street and along Sankey- street to the Town Hall. Their Majesties will ride in an open carriage. After the civic reception at the Town Hall their Majesties will proceed to Messrs. Crosfield's works and, after inspecting them, will leave in a motor car for Widnes, en route for Knowsley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
