      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>1,179 Moore won by 136.</head>
        <p>1,179 Moore won by 136.</p>
        <p>Brandon, Manitoba, affords a typical instance of the methods that are adopted by enterprising growing towns of Western Canada in securing the establishment in their midst of industrial undertakings. Since the creation of a commercial burtau by the municipality two years ago, to make known its opportunities as an industrial centre, EOM 3G manufacturing industries, after investigation of the conditions, have definitely located and opened business in Brandon. They include factories turning out furniture, sashes, and doors and general wood work, windmill pumps and gasoline engines, flour mills, tannery, saddlery, and harness, ready-made clothing, bri3ks and tiles, saw mills, threshing and traction engines, mattresses, tents and canvas goods, a boot and shoe factory, besides a large number of warehouses of Eastern manufacturers of goods of every description which are required by a prosperous!agricnitural community. Brandon's position as a railway point undoubtedly offers special opportunities as a distributing centre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTIONS. REV. A. M. MITCHEIE A CANDIDATE.</head>
        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL ELECTIONS. REV. A. M. MITCHEIE A CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>The Winwick electoral division—which embraces Winwick, Padgato, Penketh, Sankey, Burtonwood and district—is to be favoured with the mild excitement of a contested election fir a representative on the Lanca- shire County Council. The present member (Mr Richard Forshaw, managing director of the Burton- wood Brewery L d) is being opposed by the Rev A M Mitchell, vicar of Burtnnwood, who, it will be remembered, defeated Mr Forshaw in the Rural District Council and Board of Guardians elections three . years ago. The polling will take place on Friday next, between the hours of S a m and 8 p m, at the following schools :—Church of England Schools, Padgate, Winwick, and Hollins Green ; Council Schools, Burtcnwood and Great Sankey. The result will be declared about 9 p in by Mr Arthur Bottomley, who is acting as returning officer. The Rev A M Mitchell, MA„ Vicar of Burton wood is proposed by Mr Richard Marsh, seconded by Mr : John Charles Catterall. and assented to by Messrs William James, Tht nuts Powell, Edward Pickersgill, Levi Sims, Joseph Kelshall, John Dowd, James Halliwell,Williarn Pennington and James Worthington Mr Richard Forshaw, Montrose, Latham-toad, I Southport, managing director of the Buttonwood Brewery Co., Ltd , is proposed by Mr William Houghton, seconded. by the Rev J R ;Tones (Vicar of Sankey) and assented to by Me-are William Furness, Nicholas. Massey, Mary Al.ll Fairclough, John Warburton, Charles Edward Kilshirw, Culshaw, Albert Kilt-haw:and Dr Thomas A Murray. The Rev A M Mitchell in his address (which appears in ouri advertising columns) states that as a n strong sympathy with all sorts and conditions of workers, as educationist, hygenist, humanitarian, and temperance reformer he hopes if retu era d, to render some real practical service to the division. The call of the hour, he contends, is not for legielation, . but for a fuller and more thorough administration of existing parliamentary powers, especially in the direction of humane education, health and good environment. In a characteristically emphatic sentence the rev gentleman declares : Our Councils. Committees and Boards have too long been dominated by dummies, and the self-interested -- they heed purging. THE REV. A. M. MITCHELL'S CANDIDATURE A meeting in support of the Rev A M Mitchell's candidature was held in the Labour club, Penlieth, on Thursday evening. The rev gentleman had a hearty reception nod was listened to with a good deal of interest. He emphasised the 'importance of the work of the various Councils—Parish Council, ttural District Council, and the County Council—remarking that there ' should be a 'corking agreemeet between th.se bodies. He also touched on several important questions. Educa- tion, he said, wanted.dealinewith in no narrow way hut on bios. I democratic principles. hen again there was the health quest', n which was the most vital matter for this country. He showed, that the County Council had extraordinary posers if they vronld use them to the benefit of the ra:ep,“ ers. There were also the roads, the asylum, police force, small holdings, housing problem—all .of which came within the cognisance of the County Council. In remarking on the work of the Board of Guardian?, he said he thought it only right to give more out-relief and assist the old people in keeping out of the House, as he felt there were many old people who would be far more happy and comfortable in their humble homes.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker opposed. A contest will toke plisca in Widnes South between Mr C E Parker,of l'enketh. Lodge, Penketh (president of the Warrington Liberal Aseociatton) and Mr James El Lee, carriage proprietn, of Widnes. It was originally expected that, by an arrangement between the Liberal and Conservative parties, Nlr Parker would he returned unwposed, but the compact has not met with general acceptance, hence Mr Lee has been brought forward. Is his election address, Mr. Parker points out that be has been engaged for thirty years in inenstry, and has a material and social interest in the borough. Although in national offairs a Liberal, it would be this desire. if elected, to judge every question on its merits and to repre9eot the interests of the whole constituency, With- , •14„,, out reference to party or creed. CHESHIRE ELECTIONS. Mr Roger C Parr and Mr R H Posnett have 'hilen returned unopposed as representatives of the Anplaton and Daresbury divisions respectively on the Cheshire County Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SILVER WEDDING CELEBRATION.</head>
        <p>SILVER WEDDING CELEBRATION.</p>
        <p>UNIQUE GATHERING AT FRIA.p:, OMEN.</p>
        <p>Councillor and Mrs Henshall'as vied- ding celebration was held in the Friar's Cifeen :Sunday School on Saturday evening last. The school, which had been tastefully decorated, presented' , •,veiy pleasing appearance. 1. here were abant 300 pa ph present. It was a unique gathering and will long be remembered in the history of the church at Friar's Green. Mr Gaudy of Enfield Halt, Fearnhead, pre- sided and among those present were: Couticillor and Mrs Henshall (the host and hostess). Mrs and Miss Gandy, Aldermen James Evans and ['homes Burton, Councillor and Mrs Jas Shaw, Mr and Ms Arihur Mountield, Mr and Mrs Leonard Bennett, Captain and Mrs Savage, Mr and Mrs Andrews, Mr and Mrs J H Holt (Lynam), Mr and Mrs WSrrange, Mr and Ms Win Mercer (Lymm), Mr Platt (High Legh), Mr and Mrs Gerrard (Leigh), Mr and Mrs Jos Hesford (Lowton), Mr and Mrs Walter Inca (Leigh), Mr and Mrs James Critchley (Fearnhead), Mr R B Woods (connexional evangelist), Mr and Mrs James Birch, Mr and Mrs La Hudson, Mr and Mrs R Nelson, Mr Critchley, (Grappenhall) and Mr and Mrs Edward Howard, (Grappenhall). The Grappenhall Independent Church was well represented, many of the members being present, and quite a large number of friends from all parts of the circuit. The celebrations took the form of a social evening and concert, and at the interval refreshments were provided. The CllitemAN (Mr Gandy) said it gave him the greate.t possible pleasure to preside over such an interesting gathering. He had experienced 30 years of the warmest friendship with both Mr and Mrs Henshall, and in congratulating them on the 25th anniversary of their wedding day, he would also con- gratulate them on the good work they had both done for the cause of Independent Methodism. Mr Gandy also paid a tribute to Mr and Mrs Henshall for the unselfish way in which they bad thought of the church at Grappenhall. He felt sure all would agree that such a service as that rendered by their host and hostess was deserving of their most cordial support. (Applause). Aldermen Evans and Councillor Shaw addressed the meeting and spoke in the highest terms of Mr and Mrs Henshall's good work not only for what was being done in Grappenhall, but also for the cause in general. As was p yinted out in our last issue, Councillor and Mrs Henshall had asked for no personal gifts, but instead asked their friends to assist them with gifts of money for the new church scheme at Grappenhall. A right hearty response was the iesult, for no less than £5l was given for that purpose. Both Councillor and Mrs Henshall thanked all for the generous way in which they had responded on behalf of the Grappenhall scheme. Councillor Henshall said that he had had no refusals; they had received the widow's mite, and lavish sums and to all they were more than grateful for the way they were helping him and his colleagues to erect a new church in Grappenhall. (Applause). Mr Critchley (treasurer of the building fund) on behalf of the Grappenhall friends received the gift and tendered to all his grateful thanks. The concert was a huge success and every item was received with rounds of applause. The following ladies and gentlemen took part: - Miss Eva Shaw and Miss Bac, n, Messrs T Goostrey, G II Gough, W Barton and Christopher Furness. The accompanists were Miss Shakeshaft and Mr J W Jowett. Votes of thanks were heartily carried in favour of the artistes and all who had helped to make the celebrations a success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>MILITARY CONCERT AT THE PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY CONCERT AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>The brass and string bands of the Ist Battalion "The King's" Liverpool Regiment will give a Military Concert in the Parr Hall this (Saturday) even ing, assisted by those popular local vocalists Miss Nellie Anderton (contralto) and Mr J Babbitt (tenor). The band which will arrive in Warrington from Aldershot to-day, consists of over eighty perfomers, and is generally considered to be one of the best bands in the army. The programme includes the grand selections "11 Trovatore " and " Carmen " and the fantpsia's " Students' songs " and " Lohengrin." Miss Anderton will sing "Softly awakes my heart " and "She wandered down the mountainside" and Mr Babbitt will give The Trumpeter" and' 1 end me your aid." Bandmaster A E Hallord will conduct. This is a concert that should not be missed, as opportunities of hearing such a magnificent performance are few arid far between, to all who live at such a great dis- tance from the large military stations of the South. The proceeds will be devoted to the funds of the Old Comrades' Association of the Regitnent. Tickets can be obtained from Mr It H Pearson, Bridge-street.</p>
      </div>
