      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>DASH FOR LIBERTY. PRISONER'S LEAP FROM MOVING TRAIN AT WINWICK.</head>
        <p>DASH FOR LIBERTY. PRISONER'S LEAP FROM MOVING TRAIN AT WINWICK.</p>
        <p>A prisoner who was being conveyed by train from Llndon to Ulverston made a daring attempt to escaper from custody on Wednesday by leaping from the train, near Winwick.</p>
        <p>The man, who was not handcuffed, had shown ncr disposition to resist en routo. The compartment con- tained other travellers up to Crewe, but there the constable and prisoner were left alone. Outside Winwick Junction, the prisoner went to the lavatory. On returning he suddenly sprang on his escort, knocking him over with a heavy blow on the neck. Before the astonished officer could regain his feet the man made a dash for the door of the compartment, opened it and leaped out on the line. The constable pulled the communication cord, and the train was pulled up at the next station. Meanwhile, the prisoner's daring leap from the train bad been witnessed by a woman who was in the vicinity of the Vulcan Foundry. Police-Constable Lambert, of the Earlestoxvn police, was on duty near the foundry, and after hearing the woman's statement hurried along the line. He found the man, whose name is given as Fred Brisbane, lying in a dazed condition on the railway embankment. He was suffer- ing from cuts about the head and bruises on the body and offered no resistance whatever._ _</p>
        <p>His injuries were attended to at Earlestovvn Police Station by Dr Valentine and next day he WWI removed to the Warrington Union Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>NERVE PARALYSIS. Loss of Flesh, Appetite aud Sleep Thought would became Insane. CURED AT 70.</head>
        <p>NERVE PARALYSIS. Loss of Flesh, Appetite aud Sleep Thought would became Insane. CURED AT 70.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Gerry, a retired steward and pensioner, of the Royal Navy, residing at 2, Lyons-street, Stoke, Devonport, writes :—" lam 70 years of age, and for months suffered from a bad nervous breakdown, my symptoms being extreme nervousness and depression, loss of appetite and sleep, and a fear I should became insane. The best medical treatment was of no avail, and I lost two stone in weight. Then I tried Dr. CaQsell's Tablets, and in a very few days I felt better, and now I am feeling as well as ever and gradually recovering my lost flesh. I attribute my cure without any doubt to Dr. Cassell's Tablets.' Dr. Cassell's Tablets will cure all forms of nerve, organic, and physical weakness in children or adults, Price 101 d., Is. 11d. and 2s. 9d. of all chemists, Send 2d. in stamps to Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd., 418, Chester rd., Manchester, for a free trial box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-01_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>NEARLY BLINDED WITH BOILING SOUP.</head>
        <p>NEARLY BLINDED WITH BOILING SOUP.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court, on Monday, Frank Marren, lodging-house keeper, of 24, Market-street,was tried on a charge of doing grievous bodily harm to Richard Molyneux, one of his lodgerc, on Saturday night, February 15th. Mr Garner defended. The Chief Constable said that Marren had been on remand for a week while Molyneux had been in hospital.</p>
        <p>Molgneux described how Marren threw the boiling contents of a pan into his face. His eyes were filled with soup, and he thanked God that his sight had beau saved.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable : You must thank the doctor a bit, and the police. Have you suffered much pain? Molyneux : Yes ; I have gone through my drills; I don't want any more. Peter Dowling, deputy at the lodging-house, said+ that Molyneux struck Marren some time before the pan was thrown. Marren bad objected to Molyneux cooking after hours. Mr E M Reid said that Molyneux was in a serious state, and for some time it appeared that his left eye would be destroyed. Marren was now charged with a common assault, and he said he was guilty of throwing the soup, but it waa• five minutes after Molyneux bad stabbed him. Mr Garner pointed out that Marren's left ear was severely cut, and the wound must have been done by a knife.</p>
        <p>He called defendant, who swore that Molyneus was the aggressor. Molynewx, however, denied that he used a knife. Prisoner's son swore that he saw Molyneus use the knife on his father. _</p>
        <p>The bench fined Marren £2 183 6d, including costs.</p>
