        <p>as " Michaela " and Mr. Tait-Knight as " Escamillo,' these principal characters in the opera being capably supported by various other members of the company, It was quite early noticeable that Mr. O'Mara was suffering from a cold. Nevertheless, as he warmed to his work, he sang and acted the part of the scorned and distraught lover with both appropriate eeergy and due restraint—a combination of qualities which it is by no means easy to maintain in correct balance. Of Miss Ainsley's " Carmen " it must be confessed that it was the most vivacious pot trayal of the part we remember to have wi'm.ssed, and if it did not alwaye realise one'e ideal of it., mote refined subtleties, there was about it a degree of self-abandonment in the singing as well as the acting, which carried convic- tion and rivetted interest in all Miss Ainsley did. The singing of Miss Wallace was particularly sweet and natural, and in the appeal Xlichaela makes to Don Jose in the third act, Miss Wallace evinced a great charm of manner. Mr Tait-Knight's" Escatnillo " was a fine performance, his brilliant and rousing singing of the Toreador's song and his brave demean, ur throughout this dashing part, winning for him high favour. On Wednesday evening the ever-green " Maritana " attracted the largest audience of the three night's per- formances, and, judged hy the warm welcome given to its string of melodious songs and interesting concerted pieces, these are and will evidently indefinitely remain, potent in their appeal to the sympathies of popular audiences. The principal characters were taken by Miss Kathleen Mc'Cully (M,iritana), Mr William Boland (Don Ca-ar), Mr Tait-Knight (Don, Jose) and Mr Williitn Heughan (The King). Verdi's " 11 Trovatore " was performed on Wednea- nesday afternoon before a small but very enthusiastic audiet ce. "One Life, One Love." For the latter part of the week Mr Charles Locke's company is appearing in the domestic drama " One Life, one Love" which is presented twice nightly. Miss Dora Pass takes the part of Elsie Dale in con- vipcing style, and is especially good in the cottage scene. Mr T Arthur Ellis, as Richard Medway, Mr Poison Turner as Hubsirt Manners and Mr Ernest Yandall as Victor Vaughan also play prominent parts in the exciting situations in which the play abounds. "The Pride of Byzantia." A NEW SUCCESSFUL COMEDY PRODUCTION r</p>
